Chapter: Parsmi--X86-64 Code Generator

Section: Heap Bump

 The guard is 31 bytes and needs no scratch register, which is the constraint
 that decides both encodings -- see `cmp-r-abs` and `cmp-abs-imm8`. It runs at
 59 allocation sites across 8 emitter chapters, where every register is already
 live.

 A CEILING OF ZERO MEANS UNARMED, AND THE FIRST TEST IS WHAT MAKES THE GUARD
 SAFE OUTSIDE THE COMPILER. Without this test the second compare reads a
 zero-initialised cell as a ceiling, every frontier exceeds it, and the trap
 fires on the FIRST heap allocation of every emitted program: measured
 2026-08-27, the sign tool and a five-line probe both died `!EXC=06` at
 `R10=0x600008` while the guarded compiler itself ran clean, because the
 compiler is the one program that arms the cell.

 A GREEN GATE IS NOT EVIDENCE THIS GUARD WORKS, AND NEITHER IS THE `-Decks`
 KNOB. The per-site `deck-short-of` checks raise CDX9002 before the deck
 cursor ever reaches this ceiling, so every starvation route that exists
 reports the OLDER guard's refusal and this one stays silent: measured
 2026-08-28, a sweep at 50/60/70/80/90 was byte-for-byte identical between a
 guarded and an unguarded kernel, and `deck-floor-test.ps1` passes all four
 arms against the guarded kernel while testing that same per-site path. A new
 guard sitting behind an older one inherits its green rather than earning it.
 The only instrument that separates this working from dead code is a SABOTAGE
 of the arming value, one edit and one build: `build` writing `p + 4096`
 instead of `p + size` dies `!EXC=06` with no output where the good kernel
 compiles the same chapter at exit 0.

 THE CELL IS ARMED FOR THE LENGTH OF A DECK EXTENT AND ZERO OUTSIDE IT.
 `__deck-enter` copies the reservation top into it on the counter's 0 to 1
 crossing and `__deck-exit` writes zero back on the crossing to 0
 (`emit-deck-enter-builtin` / `emit-deck-exit-builtin`, `X86_64Builtins.codex`);
 `build` writes only the reservation top, in its own cell. That is what makes
 R10 here a statement about the DECK CURSOR: inside an extent `__deck-enter`
 has loaded R10 from the deck-pos cell and every allocation walks it up the
 deck, while outside one R10 is the bivy frontier, which sits above the
 reservation by construction and would trip a ceiling that stayed armed. The
 argument is written once, where the top is written, in
 `Core/PhaseAllocator.codex`.

 The two rel32 displacements are the lengths of what each branch skips: 16 is
 the armed compare, its branch and the UD2, and 2 is the UD2 alone.

  deck-guard-code : Integer -> List Integer
  deck-guard-code (base) =
   cmp-abs-imm8 (base + deck-ceiling-addr) 0
     & jcc cc-e 16
     & cmp-r-abs reg-r10 (base + deck-ceiling-addr)
     & jcc cc-be 2
     & [15, 11]

  heap-bump-imm : CodegenState, Integer -> CodegenState
  heap-bump-imm (st) (n) =
   if deck-guard-enabled == 0 then st-append-code st (add-ri reg-r10 n)
   else st-append-code st (add-ri reg-r10 n & deck-guard-code (st-base st))

  heap-bump-reg : CodegenState, Integer -> CodegenState
  heap-bump-reg (st) (r) =
   if deck-guard-enabled == 0 then st-append-code st (add-rr reg-r10 r)
   else st-append-code st (add-rr reg-r10 r & deck-guard-code (st-base st))

