 Staged print sink. __serial_put takes the byte in AL and clobbers only
 RDX and flags, the same surface as the wait-and-out triple it replaces.
 When the boot probe found the codex-vm blit (flag cell 36176) the byte
 is staged at the cursor cell 36192 instead of leaving through the UART
 - no VM exit. Anywhere else the legacy LSR-poll-and-OUT runs. The
 __print_begin and __print_flush brackets anchor the staging region at
 the heap top (R10 scratch; nothing allocates during a print) and blit
 the whole print in one doorbell. Single-writer discipline: the BSP
 owns the cells; concurrent AP prints were already garbled per-byte and
 remain unsupported.

 The hosted print sink writes each byte through write(2) as it arrives rather
 than staging a whole line the way the bare-metal blit path does. One syscall
 per byte is the slow choice and it is deliberate for now: staging would make
 correctness depend on every caller bracketing its print with __print_begin and
 __print_flush, and the raw and itoa paths do not. An unbracketed stage writes
 through a base cell nobody set, which is a wild store rather than a wrong
 character. The brackets stay as no-ops so the shared print path above the sink
 is unchanged.

 SYSCALL clobbers RAX, RCX and R11 by architecture. __serial_put is documented
 to clobber only RDX and flags, so all three are saved here and the byte in AL
 is saved with them.

  emit-hosted-serial-put-helper : CodegenState -> CodegenState
  emit-hosted-serial-put-helper (st) =
   let st0 = record-func-offset st "__serial_put"
   in let st1 = st-append-code st0 (push-r reg-rcx & push-r reg-r11 & push-r reg-rdi & push-r reg-rsi & push-r reg-rdx & push-r reg-rax)
   in let st2 = st-append-code st1 (li reg-rdx hosted-print-base-addr)
   in let st3 = st-append-code st2 (mov-store-byte reg-rdx reg-rax 0)
   in let st4 = st-append-code st3 (mov-rr reg-rsi reg-rdx)
   in let st5 = st-append-code st4 (li reg-rax linux-sys-write)
   in let st6 = st-append-code st5 (li reg-rdi linux-stdout-fd)
   in let st7 = st-append-code st6 (li reg-rdx 1)
   in let st8 = st-append-code st7 x86-syscall
   in let st9 = st-append-code st8 (pop-r reg-rax & pop-r reg-rdx & pop-r reg-rsi & pop-r reg-rdi & pop-r reg-r11 & pop-r reg-rcx)
   in st-append-code st9 x86-ret

 The Windows sink. Same shape as the Linux one -- one byte, written where it
 arrives -- but through kernel32 rather than a syscall, so it obeys the Windows
 x64 calling convention: arguments in RCX, RDX, R8, R9, a fifth on the stack,
 and 32 bytes of shadow space the callee owns. RSP must be 16-byte aligned at
 the CALL. It is by construction here: the call into this helper leaves RSP at
 8 mod 16, PUSH RBP makes it 0, six more pushes are 48 bytes, and 0x40 more
 keeps it there, so no runtime AND of RSP is needed.

 GetStdHandle is called once and its answer cached, because a console handle
 does not change and asking per byte would double the syscall count.

  emit-hosted-win-serial-put-helper : CodegenState -> CodegenState
  emit-hosted-win-serial-put-helper (st) =
   let st0 = record-func-offset st "__serial_put"
   in let st1 = st-append-code st0 (li reg-rdx hosted-win-print-base-addr)
   in let st2 = st-append-code st1 (mov-store-byte reg-rdx reg-rax 0)
   in let st3 = st-append-code st2 (push-r reg-rbp & mov-rr reg-rbp reg-rsp)   in let st4 = st-append-code st3 (push-r reg-rax & push-r reg-rcx & push-r reg-r8 & push-r reg-r9 & push-r reg-r10 & push-r reg-r11)
   in let st5 = st-append-code st4 (emit-sub-rsp-imm32 64)
   in let st6 = st-append-code st5 (li reg-r10 hosted-win-handle-addr)
   in let st7 = st-append-code st6 (mov-load reg-rax reg-r10 0)
   in let st8 = st-append-code st7 (test-rr reg-rax reg-rax)
   in let have-pos = st8.code-len
   in let st9 = st-append-code st8 (jcc cc-ne 0)
   in let st10 = st-append-code st9 (li reg-rcx (0 - 11))
   in let st11 = st-append-code st10 (call-abs-ind hosted-win-iat-getstdhandle)
   in let st12 = st-append-code st11 (li reg-r10 hosted-win-handle-addr)
   in let st13 = st-append-code st12 (mov-store reg-r10 reg-rax 0)
   in let st14 = patch-jcc-at st13 have-pos (st13.code-len)
   in let st15 = st-append-code st14 (li reg-r10 hosted-win-handle-addr)
   in let st16 = st-append-code st15 (mov-load reg-rcx reg-r10 0)
   in let st17 = st-append-code st16 (li reg-rdx hosted-win-print-base-addr)
   in let st18 = st-append-code st17 (li reg-r8 1)
   in let st19 = st-append-code st18 (li reg-r9 hosted-win-written-addr)
   in let st20 = st-append-code st19 (li reg-r10 0)
   in let st21 = st-append-code st20 (mov-store reg-rsp reg-r10 32)
   in let st22 = st-append-code st21 (call-abs-ind hosted-win-iat-writefile)
   in let st23 = st-append-code st22 (add-ri reg-rsp 64)
   in let st24 = st-append-code st23 (pop-r reg-r11 & pop-r reg-r10 & pop-r reg-r9 & pop-r reg-r8 & pop-r reg-rcx & pop-r reg-rax)
   in let st25 = st-append-code st24 (pop-r reg-rbp)
   in st-append-code st25 x86-ret
 ONE BYTE OF STDIN, hosted. Both arms answer the byte in RAX and 0 at end of
 input, which is the sentinel every reader above already stops on, so the
 conversion and termination logic is shared rather than written twice.

 The Windows arm caches the handle exactly as the write side does, and zeroes
 the count cell before the call: ReadFile leaves it untouched on failure, and a
 stale count from the previous byte would read as a successful byte that was
 never delivered. RCX and R11 are pushed for the Linux arm because SYSCALL
 clobbers them by architecture, and R10 for the Windows arm because it carries
 the heap frontier and is volatile under the Windows ABI.

