 WHAT THE TARGET IS, ANSWERED TO SOURCE: 0 bare metal, 1 hosted Linux, 2
 hosted Windows, the same three values `st.hosted-target` carries. Two cells
 the compiler reads are FIXED PHYSICAL addresses that only a bare-metal payload
 can reach, and until this existed there was no way for the source holding
 those reads to know not to take them. `bp-cell` (126976) is the UEFI stub's
 framebuffer handoff block, and `guard-page-base-cell` (36320) is the deck
 guard's, written by the page-table setup above that a hosted build never runs.
 No hosted target maps either: Windows refuses a fixed low VirtualAlloc from a
 freshly loaded image, which is why `hosted-win-cell-base` sits at 0x50000000,
 and Linux starts its band at 131072 with `bp-cell` on the page below.

 Measured 2026-08-30, and this is why it is worth a builtin: `peek-qword
 126976 0` in an eight-line hosted-windows program prints its first line and
 dies at 0xC0000005. The compiler's own `opening` reaches `bp-present` on its
 first rung, so every hosted build of the compiler faulted before running.

  emit-hosted-kind-helper : CodegenState -> CodegenState

  emit-hosted-kind-helper (st) =

   let fn-off = st.code-len

   in let n = list-push (st.fo-names) "hosted-kind"

   in let o = list-push (st.fo-offsets) fn-off

   in let st0 = __record-set (__record-set st "fo-names" n) "fo-offsets" o

   in let st1 = st-append-code st0 (li reg-rax (st.hosted-target))

   in st-append-code st1 x86-ret

