Chapter: Parsmi--Phase

Section: Constants
  phase-infrastructure : Integer = 0

  phase-lexer : Integer = 1

  phase-parser : Integer = 2

  phase-prose-validation : Integer = 3

  phase-name-resolver : Integer = 4

  phase-type-checker : Integer = 5

  phase-linearity : Integer = 6

  phase-proof-checker : Integer = 7

  phase-capability-checker : Integer = 8

  phase-codegen : Integer = 9

Section: Display
  phase-label : Integer -> Text
  phase-label (p) =
   if p == phase-infrastructure then "Infrastructure"
   else if p == phase-lexer then "Lexer"
   else if p == phase-parser then "Parser"
   else if p == phase-prose-validation then "ProseValidation"
   else if p == phase-name-resolver then "NameResolver"
   else if p == phase-type-checker then "TypeChecker"
   else if p == phase-linearity then "Linearity"
   else if p == phase-proof-checker then "ProofChecker"
   else if p == phase-capability-checker then "CapabilityChecker"
   else if p == phase-codegen then "CodeGen"
   else "Unknown"

Page 1


Chapter: Parsmi--Phase Allocator
  cites Codex chapter BootPaint (bp-rtc-seconds, bp-log-phase)

Section: Primitives

  pitch : Integer -> Integer
  pitch (size) =
   let p = __heap-save
   in let advanced = __heap-advance size
   in p

  strike : Integer -> Nothing
  strike (start) =
   __heap-restore start

 A deck reservation is ONE advance of the whole deck height, hundreds of
 megabytes, so it steps clean over the 2 MB guard page below the boot stack and
 the page never sees it. Measured 2026-08-04: the whole-compiler `-IrCce` run
 crossed the guard at 2974 MB and died at 3034 MB in a 75-byte `__str_concat`
 with the guard page present and silent. The page catches many small bumps; it
 structurally cannot catch one big one.

 So the big ones are checked here instead, which is affordable because this is
 the chokepoint the 58 inline bump sites do not have: one compare per deck,
 thirteen per compile, against one per allocation.

  guard-page-base-cell : Integer = 36320

  guard-page-plausible-floor : Integer = 1073741824

  deck-reservation-top-cell : Integer = 36344

  build : Integer -> Integer
  build (size) =
   let p = __heap-save
   in let deck-init = __deck-set p
   in let guarded = deck-reservation-guard p size
   in let top = if deck-guard-enabled == 0 | hosted-kind /= 0 then 0 else poke-qword-cell deck-reservation-top-cell (p + size)
   in let advanced = __heap-advance size
   in p

 The reservation's top, written here because this is where it is known, and
 NOT the value the per-allocation guard compares against. Those were the same
 cell until 2026-08-28 and that is the whole of what the re-point changes.

 R10 IS THE DECK CURSOR, BUT ONLY INSIDE AN EXTENT. `__deck-enter` copies the
 deck-pos cell into R10 and `__deck-exit` writes it back, on the zero crossings
 of the nesting counter (`emit-deck-enter-builtin` / `emit-deck-exit-builtin`,
 `Emit/X86_64Builtins.codex`), so within an extent every allocation moves R10
 down the deck and R10 against this top is exactly the question the row asks.
 Outside an extent R10 is the bivy frontier, which lives ABOVE the reservation
 and is SUPPOSED to: `build` advances the heap over the whole deck height, so
 the frontier parks on this top the instant the reservation is made and every
 ordinary allocation afterwards is legitimately past it.

 Arming the ceiling here is therefore the defect measured 2026-08-27: the guard
 fired on the first ordinary allocation after every reservation and the guarded
 compiler could not compile itself (`!EXC=06` at R10=0x14ADAECC, 324.9 MB). The
 ceiling is armed at `__deck-enter` and disarmed at `__deck-exit` instead, which
 is what makes the comparison a statement about the deck cursor rather than
 about the bivy, and it costs the 59 allocation sites nothing: they keep the
 same two compares, and zero still means unarmed.

 Byte-wise because poke-byte is the only poke primitive; build runs thirteen
 times per compile, so eight stores apiece is not a cost worth a new builtin.

  poke-qword-cell : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poke-qword-cell (addr) (v) =
   let b0 = poke-byte addr 0 (int-mod v 256)
   in let b1 = poke-byte addr 1 (int-mod (v / 256) 256)
   in let b2 = poke-byte addr 2 (int-mod (v / 65536) 256)
   in let b3 = poke-byte addr 3 (int-mod (v / 16777216) 256)
   in let b4 = poke-byte addr 4 (int-mod (v / 4294967296) 256)
   in let b5 = poke-byte addr 5 (int-mod (v / 1099511627776) 256)
   in let b6 = poke-byte addr 6 (int-mod (v / 281474976710656) 256)
   in let b7 = poke-byte addr 7 (int-mod (v / 72057594037927936) 256)
   in v

 The cell is written by the page-table setup in X86_64Boot, which a hosted
 build never runs, and it is a fixed physical address a hosted process does not
 map. So on a hosted target the read faults instead of answering, and there is
 nothing for the guard to read even if it did.

  deck-reservation-guard : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  deck-reservation-guard (p) (size) =
   if hosted-kind /= 0 then 0
   else let guard-base = peek-qword guard-page-base-cell 0
   in if guard-base >= guard-page-plausible-floor & p + size >= guard-base
      then poke-byte guard-base 0 0
      else 0

  seal : Integer -> Nothing
  seal (start) =
   __heap-advance 0

  deck-record : a -> a
  deck-record (x) =
   x

Section: Mountain Base

  init-phase-allocator : Integer
  init-phase-allocator =
   let base = __heap-save
   in let deck-init = __deck-set base
   in base

Section: Phase Measurement

  PhaseStart = record {
   bivy-origin : Integer,
   deck-origin : Integer,
   started : Integer
  }

  PhaseMetrics = record {
   label : Text,
   bivy-origin : Integer,
   bivy-hwm : Integer,
   deck-origin : Integer,
   deck-end : Integer
  }

  phase-start : PhaseStart
  phase-start =
   let bivy = pitch 0
   in PhaseStart { bivy-origin = bivy, deck-origin = __deck-pos, started = bp-rtc-seconds 0 }

 The log row is banked as the phase closes: label, wall seconds (RTC,
 armed-guarded, zero everywhere but a flight), and megabytes of bivy growth.
 bp-log-phase also advances the painted phase strip, which is what keeps a
 long silent phase legible on a board whose only channel is the glass.

  phase-took : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  phase-took (started) (now) =
   if now >= started then now - started else now - started + 86400

  phase-measure : PhaseStart, Text -> PhaseMetrics
  phase-measure (ps) (label) =
   let hwm = __heap-save
   in let deck-now = __deck-pos
   in let lg = bp-log-phase label (phase-took (ps.started) (bp-rtc-seconds 0)) ((hwm - (ps.bivy-origin)) / 1048576)
   in deck-record (PhaseMetrics {
    label = label,
    bivy-origin = ps.bivy-origin,
    bivy-hwm = hwm,
    deck-origin = ps.deck-origin,
    deck-end = deck-now
   })

  deck-short-of : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  deck-short-of (ceiling) (band) =
   if ceiling > 0 & __deck-pos + band >= ceiling then True else False

 __deck-pos reads the deck-pos cell. deck-record is compiled by
 emit-deck-record-wrapper into __deck-enter, the body, __deck-exit:
 enter copies the cell into R10 and exit writes R10 back, and the
 write-back happens only when the nesting counter returns to zero. So
 inside a phase-wide deck-record extent the cell is FROZEN at the
 deck's base for the whole phase, and every allocation in that extent
 moves R10 instead. A loop that sits inside such an extent and asks
 deck-short-of is asking whether base + band >= base + height -- a
 question about the FLOOR's width that never mentions the usage, true
 only when the floor is narrower than the band.

 Measured 2026-07-21: an in-loop probe through rewrite-ir-defs, over
 the 2.81 MB selfhost, printed __deck-pos as 0x168961a2 on every one
 of its iterations while the phase wrote 19.1 MB. Starving that floor
 to 16 MB did not raise CDX9002; it crashed in __text_compare on a
 garbage pointer, which is the failure the guard exists to prevent,
 with the guard present and compiled in.

  deck-bound-short-of : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  deck-bound-short-of (ceiling) (band) =
   if ceiling > 0 & __heap-save + band >= ceiling then True else False

  phase-compact : Nothing = __heap-restore (__deck-pos)

  phase-compact-poison : Integer -> Nothing
  phase-compact-poison (byte) =
   let old-top = __heap-save
   in let new-top = __deck-pos
   in let restored = __heap-restore new-top
   in if old-top > new-top then __memset new-top byte (old-top - new-top)
      else restored

Page 1


Chapter: Parsmi--TextFormat

Section: Zero-padded integers
  pad-zeros-loop : Integer, Text -> Text
  pad-zeros-loop (remaining) (s) =
   if remaining <= 0 then s
   else pad-zeros-loop (remaining - 1) ("0" & s)

  integer-to-text-padded : Integer, Integer -> Text
  integer-to-text-padded (n) (width) =
   let s = integer-to-text n
   in let len = text-length s
   in if len >= width then s
      else pad-zeros-loop (width - len) s

