Section: Compile Flags

  CompileFlags = record {
   prose : Boolean,
   escape-check : Boolean,
   poison-compact : Boolean,
   deck-scale : Integer,
   phase-trace : Boolean,
   wire-trace : Boolean,
   cost-report : Boolean,
   no-lift : Boolean,
   pipeline : List Text
  }

  compile-flags-default : CompileFlags
  compile-flags-default = CompileFlags { prose = False, escape-check = False, poison-compact = False, deck-scale = 100, phase-trace = False, wire-trace = False, cost-report = False, no-lift = False, pipeline = default-ir-pipeline }

 A floor is a guard band plus a workspace, and only the workspace scales. The
 band is what the phase's write-path check reserves to stop short in, so a
 scaled floor that eats into it makes the guard fire on a deck holding almost
 nothing: measured 2026-08-03, a 1082-byte app refused at decks=11 with
 CDX9002 from DESUGAR, whose scaled floor was 7.92 MB against an 8 MB band,
 while the phase's real usage was 88 KB. That put a hard bottom on the knob at
 about 12 per cent for reasons unrelated to how much room anything needed.

 At pct 100 the branch evaluates the previous expression exactly, truncation
 included, so every reservation is unchanged at the shipping setting. The
 emitted binary does change, because this function gained a parameter; what
 is preserved is what it computes. Verified rather than argued: the same
 source measured under the seed and under the new compiler produces
 byte-identical deck origins across all seven decks.

  deck-min-workspace : Integer = 2097152

  scaled-floor : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  scaled-floor (pct) (floor) (band) =
   if pct == 100 then floor / 100 * pct
   else let ws = (floor - band) / 100 * pct
    in band + (if ws < deck-min-workspace then deck-min-workspace else ws)

  deck-scale-of : Text -> Integer
  deck-scale-of (mode) =
   let v = mode-flag-value mode "decks=" 0
   in if v < 1 then 0 else v

 deck-scale-anchor is the compiler's own concatenated unit, measured 2026-08-04
 (`build/concat-codex-self.ps1 -CodexDir codex/compiler`). This said it is the
 length that maps to a scale of 100. It is not: deck-scale-margin doubles the
 estimate, so a unit of exactly this length derives 200 and is clamped, and the
 length that actually maps to 100 is HALF the anchor, 1,496,788. Everything
 above that gets the same reservation, so the derivation stops tracking length
 at half the anchor rather than at the anchor.

 Staleness is not safe in both directions either, though this said it was. The
 argument covered only the compiler growing. An anchor LARGER than the true
 unit divides every estimate down and hands the linear band less room, and that
 is the direction that happened: measured 2026-08-09 the unit is 2,746,259
 bytes, 247,317 below the anchor. It costs nothing today because the linear
 band holds 15 of 2814 measurable entry points and the other 2799 are at the
 floor or the clamp, neither of which the anchor touches.

 deck-scale-margin is headroom over the linear estimate, and 2 is measured
 rather than chosen for roundness. Across 29 units above 150 KB the binding
 phase is DESUGAR and the ratio of the linear estimate to the scale actually
 required ran from 1.24 to 1.71, so the bare estimate clears the requirement by
 as little as a quarter. Doubling it puts the tightest observed case at 2.5x.
 The reason margin is needed at all is that phase usage is NOT reliably linear
 in unit length below that size: CHECK usage per unit byte was measured between
 21x and 167x and is not monotonic, so a bare linear estimate is a line through
 points that do not lie on one.

 deck-scale-min is what 99 per cent of the corpus actually receives: measured
 2026-08-09 over every quire, 2798 of 2814 measurable entry points derive the
 floor, 15 derive from the linear term and 1 derives the clamp. So the floor is
 not a fallback, it IS the derivation, and the linear term between them governs
 fifteen units.

 It is set from density rather than from length, because density is where a
 length-derived scale fails. It was set from the two worst-density units known
 in 2026-08-04, and THOSE ARE NO LONGER THE BINDING CASE. Bisected 2026-08-09
 against seed A66E54F57CBAEBFD, the 46 units of the Shell quire (`codex/build`,
 70 to 102 KB each) need 34 to 43, the worst being `vmconfigScript`, which
 refuses at 42 and compiles at 43. `codex/test/shell-build-keep` needs 30. The
 Shell quire is what the floor must now clear, and 43 against 64 is 1.49x.
 Outside those 46 and the compiler, nothing in the tree is closer than 2.13x.

 The floor was 32, and at 32 a short deck fails BOTH ways. Measured 2026-08-05
 against seed 52E0A3A00218E19F, on `codex/build`, every unit of which derives
 the floor exactly:

 - **Silently.** `checkappsScript.codex` compiles clean at 32 and its output
   carries the intermediate states of a string concatenation, each with its
   length byte. At 33 and above it is byte-identical to the same unit at 150.
   Deterministic across three compiles, with a byte-identical CDX. This is the
   defect that was bisected to a seed change and read as a Text or rope fault.
   The seed is not at fault; the knob is.
 - **Loudly.** `BuildScript.codex`, `vmconfigScript.codex` and
   `comparecodexsemanticScript.codex` all refuse at 32 with `CDX9002: Deck
   overflow in CHECK` and compile at 64.

 The claim that certified 32 was that a sweep of all 1674 entry points at this
 setting raises no CDX9002. `BuildScript.codex` raises exactly that at 32, so
 the sweep's corpus did not cover the units that fail -- the Shell quire is not
 in the seed's reach and nothing gated compiles it. **A sweep cannot certify a
 floor for a quire it does not enumerate, and CDX9002 cannot certify one at
 all**, because the silent arm above raises nothing. Certify a floor by OUTPUT
 EQUALITY over a corpus that names its scope.

 64 was double the value known to break when it was chosen. It is 1.49x it now,
 because the Shell quire needs 43 and needed 33 four days earlier: measured
 2026-08-09, `checkappsScript` refuses at 34 and compiles at 36 where
 2026-08-05 recorded it byte-identical at 33. The compiler is not what moved.
 The same unit's CHECK deck usage is byte-identical under seeds
 AEB5ED2B5043C7C1, 065D92E60292492D and A66E54F57CBAEBFD, so the requirement is
 growing with the units and the floor is a constant that does not follow them.
 `build/deck-headroom.ps1` is the runner that re-measures this.

 The ceiling on the cost is that `derive-deck-scale` clamps at 100 and 100 is
 what every unit received before this derivation existed, so 64 cannot reserve
 more than the arrangement it replaced.

 The clamp is a ceiling on the CURE as well as on the cost, and one unit is
 already past it. `codex/test/apps/foreword-all-compile` needs at least 131:
 measured 2026-08-09 at decks=200 its LOWER deck uses 437,990,744 bytes, and
 -Measure runs no IR pipeline so the shipping LOWER is larger again. It
 compiles only because a `.flags` sidecar overrides the derivation with 200.
 At the 100 it would derive, the CDX path refuses with CDX9002 naming LOWER,
 which is the right answer, and the -Measure path faults in `bag-add` instead
 of raising, which is C2's open item reproduced on a real unit.

 **The mechanism this paragraph called unnamed is CHECK-KEEP, and `mc-sat` WAS
 it.** `mcopy-sat` read `let z = __record-set mc "mc-sat" 1 in x` and `MemoCtx`
 is not `mutable`, so the set built a record that was bound to `z` and
 discarded; the caller's `mc.mc-sat` was 0 on every path ever taken. When the
 keep frontier passes `mc-ceiling` the copy hands back the ORIGINAL pointer,
 which is scratch the caller reclaims a moment later, and the flag that was
 supposed to turn that into CDX9002 could not be set. A dangling `CodexType`
 then reaches `lookup-expr-type`, an append over it classifies as neither text
 nor list, and the program is miscompiled rather than refused. The flag is
 deleted and the caller now compares the keep deck's measured end against
 `mc-ceiling`, which is sound because the keep frontier is monotonic across
 these copies.

  deck-scale-anchor : Integer = 2993576
  deck-scale-margin : Integer = 2
  deck-scale-min : Integer = 64

  derive-deck-scale : Integer -> Integer
  derive-deck-scale (unit-len) =
   let est = unit-len * 100 * deck-scale-margin / deck-scale-anchor
   in if est < deck-scale-min then deck-scale-min
      else if est > 100 then 100
      else est

  effective-deck-scale : Text, Integer -> Integer
  effective-deck-scale (mode) (unit-len) =
   let explicit = deck-scale-of mode
   in if explicit > 0 then explicit else derive-deck-scale unit-len

  pipeline-of : Text -> List Text
  pipeline-of (mode) =
   let v = mode-flag-text mode "passes="
   in if v == "" then default-ir-pipeline
      else if v == "none" then []
      else if v == "text-plug" then text-plug-ir-pipeline
      else if text-starts-with v "+" then default-ir-pipeline & parse-pipeline-names (substring v 1 (text-length v - 1))
      else parse-pipeline-names v

  poison-lex : Integer = 161
  poison-parse : Integer = 162
  poison-desugar : Integer = 163
  poison-scope : Integer = 164
  poison-check : Integer = 165
  poison-lower : Integer = 166
  poison-resolve : Integer = 167
  poison-lift : Integer = 168
  poison-frontend : Integer = 169

  poison-phase-label : Integer -> Text
  poison-phase-label (byte) =
   if byte == 161 then "LEX"
   else if byte == 162 then "PARSE"
   else if byte == 163 then "DESUGAR"
   else if byte == 164 then "SCOPE"
   else if byte == 165 then "CHECK"
   else if byte == 166 then "LOWER"
   else if byte == 167 then "RESOLVE"
   else if byte == 168 then "LIFT"
   else if byte == 169 then "FRONTEND"
   else ""

  compact-phase : CompileFlags, Integer -> Nothing
  compact-phase (flags) (byte) =
   let marked = phase-trace-mark (flags.phase-trace) byte
   in if flags.poison-compact then phase-compact-poison byte
      else phase-compact

Section: Compile Target

  CompileTarget =
   | CtCodexText
   | CtX86_64Bare
   | CtCdx

Section: Frontend

  CompileFrontend = record {
   type-defs : List ATypeDef,
   text-meta : IRTextMeta,
   ir : IRChapter,
   cdx-ir : IRChapter,
   sorted-type-defs : List TypeBinding,
   renames : List RenameEntry,
   bag : DiagnosticBag,
   heap-marks : List Integer,
   phase-metrics : List PhaseMetrics
  }

  LexResult = record {
   tokens : List Token,
   bag : DiagnosticBag,
   heap-marks : List Integer,
   phase-metrics : List PhaseMetrics
  }

  ParseResult = record {
   doc : Document,
   colliding : SkipListText,
   assignments : List ChapterAssignment,
   chapter-names : List Text,
   parse-bag : DiagnosticBag,
   heap-marks : List Integer,
   phase-metrics : List PhaseMetrics
  }

  ScopeResult = record {
   scoped : AChapter,
   ctor-names : List Text,
   renames : List RenameEntry,
   colliding : SkipListText,
   assignments : List ChapterAssignment,
   resolve-bag : DiagnosticBag,
   heap-marks : List Integer,
   phase-metrics : List PhaseMetrics
  }

  CompileChecked = record {
   scoped : AChapter,
   all-bindings : List TypeBinding,
   ust : UnificationState,
   ctor-names : List Text,
   renames : List RenameEntry,
   colliding : SkipListText,
   assignments : List ChapterAssignment,
   bag : DiagnosticBag,
   heap-marks : List Integer,
   phase-metrics : List PhaseMetrics
  }

  halted-frontend : DiagnosticBag, List Integer -> CompileFrontend
  halted-frontend (bag) (marks) =
   let blank-name = Name { value = "" }
   in let blank-ir = IRChapter {
       name = blank-name,
       defs = [],
       effect-op-names = [],
       span = synthetic-span
      }
   in CompileFrontend {
    type-defs = [],
    text-meta = IRTextMeta { chapter-title = "", prose = "", section-titles = [], ctor-names = [], prose-blocks = [], annotations = [], ground-effects = [] },
    ir = blank-ir,
    cdx-ir = blank-ir,
    sorted-type-defs = [],
    renames = [],
    bag = bag,
    heap-marks = marks,
    phase-metrics = []
   }

  halted-checked : DiagnosticBag, List Integer -> CompileChecked
  halted-checked (bag) (marks) =
   CompileChecked {
    scoped = AChapter {
      name = Name { value = "" },
      defs = [],
      type-defs = [],
      effect-defs = [],
      class-defs = [],
      instance-defs = [],
      citations = [],
      ground-effects = [],
      chapter-title = "",
      prose = "",
      prose-blocks = [],
      annotations = [],
      section-titles = [],
      rt-names = [],
      rt-budgets = [],
      conversions = [],
      span = synthetic-span
    },
    all-bindings = [],
    ust = empty-unification-state,
    ctor-names = [],
    renames = [],
    colliding = skip-list-text-empty,
    assignments = [],
    bag = bag,
    heap-marks = marks,
    phase-metrics = []
   }

  check-deck-overflow : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  check-deck-overflow (usage) (budget) =
   if usage > budget then 1 else 0

  deck-overflow-bag : Text -> DiagnosticBag
  deck-overflow-bag (phase-name) =
   bag-from-list [make-error cdx-deck-overflow ("Deck overflow in " & phase-name & "; deck floor exceeded") synthetic-span]

  no-overflow-bag : DiagnosticBag = bag-from-list []

  serial-byte : Integer -> Integer
  serial-byte (b) = port-out-byte 1016 b

  serial-hex-digit : Integer -> Integer
  serial-hex-digit (d) =
   if d < 10 then serial-byte (48 + d)
   else serial-byte (87 + d)

  serial-hex-loop : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  serial-hex-loop (val) (shift) =
   if shift < 0 then 0
   else let d = bit-and (bit-shru val shift) 15
   in let w = serial-hex-digit d
   in serial-hex-loop val (shift - 4)

  serial-hex : Integer -> Integer
  serial-hex (val) = serial-hex-loop val 60

  serial-newline : Integer = serial-byte 10

  serial-text-loop : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  serial-text-loop (t) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0
   else let w = serial-byte (to-unicode (char-code (char-at t i)))
   in serial-text-loop t (i + 1) len

  serial-text : Text -> Integer
  serial-text (t) = serial-text-loop t 0 (text-length t)

  phase-trace-mark : Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  phase-trace-mark (on) (byte) =
   if on
    then let w = serial-text ("PH:" & poison-phase-label byte)
    in serial-newline
   else 0

  scan-deck-dangling : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  scan-deck-dangling (addr) (deck-end) (bivy-lo) (bivy-hi) (count) =
   if addr >= deck-end then count
   else let val = peek-qword addr 0
   in if val >= bivy-lo
      then if val < bivy-hi
           then scan-deck-dangling (addr + 8) deck-end bivy-lo bivy-hi (count + 1)
           else scan-deck-dangling (addr + 8) deck-end bivy-lo bivy-hi count
      else scan-deck-dangling (addr + 8) deck-end bivy-lo bivy-hi count

  check-escape-invariant : PhaseMetrics, Text -> DiagnosticBag
  check-escape-invariant (m) (phase-name) =
   let violations = scan-deck-dangling (m.deck-origin) (m.deck-end) (m.bivy-origin) (m.bivy-hwm) 0
   in if violations > 0
      then bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("Escape invariant: " & show violations & " deck pointer(s) target bivy in " & phase-name) synthetic-span]
      else bag-from-list []

  escape-check-bag : Boolean, PhaseMetrics, Text -> DiagnosticBag
  escape-check-bag (enabled) (m) (phase-name) =
   if enabled then check-escape-invariant m phase-name
   else bag-from-list []

  precise-escape-bag : Boolean, Integer, Text, PhaseMetrics, Text -> DiagnosticBag
  precise-escape-bag (enabled) (root-addr) (type-name) (m) (phase-name) =
   if enabled then
    let escaped = pmap-escape-count root-addr type-name (m.bivy-origin) (m.bivy-hwm)
    in let reached = pmap-escape-reached root-addr type-name
    in bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("PRECISE escape (" & phase-name & "/" & type-name & "): " & show escaped & " ptr(s) into bivy, reach " & show reached) synthetic-span]
   else bag-from-list []

  pmap-selftest-bag : Boolean -> DiagnosticBag
  pmap-selftest-bag (enabled) =
   if enabled then pmap-selftest-result pmap-self-test
   else bag-from-list []

  deck-live-warn : Text, Text, Integer -> DiagnosticBag
  deck-live-warn (root-name) (seg) (n) =
   bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("DECK-LIVE " & root-name & " -> " & seg & ": " & show n & " ref(s)") synthetic-span]

  sum-tylist-lens : List (List CodexType), Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-tylist-lens (ls) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else sum-tylist-lens ls (i + 1) len (acc + list-length (list-at ls i))

  sum-fieldlist-lens : List (List RecordField), Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-fieldlist-lens (ls) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else sum-fieldlist-lens ls (i + 1) len (acc + list-length (list-at ls i))

  sum-ctorlist-lens : List (List SumCtor), Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-ctorlist-lens (ls) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else sum-ctorlist-lens ls (i + 1) len (acc + list-length (list-at ls i))

  sum-labellist-lens : List (List EffectLabel), Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-labellist-lens (ls) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else sum-labellist-lens ls (i + 1) len (acc + list-length (list-at ls i))

  pmap-selftest-result : Integer -> DiagnosticBag
  pmap-selftest-result (r) =
   if r == 3 then bag-from-list []
   else if r == 0 - 2 then bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation "pmap-walk self-test SKIPPED: this host has no self type table, so there is no pointer map to walk" synthetic-span]
   else bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("pmap-walk self-test FAILED: got " & show r & " (expect 3)") synthetic-span]

  compile-lex : Text, Integer, CompileFlags -> LexResult
  compile-lex (source) (h0) (flags) =
   let mountain-base = init-phase-allocator
   in let lex-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lex-floor demand-lex-guard-band
   in let lex-base = build lex-deck-height
   in let lex-phase = phase-start
   in let tok-result = tokenize-into source 1 (flags.prose) (lex-base + lex-deck-height)
   in let tokens = tok-result.tokens
   in let lex-errors = tok-result.errors
   in let lex-bag = deck-record (bag-from-list lex-errors)
   in if bag-has-errors lex-bag
      then LexResult { tokens = [], bag = lex-bag, heap-marks = [h0], phase-metrics = [] }
      else let lex-metrics = phase-measure lex-phase "LEX"
   in let lex-ov = check-deck-overflow (lex-metrics.deck-end - lex-metrics.deck-origin) lex-deck-height
   in let lex-ov-bag = if lex-ov == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "LEX" else no-overflow-bag
   in let lex-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) lex-metrics "LEX"
   in let lex-bag2 = deck-record (bag-merge-all [lex-bag, lex-ov-bag, lex-esc-bag])
   in let result = deck-record (LexResult { tokens = tokens, bag = lex-bag2, heap-marks = [h0], phase-metrics = [lex-metrics] })
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-lex
   in result

  compile-parse : List Token, Text, Integer, List PhaseMetrics, CompileFlags -> ParseResult
  compile-parse (tokens) (source) (h0) (prior-metrics) (flags) =
   let h1 = __heap-save
   in let parse-keep-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-parse-keep-floor demand-parse-guard-band
   in let parse-base = build parse-keep-height
   in let parse-guard = pitch demand-parse-guard-band
   in let parse-scratch-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-parse-scratch-floor demand-parse-scratch-guard-band
   in let scratch-base = build (parse-scratch-height + (if parse-guard < 0 then 1 else 0))
   in let scratch-ceiling = scratch-base + parse-scratch-height
   in let parse-phase = phase-start
   in let st = make-parse-state tokens source
   in let scan-mark = __heap-save
   in let scan-floor = __deck-pos
   in let scan0 = scan-document st
   in let scan = deck-record (ScanResult {
    type-defs = [],
    effect-defs = [],
    class-defs = [],
    instance-defs = [],
    def-headers = for h in (scan0.def-headers) -> DefHeader { name = copy-sx-token scan-floor (h.name), params = copy-sx-tokens scan-floor (h.params), ann = for a in (h.ann) -> copy-sx-typeann scan-floor a, body-pos = h.body-pos, chapter-slug = copy-sx-text scan-floor (h.chapter-slug) },
    citations = [],
    chapter-title = "",
    section-titles = [],
    chapter-names = copy-sx-texts scan-floor (scan0.chapter-names)
   })
   in let scan-back = __heap-restore scan-mark
   in let h2 = __heap-save
   in let assignments-s = if deck-short-of scratch-ceiling demand-parse-scratch-guard-band then [] else deck-record (build-all-assignments source (scan.def-headers) 0 [])
   in let colliding-s = if deck-short-of scratch-ceiling demand-parse-scratch-guard-band then skip-list-text-empty else deck-record (find-colliding-names assignments-s)
   in let h3 = __heap-save
   in let entry-dup-bag = bag-from-list (check-entry-point-duplicate assignments-s)
   in let bset = deck-record (skip-list-text-from-list builtin-names)
   in let shadow-bag = bag-from-list (check-shadowed-builtins (scan.def-headers) bset 0 (list-length (scan.def-headers)) [])
   in let collide-bag = bag-from-list (check-cross-chapter-collisions (scan.def-headers) colliding-s assignments-s 0 (list-length (scan.def-headers)) [])
   in let doc = parse-document (make-parse-state tokens source) scratch-ceiling
   in let prose-diags = if flags.prose then check-prose-consistency doc & check-prose-banned doc else []
   in let h4 = __heap-save
   in let parse-sat = if deck-short-of scratch-ceiling demand-parse-scratch-guard-band then 1 else 0
   in let parse-bag = deck-record (bag-merge-all [doc.parse-bag, entry-dup-bag, shadow-bag, collide-bag, bag-from-list prose-diags])
   in if bag-has-errors parse-bag
      then ParseResult { doc = doc, colliding = colliding-s, assignments = assignments-s, chapter-names = scan.chapter-names, parse-bag = parse-bag, heap-marks = [h0, h1, h2, h3, h4], phase-metrics = prior-metrics }
      else let parse-metrics = phase-measure parse-phase "PARSE"
   in let parse-ov = check-deck-overflow (parse-metrics.deck-end - parse-metrics.deck-origin) parse-scratch-height
   in let dset-keep = __deck-set parse-base
   in let keep-phase = phase-start
   in let gcopy = deck-record (copy-sx-document-guarded parse-base (parse-base + parse-keep-height) doc)
   in let doc2 = gcopy.doc
   in let assignments = deck-record (for a in assignments-s -> deck-record (ChapterAssignment { def-name = copy-sx-text parse-base (a.def-name), chapter-slug = copy-sx-text parse-base (a.chapter-slug) }))
   in let chapter-names-k = deck-record (copy-sx-texts parse-base (scan.chapter-names))
   in let colliding-names = skip-list-text-to-list colliding-s
   in let colliding = deck-record (skip-list-text-from-list (copy-sx-texts parse-base colliding-names))
   in let parse-bag-d = deck-record (copy-sx-bag parse-base parse-bag)
   in let keep-metrics = phase-measure keep-phase "PARSE-KEEP"
   in let keep-ov = check-deck-overflow (keep-metrics.deck-end - keep-metrics.deck-origin) parse-keep-height
   in let parse-metrics-d = deck-record (PhaseMetrics {
    label = parse-metrics.label,
    bivy-origin = parse-metrics.bivy-origin,
    bivy-hwm = parse-metrics.bivy-hwm,
    deck-origin = parse-metrics.deck-origin,
    deck-end = parse-metrics.deck-end
   })
   in let scratch-top = if parse-metrics.deck-end > parse-metrics.bivy-hwm then parse-metrics.deck-end else parse-metrics.bivy-hwm
   in let keep-esc-metrics = PhaseMetrics {
    label = keep-metrics.label,
    bivy-origin = parse-metrics.deck-origin,
    bivy-hwm = scratch-top,
    deck-origin = keep-metrics.deck-origin,
    deck-end = keep-metrics.deck-end
   }
   in let keep-esc-bag = deck-record (escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) keep-esc-metrics "PARSE")
   in let parse-precise-bag = deck-record (precise-escape-bag (flags.escape-check) (address-of doc2) "Document" keep-esc-metrics "PARSE")
   in let parse-ov-bag = if parse-ov == 1 | parse-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "PARSE" else no-overflow-bag
   in let keep-ov-bag = if keep-ov == 1 | gcopy.sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "PARSE-KEEP" else no-overflow-bag
   in let parse-bag2 = deck-record (bag-merge-all [parse-bag-d, parse-ov-bag, keep-ov-bag, keep-esc-bag, parse-precise-bag])
   in let keep-metrics-d = deck-record (PhaseMetrics {
    label = keep-metrics.label,
    bivy-origin = keep-metrics.bivy-origin,
    bivy-hwm = keep-metrics.bivy-hwm,
    deck-origin = keep-metrics.deck-origin,
    deck-end = keep-metrics.deck-end
   })
   in let parse-phase-metrics = deck-record (list-push (list-push prior-metrics parse-metrics-d) keep-metrics-d)
   in let result = deck-record (ParseResult { doc = doc2, colliding = colliding, assignments = assignments, chapter-names = chapter-names-k, parse-bag = parse-bag2, heap-marks = [h0, h1, h2, h3, h4], phase-metrics = parse-phase-metrics })
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-parse
   in result

  compile-desugar-and-scope : Text, ParseResult, Text, CompileFlags, Integer, Integer -> ScopeResult
  compile-desugar-and-scope (source) (pr) (chapter-name) (flags) (fk-base) (fk-height) =
   let dset-keep = __deck-set fk-base
   in let fk-ceiling = fk-base + fk-height - demand-frontend-keep-guard-band
   in let desugar-phase = phase-start
   in let dr = deck-record (desugar-document source (pr.doc) chapter-name fk-ceiling)
   in let ast = dr.dr-chapter
   in let desugar-metrics = phase-measure desugar-phase "DESUGAR"
   in let desugar-ov = check-deck-overflow (desugar-metrics.deck-end - desugar-metrics.deck-origin) fk-height
   in let keep-phase = phase-start
   in let fkcopy = deck-record (relocate-chapter-tail fk-base fk-ceiling ast (dr.dr-sat))
   in let ast2 = fkcopy.chapter
   in let keep-cursor = __deck-pos
   in let keep-escapes = if flags.escape-check then pmap-escape-count-quiet (address-of ast2) "AChapter" keep-cursor __heap-save else 0
   in let assignments = deck-record (for a in (pr.assignments) -> deck-record (ChapterAssignment { def-name = copy-sx-text fk-base (a.def-name), chapter-slug = copy-sx-text fk-base (a.chapter-slug) }))
   in let chapter-names = deck-record (copy-sx-texts fk-base (pr.chapter-names))
   in let colliding-names = skip-list-text-to-list (pr.colliding)
   in let colliding = deck-record (skip-list-text-from-list (copy-sx-texts fk-base colliding-names))
   in let parse-bag-d = deck-record (copy-sx-bag fk-base (pr.parse-bag))
   in let heap-marks-d = deck-record (for h in (pr.heap-marks) -> h)
   in let desugar-metrics-d = deck-record (PhaseMetrics { label = desugar-metrics.label, bivy-origin = desugar-metrics.bivy-origin, bivy-hwm = desugar-metrics.bivy-hwm, deck-origin = desugar-metrics.deck-origin, deck-end = desugar-metrics.deck-end })
   in let phase-metrics-d = deck-record (for m in (pr.phase-metrics) -> deck-record (PhaseMetrics { label = m.label, bivy-origin = m.bivy-origin, bivy-hwm = m.bivy-hwm, deck-origin = m.deck-origin, deck-end = m.deck-end }))
   in let keep-metrics = phase-measure keep-phase "DESUGAR-KEEP"
   in let keep-ov = check-deck-overflow (keep-metrics.deck-end - keep-metrics.deck-origin) fk-height
   in let keep-note-bag = deck-record (if flags.phase-trace | flags.escape-check then bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("DESUGAR-KEEP fk-base=" & show fk-base & " origin=" & show (keep-metrics.deck-origin) & " end=" & show (keep-metrics.deck-end) & " used=" & show (keep-metrics.deck-end - keep-metrics.deck-origin) & " desugar-deck=" & show (desugar-metrics.deck-end - desugar-metrics.deck-origin) & " desugar-bivy=" & show (desugar-metrics.bivy-hwm - desugar-metrics.bivy-origin) & " escapes-above-keep=" & show keep-escapes) synthetic-span] else no-overflow-bag)
   in let desugar-ov-bag = deck-record (if desugar-ov == 1 | dr.dr-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "DESUGAR" else no-overflow-bag)
   in let keep-ov-bag = deck-record (if keep-ov == 1 | fkcopy.sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "DESUGAR-KEEP" else no-overflow-bag)
   in let desugar-esc-bag = deck-record (escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) desugar-metrics "DESUGAR")
   in let desugar-sealed = compact-phase flags poison-desugar
   in let h5 = __heap-save
   in let scope-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-scope-floor demand-scope-guard-band
   in let scope-base = build scope-deck-height
   in let scope-ceiling = scope-base + scope-deck-height
   in let scope-origin = __deck-pos
   in let scope-phase = phase-start
   in let scoped = scope-achapter ast2 colliding assignments scope-ceiling
   in let h6 = __heap-save
   in let cite-bag = deck-record (check-duplicate-cites (scoped.citations))
   in let missing-cite-bag = deck-record (check-cites-resolve (scoped.citations) assignments chapter-names)
   in let resolve-result = resolve-chapter scoped colliding assignments scope-ceiling
   in let resolve-bag = deck-record (bag-merge-all [parse-bag-d, cite-bag, missing-cite-bag, resolve-result.bag, desugar-ov-bag, keep-ov-bag, desugar-esc-bag, keep-note-bag])
   in let scope-end = __deck-pos
   in let scope-sat = if deck-short-of scope-ceiling demand-scope-guard-band then 1 else 0
   in let scope-metrics = phase-measure scope-phase "SCOPE"
   in let scope-ov = check-deck-overflow (scope-end - scope-origin) scope-deck-height
   in let scope-ov-bag = if scope-ov == 1 | scope-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "SCOPE" else no-overflow-bag
   in let scope-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) scope-metrics "SCOPE"
   in let scope-precise-bag = precise-escape-bag (flags.escape-check) (address-of scoped) "AChapter" scope-metrics "SCOPE"
   in let resolve-bag2 = deck-record (bag-merge-all [resolve-bag, scope-ov-bag, scope-esc-bag, scope-precise-bag])
   in let hm1 = deck-record (list-push heap-marks-d h5)
   in let scope-heap-marks = deck-record (list-push hm1 h6)
   in let pm1 = deck-record (list-push phase-metrics-d desugar-metrics-d)
   in let scope-phase-metrics = deck-record (list-push pm1 scope-metrics)
   in let global-renames = deck-record (build-global-rename-table assignments colliding)
   in let result = deck-record (ScopeResult {
    scoped = scoped,
    ctor-names = resolve-result.ctor-names,
    renames = global-renames,
    colliding = colliding,
    assignments = assignments,
    resolve-bag = resolve-bag2,
    heap-marks = scope-heap-marks,
    phase-metrics = scope-phase-metrics
   })
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-scope
   in result

 The CHECK deck is measured TWICE and the two are not redundant. `check-metrics`
 is taken before the resolve tail because `memo-graph` sizes the memo table from
 it, so moving it later makes that sizing depend on itself. `CHECK-RESOLVE` is
 the same deck at its true end, after `resolved-env`, `sorted-all0` and
 `sorted-et0`, which are the deck-recorded allocations `post-ov-bag` exists to
 catch. Reading CHECK's requirement off the first number understates it by 7 to
 29 per cent, measured over four units 2026-08-09.

  compile-type-check : ScopeResult, Integer, CompileFlags -> CompileChecked
  compile-type-check (sr) (source-len) (flags) =
   let check-units = count-typecheck-units (sr.scoped.type-defs) 0 (list-length (sr.scoped.type-defs)) 0
   in let check-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-check-floor demand-check-guard-band
   in let keep-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-check-keep-floor 0
   in let keep-base = build keep-height
   in let check-base = build check-deck-height
   in let check-ceiling = check-base + check-deck-height
   in let check-origin = __deck-pos
   in let check-phase = phase-start
   in let check-result = deck-record (check-chapter (sr.scoped) (sr.renames) (sr.colliding) (sr.assignments) check-ceiling check-base keep-base (keep-base + keep-height - 4194304) (if flags.poison-compact then poison-check else 0))
   in let batch-bag = if flags.phase-trace then deck-record (bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation (check-result.batch-note) synthetic-span]) else no-overflow-bag
   in let h8 = __heap-save
   in let cap-diags = deck-record (check-opening-capabilities (check-result.types))
   in let check-bag0 = deck-record (bag-merge-all [sr.resolve-bag, check-result.state.bag, bag-from-list cap-diags])
   in let check-end = __deck-pos
   in let check-sat = if deck-short-of check-ceiling demand-check-guard-band then 1 else 0
   in let check-metrics = phase-measure check-phase "CHECK"
   in let check-ov = check-deck-overflow (check-end - check-origin) check-deck-height
   in let check-ov-bag = if check-ov == 1 | check-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "CHECK" else no-overflow-bag
   in let check-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) check-metrics "CHECK"
   in let cost-bag = deck-record (cost-growth-bag (flags.cost-report) (sr.scoped.defs) (check-result.state))
   in let check-bag = deck-record (bag-merge-all [check-bag0, check-ov-bag, check-esc-bag, cost-bag, batch-bag])
   in let check-heap-marks = deck-record (list-push (sr.heap-marks) h8)
   in let check-phase-metrics = deck-record (list-push (sr.phase-metrics) check-metrics)
   in if bag-has-errors check-bag
      then halted-checked check-bag check-heap-marks
      else let pre-sorted = deck-record (sort-bindings (check-result.types & check-result.env.bindings))
      in let pre-et = deck-record (sort-expr-types (check-result.state.expr-types))
      in let post-ov-bag = if __deck-pos > check-ceiling then deck-overflow-bag "CHECK" else no-overflow-bag
      in let memo-pop0 = list-length pre-et / 4 + list-length pre-sorted + 65536
      in let memo-graph = (check-metrics.deck-end - check-metrics.deck-origin) / 64
      in let memo-pop = if memo-graph > memo-pop0 then memo-graph else memo-pop0
      in let memo-slots = memo-pow2-at-least (memo-pop * 2) 4096
      in let memo-table = pitch (memo-slots * 8)
      in let memo-z = __memset memo-table 0 (memo-slots * 8)
      in let cons-slots = memo-pow2-at-least (memo-pop / 2) 4096
      in let cons-table = pitch (cons-slots * 16)
      in let cons-z = __memset cons-table 0 (cons-slots * 16)
      in let tcons-slots = memo-pow2-at-least (memo-pop / 4) 4096
      in let tcons-table = pitch (tcons-slots * 16)
      in let tcons-z = __memset tcons-table 0 (tcons-slots * 16)
      in let mc = MemoCtx {
       mc-table = memo-table,
       mc-slots = memo-slots,
       mc-shift = memo-shift-for memo-slots 64,
       mc-floor = check-base,
       mc-ty = __list-with-capacity memo-pop,
       mc-text = __list-with-capacity memo-pop,
       mc-name = __list-with-capacity (memo-pop / 2 + 16),
       mc-row = __list-with-capacity (memo-pop / 16 + 16),
       mc-tylist = __list-with-capacity (memo-pop / 4 + 16),
       mc-fieldlist = __list-with-capacity (memo-pop / 4 + 16),
       mc-ctorlist = __list-with-capacity (memo-pop / 4 + 16),
       mc-labellist = __list-with-capacity (memo-pop / 4 + 16),
       mc-cons = cons-table,
       mc-cons-slots = cons-slots,
       mc-cons-shift = memo-shift-for cons-slots 64,
       mc-cap-ty = memo-pop,
       mc-cap-text = memo-pop,
       mc-cap-name = memo-pop / 2 + 16,
       mc-cap-row = memo-pop / 16 + 16,
       mc-cap-list = memo-pop / 4 + 16,
       mc-ceiling = keep-base + keep-height - 4194304,
       mc-tcons = tcons-table,
       mc-tcons-slots = tcons-slots,
       mc-tcons-shift = memo-shift-for tcons-slots 64
      }
      in let check-resolve-metrics = phase-measure check-phase "CHECK-RESOLVE"
      in let all-phase-metrics = deck-record (list-push check-phase-metrics check-resolve-metrics)
      in let tail-scratch = __deck-pos
      in let keep-set = __deck-set (check-result.keep-end)
      in let keep-phase = phase-start
      in let tail-bivy = __heap-save
      in let acc-all = deck-record (__list-with-capacity (list-length pre-sorted))
      in let (sorted-all, kc-all) = tail-resolve-bindings (check-result.state) pre-sorted 0 (list-length pre-sorted) acc-all mc tail-scratch tail-bivy __deck-pos
      in let acc-et = deck-record (__list-with-capacity (list-length pre-et))
      in let (sorted-et, kc-et) = tail-resolve-expr-types (check-result.state) pre-et 0 (list-length pre-et) acc-et mc tail-scratch tail-bivy __deck-pos
      in let tail-rewind = __heap-restore tail-bivy
      in let tail-memo-z = __memset (mc.mc-table) 0 (mc.mc-slots * 8)
      in let tail-bag = if flags.phase-trace then deck-record (bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("CHECK-TAIL binds=" & show (list-length sorted-all) & " et=" & show (list-length sorted-et) & " keep-used=" & show (kc-et - check-result.keep-end)) synthetic-span]) else no-overflow-bag
      in let kept-subs = deck-record (mcopy-types (check-result.state.substitutions) 0 mc)
      in let kept-rows = deck-record (mcopy-rows (check-result.state.row-substitutions) mc)
      in let kept-bag = deck-record (copy-sx-bag keep-base check-bag)
      in let kept-ust-bag = deck-record (copy-sx-bag keep-base (check-result.state.bag))
      in let kept-marks = deck-record (copy-list-with-headroom check-heap-marks 0)
      in let kept-metrics = deck-record (for m in all-phase-metrics -> deck-record (PhaseMetrics { label = m.label, bivy-origin = m.bivy-origin, bivy-hwm = m.bivy-hwm, deck-origin = m.deck-origin, deck-end = m.deck-end }))
      in let keep-metrics = phase-measure keep-phase "CHECK-KEEP"
      in let keep-ov = check-deck-overflow (keep-metrics.deck-end - keep-metrics.deck-origin) keep-height
      in let keep-ov-bag = if keep-ov == 1 | keep-metrics.deck-end >= mc.mc-ceiling | check-result.keep-overflow then deck-overflow-bag "CHECK-KEEP" else no-overflow-bag
      in let keep-metrics-bag = if flags.phase-trace then deck-record (bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("CHECK-KEEP-METRICS origin=" & show (keep-metrics.deck-origin) & " end=" & show (keep-metrics.deck-end) & " height=" & show keep-height & " ceiling=" & show (mc.mc-ceiling) & " keep-base=" & show keep-base & " walk-keep-end=" & show (check-result.keep-end) & " ov=" & show keep-ov & " walk-overflow=" & (if check-result.keep-overflow then "1" else "0")) synthetic-span]) else no-overflow-bag
      in let keep-stat-bag = if flags.escape-check then deck-record (bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("KEEP-STAT used=" & show (keep-metrics.deck-end - keep-metrics.deck-origin) & " ty=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-ty)) & " text=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-text)) & " name=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-name)) & " row=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-row)) & " tylists=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-tylist)) & "/" & show (sum-tylist-lens (mc.mc-tylist) 0 (list-length (mc.mc-tylist)) 0) & " fieldlists=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-fieldlist)) & "/" & show (sum-fieldlist-lens (mc.mc-fieldlist) 0 (list-length (mc.mc-fieldlist)) 0) & " ctorlists=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-ctorlist)) & "/" & show (sum-ctorlist-lens (mc.mc-ctorlist) 0 (list-length (mc.mc-ctorlist)) 0) & " labellists=" & show (list-length (mc.mc-labellist)) & "/" & show (sum-labellist-lens (mc.mc-labellist) 0 (list-length (mc.mc-labellist)) 0) & " subs=" & show (list-length kept-subs) & " et=" & show (list-length sorted-et) & " binds=" & show (list-length sorted-all)) synthetic-span]) else bag-from-list []
      in let final-bag = deck-record (bag-merge-all [kept-bag, keep-ov-bag, keep-stat-bag, post-ov-bag, tail-bag, keep-metrics-bag])
      in let pre-sorted-ust = deck-record (UnificationState {
       substitutions = kept-subs,
       row-substitutions = kept-rows,
       next-id = check-result.state.next-id,
       next-row-id = check-result.state.next-row-id,
       bag = kept-ust-bag,
       expr-types = sorted-et,
       effect-exempt = False,
       scope-grants = [],
       chapter-grounds = [],
       dirty-slots = []
      })
      in let result0 = deck-record (CompileChecked {
       scoped = sr.scoped,
       all-bindings = sorted-all,
       ust = pre-sorted-ust,
       ctor-names = sr.ctor-names,
       renames = sr.renames,
       colliding = sr.colliding,
       assignments = sr.assignments,
       bag = final-bag,
       heap-marks = kept-marks,
       phase-metrics = kept-metrics
      })
      in let check-scratch-top = if check-metrics.deck-end > check-metrics.bivy-hwm then check-metrics.deck-end else check-metrics.bivy-hwm
      in let check-esc-metrics = PhaseMetrics {
       label = keep-metrics.label,
       bivy-origin = check-metrics.deck-origin,
       bivy-hwm = check-scratch-top,
       deck-origin = keep-metrics.deck-origin,
       deck-end = keep-metrics.deck-end
      }
      in let check-precise-bag = deck-record (precise-escape-bag (flags.escape-check) (address-of result0) "CompileChecked" check-esc-metrics "CHECK")
      in let result = __record-set result0 "bag" (deck-record (bag-merge-all [final-bag, check-precise-bag]))
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-check
   in result

  compile-checked : Text, Text, CompileFlags -> CompileChecked
  compile-checked (source) (chapter-name) (flags) =
   let fk-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-frontend-keep-floor demand-frontend-keep-guard-band
   in let fk-base = build fk-height
   in let h0 = __heap-save
   in let lr = compile-lex source h0 flags
   in if bag-has-errors (lr.bag)
      then halted-checked (lr.bag) (lr.heap-marks)
      else let pr = compile-parse (lr.tokens) source h0 (lr.phase-metrics) flags
   in if bag-has-errors (pr.parse-bag)
      then halted-checked (pr.parse-bag) (pr.heap-marks)
      else let sr = compile-desugar-and-scope source pr chapter-name flags fk-base fk-height
   in if bag-has-errors (sr.resolve-bag)
      then halted-checked (sr.resolve-bag) (sr.heap-marks)
      else compile-type-check sr (text-length source) flags

  compile-frontend : Text, Text, CompileFlags -> CompileFrontend
  compile-frontend (source) (chapter-name) (flags) =
   compile-frontend-passes source chapter-name flags False

  compile-frontend-ir : Text, Text, CompileFlags -> CompileFrontend
  compile-frontend-ir (source) (chapter-name) (flags) =
   compile-frontend-passes source chapter-name flags True

  frontend-bag-with-passes : DiagnosticBag, DiagnosticBag, DiagnosticBag, DiagnosticBag, IRPassResult -> DiagnosticBag
  frontend-bag-with-passes (base) (ov) (esc) (precise) (passed) =
   bag-merge-all [base, ov, esc, precise,
     deck-record (ir-check-violation-bag (passed.violations)),
     deck-record (occ-info-bag (passed.infos))]

  compile-frontend-passes : Text, Text, CompileFlags, Boolean -> CompileFrontend
  compile-frontend-passes (source) (chapter-name) (flags) (run-passes) =
   let checked = compile-checked source chapter-name flags
   in if bag-has-errors (checked.bag) then halted-frontend (checked.bag) (checked.heap-marks)
   else let lower-keep-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lower-keep-floor 0
   in let lower-keep-base = build lower-keep-height
   in let lower-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lower-floor demand-lower-guard-band
   in let lower-base = build lower-deck-height
   in let lower-ceiling = lower-base + lower-deck-height
   in let set-lo = __deck-set lower-keep-base
   in let lower-origin = __deck-pos
   in let lower-phase = phase-start
   in let set-ls = __deck-set lower-base
   in let (ir-raw, lower-keep-end) = deck-record (lower-chapter (checked.scoped) (checked.all-bindings) (checked.ust) (checked.ctor-names) (checked.renames) (checked.colliding) (checked.assignments) lower-ceiling run-passes lower-keep-base (lower-keep-base + lower-keep-height))
   in let lower-keep-sat = lower-keep-end + demand-lower-guard-band >= lower-keep-base + lower-keep-height
   in let lower-keep-bag = deck-record (if flags.phase-trace then bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("LOWER-KEEP used=" & integer-to-text (lower-keep-end - lower-keep-base) & " of " & integer-to-text lower-keep-height) synthetic-span] else no-overflow-bag)
   in let passed = if run-passes then deck-record (run-ir-pipeline (flags.pipeline) ir-raw (flags.phase-trace))
      else IRPassResult { chapter = ir-raw, violations = [], infos = [] }
   in let ir-passed = passed.chapter
   in let ir = passed.chapter
   in let tm = deck-record (IRTextMeta {
    chapter-title = checked.scoped.chapter-title,
    prose = checked.scoped.prose,
    section-titles = checked.scoped.section-titles,
    ctor-names = checked.ctor-names,
    prose-blocks = checked.scoped.prose-blocks,
    annotations = checked.scoped.annotations,
    ground-effects = checked.scoped.ground-effects
   })
   in let lower-end = __deck-pos
   in let lower-sat = if list-length (ir-raw.defs) < list-length (checked.scoped.defs) then 1 else 0
   in let lower-metrics = phase-measure lower-phase "LOWER"
   in let lower-ov = check-deck-overflow (lower-end - lower-origin) lower-deck-height
   in let lower-precise-bag = deck-record (precise-escape-bag (flags.escape-check) (address-of ir) "IRChapter" lower-metrics "LOWER")
   in let blank-ir = IRChapter { name = ir.name, defs = [], effect-op-names = ir.effect-op-names, span = ir.span }
   in let pm1 = deck-record (list-push (checked.phase-metrics) lower-metrics)
   in let lower-ov-bag = if lower-ov == 1 | lower-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "LOWER" else if lower-keep-sat then deck-overflow-bag "LOWER-KEEP" else no-overflow-bag
   in let lower-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) lower-metrics "LOWER"
   in let fe-bag = deck-record (frontend-bag-with-passes (checked.bag) (bag-merge-all [lower-ov-bag, lower-keep-bag]) lower-esc-bag lower-precise-bag passed)
   in let lower-sealed = compact-phase flags poison-lower
   in let resolved-defs = if run-passes then
       let resolve-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-resolve-floor demand-resolve-guard-band
       in let resolve-base = build resolve-deck-height
       in let resolve-ceiling = resolve-base + resolve-deck-height
       in let type-map = deck-record (build-type-def-map (checked.scoped.type-defs) 0 (list-length (checked.scoped.type-defs)) [])
       in let stds = deck-record (sort-bindings (type-map & checked.all-bindings))
       in deck-record (rewrite-ir-defs stds (ir.defs) resolve-ceiling)
      else ir.defs
   in let ir-resolved = __record-set ir "defs" resolved-defs
   in let result = deck-record (CompileFrontend {
    type-defs = checked.scoped.type-defs,
    text-meta = tm,
     ir = ir-resolved,
    cdx-ir = blank-ir,
    sorted-type-defs = [],
    renames = checked.renames,
    bag = fe-bag,
    heap-marks = checked.heap-marks,
    phase-metrics = pm1
   })
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-frontend
   in result

  compile-frontend-cdx : Text, Text, CompileFlags -> CompileFrontend
  compile-frontend-cdx (source) (chapter-name) (flags) =
   let checked = compile-checked source chapter-name flags
   in if bag-has-errors (checked.bag) then halted-frontend (checked.bag) (checked.heap-marks)
   else let lower-keep-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lower-keep-floor 0
   in let lower-keep-base = build lower-keep-height
   in let lower-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lower-floor demand-lower-guard-band
   in let lower-base = build lower-deck-height
   in let lower-ceiling = lower-base + lower-deck-height
   in let set-lo = __deck-set lower-keep-base
   in let lower-origin = __deck-pos
   in let lower-phase = phase-start
   in let set-ls = __deck-set lower-base
   in let (ir-raw, lower-keep-end) = deck-record (lower-chapter (checked.scoped) (checked.all-bindings) (checked.ust) (checked.ctor-names) (checked.renames) (checked.colliding) (checked.assignments) lower-ceiling True lower-keep-base (lower-keep-base + lower-keep-height))
   in let lower-keep-sat = lower-keep-end + demand-lower-guard-band >= lower-keep-base + lower-keep-height
   in let lower-keep-bag = deck-record (if flags.phase-trace then bag-from-list [make-warning cdx-escape-violation ("LOWER-KEEP used=" & integer-to-text (lower-keep-end - lower-keep-base) & " of " & integer-to-text lower-keep-height) synthetic-span] else no-overflow-bag)
   in let passed = deck-record (run-ir-pipeline (flags.pipeline) ir-raw (flags.phase-trace))
   in let ir = passed.chapter
   in let ir-check-bag = deck-record (ir-check-violation-bag (passed.violations))
   in let occ-report-bag = deck-record (occ-info-bag (passed.infos))
   in let lower-end = __deck-pos
   in let lower-sat = if list-length (ir-raw.defs) < list-length (checked.scoped.defs) then 1 else 0
   in let lower-metrics = phase-measure lower-phase "LOWER"
   in let lower-ov = check-deck-overflow (lower-end - lower-origin) lower-deck-height
   in let lower-precise-bag = deck-record (precise-escape-bag (flags.escape-check) (address-of ir) "IRChapter" lower-metrics "LOWER")
   in let lower-sealed = compact-phase flags poison-lower
   in let resolve-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-resolve-floor demand-resolve-guard-band
   in let resolve-base = build resolve-deck-height
   in let resolve-ceiling = resolve-base + resolve-deck-height
   in let resolve-phase = phase-start
   in let type-map = deck-record (build-type-def-map (checked.scoped.type-defs) 0 (list-length (checked.scoped.type-defs)) [])
   in let stds = deck-record (sort-bindings (type-map & checked.all-bindings))
   in let resolved-defs = deck-record (rewrite-ir-defs stds (ir.defs) resolve-ceiling)
   in let resolve-sat = if deck-short-of resolve-ceiling demand-resolve-guard-band then 1 else 0
   in let resolve-metrics = phase-measure resolve-phase "RESOLVE"
   in let resolve-ov = check-deck-overflow (resolve-metrics.deck-end - resolve-metrics.deck-origin) resolve-deck-height
   in let lift-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lift-floor demand-lift-guard-band
   in let lift-base = build lift-deck-height
   in let lift-ceiling = lift-base + lift-deck-height
   in let lift-phase = phase-start
   in let cdx-chapter = deck-record (lift-lambdas (IRChapter {
    name = ir.name,
    defs = resolved-defs,
    effect-op-names = ir.effect-op-names,
    span = ir.span
   }) lift-ceiling)
   in let lift-sat = if deck-short-of lift-ceiling demand-lift-guard-band then 1 else 0
   in let lift-metrics = phase-measure lift-phase "LIFT"
   in let lift-ov = check-deck-overflow (lift-metrics.deck-end - lift-metrics.deck-origin) lift-deck-height
   in let tm = deck-record (IRTextMeta {
    chapter-title = checked.scoped.chapter-title,
    prose = checked.scoped.prose,
    section-titles = checked.scoped.section-titles,
    ctor-names = checked.ctor-names,
    prose-blocks = checked.scoped.prose-blocks,
    annotations = checked.scoped.annotations,
    ground-effects = checked.scoped.ground-effects
   })
   in let blank-ir = IRChapter { name = ir.name, defs = [], effect-op-names = ir.effect-op-names, span = ir.span }
   in let pm1 = deck-record (list-push (checked.phase-metrics) lower-metrics)
   in let pm2 = deck-record (list-push pm1 resolve-metrics)
   in let pm3 = deck-record (list-push pm2 lift-metrics)
   in let lower-ov-bag = if lower-ov == 1 | lower-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "LOWER" else if lower-keep-sat then deck-overflow-bag "LOWER-KEEP" else no-overflow-bag
   in let resolve-ov-bag = if resolve-ov == 1 | resolve-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "RESOLVE" else no-overflow-bag
   in let lift-ov-bag = if lift-ov == 1 | lift-sat == 1 then deck-overflow-bag "LIFT" else no-overflow-bag
   in let lower-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) lower-metrics "LOWER"
   in let resolve-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) resolve-metrics "RESOLVE"
   in let lift-esc-bag = escape-check-bag (flags.escape-check) lift-metrics "LIFT"
   in let dl-bag = if flags.escape-check then
       if list-length (checked.phase-metrics) >= 5 then
        let m-lex = list-at (checked.phase-metrics) 0
        in let m-scope = list-at (checked.phase-metrics) 3
        in let m-check = list-at (checked.phase-metrics) 4
        in let ir-root = address-of cdx-chapter
        in let stds-root = address-of stds
        in deck-record (bag-merge-all [
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "pre-scope" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (m-lex.deck-origin) (m-scope.deck-origin)),
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "scope" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (m-scope.deck-origin) (m-scope.deck-end)),
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "check" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (m-check.deck-origin) (m-check.deck-end)),
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "lower" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (lower-metrics.deck-origin) (lower-metrics.deck-end)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "pre-scope" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (m-lex.deck-origin) (m-scope.deck-origin)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "scope" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (m-scope.deck-origin) (m-scope.deck-end)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "check" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (m-check.deck-origin) (m-check.deck-end)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "lower" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (lower-metrics.deck-origin) (lower-metrics.deck-end)),
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "scope-scratch" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (m-scope.deck-end) (m-scope.bivy-hwm)),
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "check-scratch" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (m-check.deck-end) (m-check.bivy-hwm)),
         deck-live-warn "cdx-ir" "lower-scratch" (pmap-escape-count-quiet ir-root "IRChapter" (lower-metrics.deck-end) (lower-metrics.bivy-hwm)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "scope-scratch" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (m-scope.deck-end) (m-scope.bivy-hwm)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "check-scratch" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (m-check.deck-end) (m-check.bivy-hwm)),
         deck-live-warn "stds" "lower-scratch" (pmap-escape-list-quiet stds-root "TypeBinding" (lower-metrics.deck-end) (lower-metrics.bivy-hwm))])
       else bag-from-list []
      else bag-from-list []
   in let lir-report-bag = deck-record (occ-info-bag (lir-report-if lir-target-x86 (flags.pipeline) cdx-chapter))
   in let lir-probe-bag = deck-record (occ-info-bag (lir-alloc-probe-if lir-target-a64-shape "A64" "lir-dump-a64" (flags.pipeline) cdx-chapter))
   in let lir-narrow-bag = deck-record (occ-info-bag (lir-alloc-probe-if lir-target-narrow-shape "NARROW" "lir-dump-a64" (flags.pipeline) cdx-chapter))
   in let fe-bag = deck-record (bag-merge-all [checked.bag, ir-check-bag, occ-report-bag, lir-report-bag, lir-probe-bag, lir-narrow-bag, lower-ov-bag, lower-keep-bag, resolve-ov-bag, lift-ov-bag, lower-esc-bag, resolve-esc-bag, lift-esc-bag, lower-precise-bag, dl-bag, pmap-selftest-bag True])
   in let result = deck-record (CompileFrontend {
    type-defs = checked.scoped.type-defs,
    text-meta = tm,
    ir = blank-ir,
    cdx-ir = cdx-chapter,
    sorted-type-defs = stds,
    renames = checked.renames,
    bag = fe-bag,
    heap-marks = checked.heap-marks,
    phase-metrics = pm3
   })
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-frontend
   in result

Section: Library Loading

  is-library-quire : Text -> Boolean
  is-library-quire (q) =
   if q == "Foreword" then True
   else if q == "Kernel" then True
   else if q == "OS" then True
   else if q == "Works" then True
   else if q == "Trust" then True
   else if q == "Net" then True
   else if q == "Verify" then True
   else if q == "Replay" then True
   else if q == "Sched" then True
   else if q == "Observe" then True
   else if q == "Game" then True
   else if q == "Signal" then True
   else if q == "Compress" then True
   else if q == "Encode" then True
   else if q == "Math" then True
   else if q == "Sim" then True
   else if q == "AI" then True
   else if q == "UI" then True
   else if q == "Punctual" then True
   else if q == "Dev" then True
   else False

  quire-to-dir : Text -> Text
  quire-to-dir (q) =
   if q == "Foreword" then "foreword/"
   else if q == "Kernel" then "kernel/"
   else if q == "OS" then "os/"
   else if q == "Works" then "works/"
   else if q == "Trust" then "os.trust/"
   else if q == "Net" then "os.net/"
   else if q == "Verify" then "os.verify/"
   else if q == "Replay" then "os.replay/"
   else if q == "Sched" then "os.sched/"
   else if q == "Observe" then "os.observe/"
   else if q == "Game" then "foreword.game/"
   else if q == "Signal" then "foreword.signal/"
   else if q == "Compress" then "foreword.compress/"
   else if q == "Encode" then "foreword.encode/"
   else if q == "Math" then "foreword.math/"
   else if q == "Sim" then "foreword.sim/"
   else if q == "AI" then "foreword.ai/"
   else if q == "UI" then "foreword.ui/"
   else if q == "Punctual" then "foreword.punctual/"
   else if q == "Dev" then "os.dev/"
   else "foreword/"

  filter-library-cites : List CitesDecl, Integer, Integer, List CitesDecl -> List CitesDecl
  filter-library-cites (citations) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let c = list-at citations i
   in if is-library-quire (token-text (c.quire))
      then filter-library-cites citations (i + 1) len (list-push acc c)
      else filter-library-cites citations (i + 1) len acc

Section: Disk Foreword Loading

  DiskLoadState = record {
   dls-seen : List Text,
   dls-chunks : List Text
  }

  disk-extract-cites : Text, Integer -> List CitesDecl
  disk-extract-cites (source) (ceiling) =
   let tok-result = tokenize-into source 1 False ceiling
   in let doc = parse-document (make-parse-state (tok-result.tokens) source) ceiling
   in filter-library-cites (doc.citations) 0 (list-length (doc.citations)) []

  disk-load-cite : Fat16Volume, DiskLoadState, Text, Text, Integer -> [Console] DiskLoadState
  disk-load-cite (vol) (st) (quire) (name) (ceiling) =
   let key = quire & ":" & name
   in if text-set-has (st.dls-seen) key then st
   else let seen2 = text-set-insert (st.dls-seen) key
   in let path = quire-to-dir quire & name & ".codex"
   in when fat16-read-source vol path
     is Just (content) ->
       let deps = disk-extract-cites content ceiling
       in let st2 = disk-load-cites-list vol (DiskLoadState { dls-seen = seen2, dls-chunks = st.dls-chunks }) deps 0 (list-length deps) ceiling
       in let renamed = text-replace content ("Chapter: " & name) ("Chapter: " & quire & "--" & name)
       in DiskLoadState { dls-seen = st2.dls-seen, dls-chunks = list-push (st2.dls-chunks) (renamed & "\n\n") }
     is None ->
       let noted = print-line-uni ("CITE-MISSING " & quire & " " & name)
       in DiskLoadState { dls-seen = seen2, dls-chunks = st.dls-chunks }

  disk-load-cites-list : Fat16Volume, DiskLoadState, List CitesDecl, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] DiskLoadState
  disk-load-cites-list (vol) (st) (cite-list) (i) (len) (ceiling) =
   if i >= len then st
   else let c = list-at cite-list i
   in let st2 = disk-load-cite vol st (token-text (c.quire)) (c.chapter-name) ceiling
   in disk-load-cites-list vol st2 cite-list (i + 1) len ceiling

  disk-resolve-forewords : Fat16Volume, Text, Text -> [Console] Text
  disk-resolve-forewords (vol) (source) (mode) =
   let scan-scale = effective-deck-scale mode (text-length source)
   in let scan-height = scaled-floor scan-scale demand-disk-scan-floor demand-disk-scan-guard-band
   in let scan-base = build scan-height
   in let ceiling = scan-base + scan-height
   in let cite-list = disk-extract-cites source ceiling
   in let st = disk-load-cites-list vol (DiskLoadState { dls-seen = [], dls-chunks = [] }) cite-list 0 (list-length cite-list) ceiling
   in let joined = deck-record (text-concat-list (st.dls-chunks))
   in let compacted = phase-compact
   in joined

Section: Quoted Works

 Threat model: the transport is untrusted. Whoever hands us these bytes
 may hand us anything at all, so the gate takes the messenger's word for
 nothing -- content that does not match its digest, a signer the source
 did not pin, a forged signature, and a score below the floor are each
 refused independently.

 The offered-works wire format, after the marker line: a KEY line per
 trusted key, giving its fingerprint, its public key in hex, and the
 score; and a WORK line per offered definition, giving its digest, its
 author, its timestamp, the fingerprint that signed it, the signature in
 hex, and the number of lines of content that follow.

  quoted-works-marker : Text = "%%QUOTED-WORKS%%"

  SourceSplit = record {
   sp-body : Text,
   sp-works : Text
  }

  match-at : Text, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  match-at (t) (m) (i) (j) (mlen) =
   if j >= mlen then True
   else if char-code-at t (i + j) /= char-code-at m j then False
   else match-at t m i (j + 1) mlen

  index-of-from : Text, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  index-of-from (t) (m) (i) (tlen) (mlen) =
   if i + mlen > tlen then -1
   else if match-at t m i 0 mlen then i
   else index-of-from t m (i + 1) tlen mlen

 The source is CCE by the time it reaches here, and CCE is not ASCII: a
 newline is 1, a space is 2, and 10 is the digit seven. A guard written
 against the ASCII numbers asks whether the character before the marker
 is a seven, decides it is not, and hands the offered works to the parser
 as if they were code. Ask the encoding for its own numbers. Never write
 them down.

  at-line-start : Text, Integer -> Boolean
  at-line-start (t) (i) =
   if i == 0 then True else char-code-at t (i - 1) == char-code '\n'

  at-line-end : Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  at-line-end (t) (i) (tlen) =
   if i >= tlen then True else char-code-at t i == char-code '\n'

  marker-line-at : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  marker-line-at (src) (from) (tlen) (mlen) =
   let at = index-of-from src quoted-works-marker from tlen mlen
   in if at < 0 then -1
      else if at-line-start src at & at-line-end src (at + mlen) tlen then at
      else marker-line-at src (at + 1) tlen mlen

  split-quoted-works : Text -> SourceSplit
  split-quoted-works (src) =
   let tlen = text-length src
   in let mlen = text-length quoted-works-marker
   in let at = marker-line-at src 0 tlen mlen
   in if at < 0
      then SourceSplit { sp-body = src, sp-works = "" }
      else SourceSplit {
        sp-body = substring src 0 at,
        sp-works = substring src (at + mlen) (tlen - at - mlen)
      }

  next-line-at : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  next-line-at (t) (i) (tlen) =
   if i >= tlen then tlen
   else if char-code-at t i == char-code '\n' then i + 1
   else next-line-at t (i + 1) tlen

  line-opens-quotes : Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  line-opens-quotes (t) (i) (tlen) =
   if i >= tlen then False
   else if char-code-at t i == char-code ' ' then line-opens-quotes t (i + 1) tlen
   else if i + 7 > tlen then False
   else if match-at t "quotes" i 0 6 then char-code-at t (i + 6) == char-code ' '
   else False

  scan-for-quotes : Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  scan-for-quotes (t) (i) (tlen) =
   if i >= tlen then False
   else if line-opens-quotes t i tlen then True
   else scan-for-quotes t (next-line-at t i tlen) tlen

  has-quotations : Text -> Boolean
  has-quotations (body) = scan-for-quotes body 0 (text-length body)

Section: Hex

 The digits are contiguous in CCE and the letters are not: CCE orders
 them by frequency, so a is 15 and f is 28, and everything from o to w
 sits between them. A hex decoder written as two range checks over ASCII
 therefore accepts a dozen letters that are not hex digits and mis-values
 every one of them.

  hex-to-bytes : Text, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  hex-to-bytes (t) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i + 1 >= len then acc
   else let hi = hex-digit-value (char-code-at t i)
   in let lo = hex-digit-value (char-code-at t (i + 1))
   in hex-to-bytes t (i + 2) len (list-push acc (hi * 16 + lo))

  decode-hex : Text -> List Integer
  decode-hex (t) = hex-to-bytes t 0 (text-length t) []

Section: Reading The Offered Works

  WorksState = record {
   ws-bundle : FactBundle,
   ws-manifest : TrustManifest
  }

  works-empty : WorksState = WorksState { ws-bundle = bundle-empty, ws-manifest = manifest-empty }

  join-work-lines : List Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  join-work-lines (lines) (i) (stop) (acc) =
   if i >= stop then acc
   else join-work-lines lines (i + 1) stop (acc & list-at lines i & "\n")

 The trust manifest is built from the SOURCE, never from the offered works.
 A KEY line in the works blob is ignored on purpose: it arrives over the
 transport, and the transport is what the gate must not believe. A signer
 the source did not pin is an unknown signer, whatever the works claim.

  build-source-manifest : List CitesDecl, Integer, Integer, TrustManifest -> TrustManifest
  build-source-manifest (ks) (i) (len) (m) =
   if i >= len then m
   else let k = list-at ks i
   in let pub = decode-hex (k.content-hash)
   in let m2 = manifest-add m (k.chapter-name) pub (k.trust-floor)
   in build-source-manifest ks (i + 1) len m2

  parse-works : List Text, Integer, Integer, WorksState -> WorksState
  parse-works (lines) (i) (len) (st) =
   if i >= len then st
   else let ln = list-at lines i
   in if text-starts-with ln "KEY " then parse-works lines (i + 1) len st
      else if text-starts-with ln "WORK " then apply-work-line lines i len st
      else if text-starts-with ln "OFFER " then apply-offer-line lines i len st
      else parse-works lines (i + 1) len st

  apply-offer-line : List Text, Integer, Integer, WorksState -> WorksState
  apply-offer-line (lines) (i) (len) (st) =
   let f = text-split (list-at lines i) " "
   in if list-length f < 5 then parse-works lines (i + 1) len st
   else let nlines = text-to-integer (list-at f 4)
   in let content = join-work-lines lines (i + 1) (i + 1 + nlines) ""
   in let ts = text-to-integer (list-at f 3)
   in let fact = Fact { hash = list-at f 1, kind = DefinitionFact, content = content, author = list-at f 2, timestamp = ts }
   in let st2 = __record-set st "ws-bundle" (bundle-offer (st.ws-bundle) fact)
   in parse-works lines (i + 1 + nlines) len st2

  apply-work-line : List Text, Integer, Integer, WorksState -> WorksState
  apply-work-line (lines) (i) (len) (st) =
   let f = text-split (list-at lines i) " "
   in if list-length f < 7 then parse-works lines (i + 1) len st
   else let nlines = text-to-integer (list-at f 6)
   in let content = join-work-lines lines (i + 1) (i + 1 + nlines) ""
   in let ts = text-to-integer (list-at f 3)
   in let fact = Fact { hash = list-at f 1, kind = DefinitionFact, content = content, author = list-at f 2, timestamp = ts }
   in let sf = SignedFact { fact = fact, signature = decode-hex (list-at f 5), signer-fingerprint = list-at f 4 }
   in let st2 = __record-set st "ws-bundle" (bundle-publish (st.ws-bundle) sf)
   in parse-works lines (i + 1 + nlines) len st2

 No carriage return survives to be stripped here. CCE has no code point
 for one -- 13 is not in the table -- so the conversion at the I/O
 boundary drops it, and a CRLF file and an LF file are the same text by
 the time the gate sees them.

  build-works : Text -> WorksState
  build-works (works) =
   let lines = text-split works "\n"
   in parse-works lines 0 (list-length lines) works-empty

Section: Refusing A Quotation

  quotation-code : ImportOutcome -> Integer
  quotation-code (o) = when o
   is ImportOk (f) -> 0
   is ImportMissing -> cdx-quotation-missing
   is ImportCorrupt -> cdx-quotation-corrupt
   is ImportUnsigned -> cdx-quotation-unsigned
   is ImportUnknownSigner -> cdx-quotation-unknown-signer
   is ImportForged -> cdx-quotation-forged
   is ImportUntrusted (score) -> cdx-quotation-untrusted

  quotation-message : CitesDecl, ImportOutcome -> Text
  quotation-message (q) (o) = when o
   is ImportOk (f) -> ""
   is ImportMissing -> "Quoted work '" & q.chapter-name & "' was not supplied; nothing offered has hash " & q.content-hash
   is ImportCorrupt -> "Quoted work '" & q.chapter-name & "' does not hash to " & q.content-hash
   is ImportUnsigned -> "Quoted work '" & q.chapter-name & "' carries no signature; a hash says what a definition is, only a signature says who stands behind it"
   is ImportUnknownSigner -> "Quoted work '" & q.chapter-name & "' is signed by a key that is not in the trust manifest"
   is ImportForged -> "Quoted work '" & q.chapter-name & "' has a signature that does not verify against its signer's key"
   is ImportUntrusted (score) -> quotation-untrusted-message q score

  quotation-untrusted-message : CitesDecl, Integer -> Text
  quotation-untrusted-message (q) (score) =
   "Quoted work '" & q.chapter-name & "' is signed by a key scoring " & show score & ", below the floor of " & show (q.trust-floor)

  quotation-diag : CitesDecl, ImportOutcome -> Diagnostic
  quotation-diag (q) (o) =
   let sp = span-at (q.quire.line) (q.quire.column) (q.quire.offset) (q.quire.length) (q.quire.file-id)
   in make-error (__narrow (quotation-code o)) (quotation-message q o) sp

  quotation-no-floor-diag : CitesDecl -> Diagnostic
  quotation-no-floor-diag (q) =
   let sp = span-at (q.quire.line) (q.quire.column) (q.quire.offset) (q.quire.length) (q.quire.file-id)
   in make-error cdx-quotation-no-floor ("Chapter '" & q.citing-chapter & "' quotes a work but declares no trust floor; write 'trusting above N' (0 to 10000) to say how much trust a quoted work's signer must clear") sp

  QuoteResolve = record {
   qr-prefix : Text,
   qr-bag : DiagnosticBag
  }

 A quotation names its digest with the algorithm that produced it, so
 that the day we outgrow SHA-256 the old quotations still say what they
 meant. The store is keyed by the digest alone.

  digest-of : Text -> Text
  digest-of (h) =
   if text-starts-with h "sha256:" then substring h 7 (text-length h - 7) else h

  resolve-each : List CitesDecl, Integer, Integer, WorksState, Text, DiagnosticBag -> QuoteResolve
  resolve-each (qs) (i) (len) (ws) (prefix) (bag) =
   if i >= len then QuoteResolve { qr-prefix = prefix, qr-bag = bag }
   else let q = list-at qs i
   in let r = gate-import (ws.ws-bundle) (ws.ws-manifest) (q.trust-floor) (digest-of (q.content-hash)) (q.chapter-name)
   in when r.outcome
    is ImportOk (f) -> resolve-admit qs i len ws prefix bag q (f.content)
    is otherwise -> resolve-each qs (i + 1) len ws prefix (bag-add bag (quotation-diag q (r.outcome)))

  resolve-admit : List CitesDecl, Integer, Integer, WorksState, Text, DiagnosticBag, CitesDecl, Text -> QuoteResolve
  resolve-admit (qs) (i) (len) (ws) (prefix) (bag) (q) (content) =
   let renamed = rewrite-chapter-header content (q.chapter-name)
   in resolve-each qs (i + 1) len ws (prefix & renamed & "\n\n") bag

  line-end-at : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  line-end-at (t) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then len
   else if char-code-at t i == char-code '\n' then i
   else line-end-at t (i + 1) len

  rewrite-chapter-header : Text, Text -> Text
  rewrite-chapter-header (content) (local-name) =
   let len = text-length content
   in let nl = line-end-at content 0 len
   in let head = substring content 0 nl
   in if text-starts-with head "Chapter:"
      then "Chapter: " & local-name & substring content nl (len - nl)
      else content

  resolve-quotations : Text, FactBundle -> QuoteResolve
  resolve-quotations (body) (bundle) =
   let tok-result = tokenize body 1
   in let doc = parse-document (make-parse-state (tok-result.tokens) body) 0
   in let qs = doc.quotations
   in if list-length qs == 0 then QuoteResolve { qr-prefix = "", qr-bag = empty-bag }
   else let q0 = list-at qs 0
   in if q0.trust-floor < 0
      then QuoteResolve { qr-prefix = "", qr-bag = bag-add empty-bag (quotation-no-floor-diag q0) }
   else let ks = doc.trust-keys
   in let manifest = build-source-manifest ks 0 (list-length ks) manifest-empty
   in let ws = WorksState { ws-bundle = bundle, ws-manifest = manifest }
   in resolve-each qs 0 (list-length qs) ws "" empty-bag

Section: Text Compilation

  compile-text : CompileFrontend, CompileTarget -> Text
  compile-text (fe) (target) =
   if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then ""
   else emit-for-text-target target (fe.ir) (fe.text-meta) (fe.type-defs) (fe.renames)

  emit-for-text-target : CompileTarget, IRChapter, IRTextMeta, List ATypeDef, List RenameEntry -> Text
  emit-for-text-target (target) (ir) (meta) (type-defs) (renames) =
   when target
    is CtCodexText -> codex-emit-text-chapter ir meta type-defs renames
    is CtX86_64Bare | CtCdx -> ""

Section: Binary Compilation

  compile-to-cdx : CompileFrontend -> EmitChapterResult
  compile-to-cdx (fe) = compile-to-cdx-with-exit-mode fe Exit False False WatchdogProgress 0

  cdx-emit-roots : List Text
  cdx-emit-roots = ["opening", "fat16-servicer-read", "fat16-servicer-write"]

 The IR handed to a native plug needs the same treatment for the same
 reason, one lane over. A plug's block builtin is emitted machine code
 that calls the Codex virtio driver (`Arm64Runtime.codex`, block device
 helpers), and the compiler cannot see that caller, so pruning from
 `opening` alone deletes the driver and the plug's branch resolves to
 nothing. These roots are the driver's entry points, and they are exactly
 as much a root as `fat16-servicer-read` is: reached from an emitter, not
 from Codex.

 Ruled 2026-08-16 (red): reachability is derived from the program's own
 declaration and a disk program never cites a driver chapter to make its
 disk exist. That would be a guarantee held by whoever remembers the
 incantation (L-FREEDOM), which fails silently for the next reader.

 **A new driver entry point must be added here.** Nothing checks that, and
 the symptom is an unresolved-call warning naming the missing function
 rather than anything at this line.

  ir-emit-roots : List Text
  ir-emit-roots = ["opening", "vb-capacity-auto", "vb-read-auto", "vb-write-auto", "fat16-servicer-read", "fat16-servicer-write"]

  compile-to-cdx-with-exit-mode : CompileFrontend, X86_64ExitMode, Boolean, Boolean, X86_64WatchdogMode, Integer between 0 and 2 -> EmitChapterResult
  compile-to-cdx-with-exit-mode (fe) (exit-mode) (poison) (trace) (wd-mode) (hosted-target) =
   if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then EmitChapterResult { header-bytes = [], content-buf = 0, content-len = 0, tail-bytes = [], bag = fe.bag, symbol-map = [] }
   else let all-defs = (fe.cdx-ir).defs
    in let proofs = keep-proof-defs [] all-defs 0
    in let pruned = ir-prune-unreachable-roots (fe.cdx-ir) cdx-emit-roots
    in let dropped = ir-dropped-budgeted-punctuals all-defs (pruned.defs)
    in let emit-result = x86-64-emit-cdx-with-exit-mode (ir-chapter-with-proofs pruned proofs) (fe.sorted-type-defs) exit-mode poison trace wd-mode dropped hosted-target
    in EmitChapterResult {
     header-bytes = emit-result.header-bytes,
     content-buf = emit-result.content-buf,
     content-len = emit-result.content-len,
     tail-bytes = emit-result.tail-bytes,
     bag = bag-merge-all [fe.bag, emit-result.bag],
     symbol-map = emit-result.symbol-map
    }

Section: Measurement

  phase-labels : List Text
  phase-labels =
   ["h0-start", "h1-tokenize", "h2-scan", "h3-assignments",
    "h4-parse", "h5-desugar", "h6-scope", "h7-resolve",
    "h8-check", "h9-lower", "h10-ctors",
    "h-pre-emit", "h-post-emit"]

  phase-metric-names : List Text
  phase-metric-names = ["lex", "parse", "desugar", "scope", "check", "lower", "resolve", "lift"]

  format-phase-metrics-wd : List PhaseMetrics -> Text
  format-phase-metrics-wd (metrics) =
   format-phase-metrics-loop metrics phase-metric-names 0 (list-length metrics) ""

  format-phase-metrics-loop : List PhaseMetrics, List Text, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  format-phase-metrics-loop (metrics) (names) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let m = list-at metrics i
   in let name = if i < list-length names then list-at names i else "?"
   in let deck-usage = m.deck-end - m.deck-origin
   in let bivy-usage = m.bivy-hwm - m.bivy-origin
   in let line = "WD:PM-" & name & ":deck-origin=" & integer-to-text (m.deck-origin) & ",deck-end=" & integer-to-text (m.deck-end) & ",deck-usage=" & integer-to-text deck-usage & ",bivy-hwm=" & integer-to-text (m.bivy-hwm) & ",bivy-usage=" & integer-to-text bivy-usage & "\n"
   in format-phase-metrics-loop metrics names (i + 1) len (acc & line)

  format-heap-marks : List Integer -> Text
  format-heap-marks (marks) =
   text-concat-list (format-heap-marks-prefix marks phase-labels "PHASE-" 0 (list-length marks) [])

  format-heap-marks-wd : List Integer -> Text
  format-heap-marks-wd (marks) =
   text-concat-list (format-heap-marks-prefix marks phase-labels "WD:PHASE-" 0 (list-length marks) [])

  emit-mark-labels : List Text
  emit-mark-labels = ["h-pre-emit", "h-post-emit"]

  format-emit-marks : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  format-emit-marks (pre) (post) (pfx) =
   text-concat-list (format-heap-marks-prefix [pre, post] emit-mark-labels pfx 0 2 [])

  format-heap-marks-prefix : List Integer, List Text, Text, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  format-heap-marks-prefix (marks) (labels) (pfx) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let label = if i < list-length labels then list-at labels i else "h?"
    in format-heap-marks-prefix marks labels pfx (i + 1) len (list-push acc (pfx & label & ":" & integer-to-text (list-at marks i) & "\n"))

Section: Expr-Type Pre-Resolution

  resolve-all-expr-types : UnificationState, List ExprTypeEntry, Integer, Integer, List ExprTypeEntry -> List ExprTypeEntry
  resolve-all-expr-types (st) (entries) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let e = list-at entries i
   in let resolved = deck-record (ExprTypeEntry { key = e.key, ty = deep-resolve st (e.ty) })
   in resolve-all-expr-types st entries (i + 1) len (list-push acc resolved)

  resolve-all-bindings : UnificationState, List TypeBinding, Integer, Integer, List TypeBinding -> List TypeBinding
  resolve-all-bindings (st) (bindings) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else let b = list-at bindings i
   in let resolved = deck-record (TypeBinding { name = b.name, bound-type = deep-resolve st (b.bound-type) })
   in resolve-all-bindings st bindings (i + 1) len (list-push acc resolved)

  tail-resolve-bindings : UnificationState, List TypeBinding, Integer, Integer, List TypeBinding, MemoCtx, Integer, Integer, Integer -> (List TypeBinding, Integer)
  tail-resolve-bindings (st) (bs) (i) (len) (acc) (mc) (scratch-base) (bivy-base) (kc) =
   if i >= len then let set-k = __deck-set kc in (acc, kc)
   else let set-s = __deck-set scratch-base
   in let chunk = deck-record (__list-with-capacity (len - i))
   in let n = tail-resolve-binding-chunk st bs i len chunk scratch-base bivy-base 0
   in let set-k = __deck-set kc
   in let acc2 = tail-copy-binding-chunk chunk 0 n acc mc
   in let kc2 = __deck-pos
   in let z = __memset (mc.mc-table) 0 (mc.mc-slots * 8)
   in let r = __heap-restore bivy-base
   in tail-resolve-bindings st bs (i + n) len acc2 mc scratch-base bivy-base kc2

  tail-resolve-binding-chunk : UnificationState, List TypeBinding, Integer, Integer, List TypeBinding, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tail-resolve-binding-chunk (st) (bs) (i) (len) (chunk) (scratch-base) (bivy-base) (n) =
   if i >= len then n
   else if n > 0 & (__deck-pos - scratch-base) + (__heap-save - bivy-base) >= demand-check-batch-budget then n
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let resolved = deck-record (TypeBinding { name = b.name, bound-type = deep-resolve st (b.bound-type) })
   in let pushed = list-push chunk resolved
   in tail-resolve-binding-chunk st bs (i + 1) len chunk scratch-base bivy-base (n + 1)

  tail-copy-binding-chunk : List TypeBinding, Integer, Integer, List TypeBinding, MemoCtx -> List TypeBinding
  tail-copy-binding-chunk (chunk) (j) (n) (acc) (mc) =
   if j >= n then acc
   else let b = list-at chunk j
   in let kept = deck-record (TypeBinding { name = mcopy-text (b.name) mc, bound-type = mcopy-type (b.bound-type) 0 mc })
   in tail-copy-binding-chunk chunk (j + 1) n (list-push acc kept) mc

  tail-resolve-expr-types : UnificationState, List ExprTypeEntry, Integer, Integer, List ExprTypeEntry, MemoCtx, Integer, Integer, Integer -> (List ExprTypeEntry, Integer)
  tail-resolve-expr-types (st) (es) (i) (len) (acc) (mc) (scratch-base) (bivy-base) (kc) =
   if i >= len then let set-k = __deck-set kc in (acc, kc)
   else let set-s = __deck-set scratch-base
   in let chunk = deck-record (__list-with-capacity (len - i))
   in let n = tail-resolve-expr-type-chunk st es i len chunk scratch-base bivy-base 0
   in let set-k = __deck-set kc
   in let acc2 = tail-copy-expr-type-chunk chunk 0 n acc mc
   in let kc2 = __deck-pos
   in let z = __memset (mc.mc-table) 0 (mc.mc-slots * 8)
   in let r = __heap-restore bivy-base
   in tail-resolve-expr-types st es (i + n) len acc2 mc scratch-base bivy-base kc2

  tail-resolve-expr-type-chunk : UnificationState, List ExprTypeEntry, Integer, Integer, List ExprTypeEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tail-resolve-expr-type-chunk (st) (es) (i) (len) (chunk) (scratch-base) (bivy-base) (n) =
   if i >= len then n
   else if n > 0 & (__deck-pos - scratch-base) + (__heap-save - bivy-base) >= demand-check-batch-budget then n
   else let e = list-at es i
   in let resolved = deck-record (ExprTypeEntry { key = e.key, ty = deep-resolve st (e.ty) })
   in let pushed = list-push chunk resolved
   in tail-resolve-expr-type-chunk st es (i + 1) len chunk scratch-base bivy-base (n + 1)

  tail-copy-expr-type-chunk : List ExprTypeEntry, Integer, Integer, List ExprTypeEntry, MemoCtx -> List ExprTypeEntry
  tail-copy-expr-type-chunk (chunk) (j) (n) (acc) (mc) =
   if j >= n then acc
   else let e = list-at chunk j
   in let kept = deck-record (ExprTypeEntry { key = e.key, ty = mcopy-type (e.ty) 0 mc })
   in tail-copy-expr-type-chunk chunk (j + 1) n (list-push acc kept) mc

Section: Codegen Diagnostics

  print-codegen-errors : List Diagnostic, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  print-codegen-errors (errs) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then print-line-uni ""
   else act
    print-line-uni (diagnostic-display (list-at errs i) empty-file-table)
    print-codegen-errors errs (i + 1) len
   end

  print-codegen-error-header : List Diagnostic -> [Console] Nothing
  print-codegen-error-header (errs) =
   let n = list-length errs
   in if n == 0 then print-line-uni ""
   else act
    print-line-uni ("CODEGEN-ERRORS:" & integer-to-text n)
    print-codegen-errors errs 0 n
   end

  print-bag-notices : DiagnosticBag -> [Console] Nothing
  print-bag-notices (bag) =
   print-notices-loop (bag.diagnostics) 0 (list-length (bag.diagnostics))

  print-notices-loop : List Diagnostic, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  print-notices-loop (ds) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then print-line-uni ""
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if d.severity > sev-error then act
     print-line-uni (diagnostic-display d empty-file-table)
     print-notices-loop ds (i + 1) len
    end
    else print-notices-loop ds (i + 1) len

Section: Streaming Emission

  emit-streaming-ir-defs : List IRDef, List Text, List RenameEntry, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-streaming-ir-defs (ir-defs) (ctor-names) (renames) (i) (len) =
   if i == len then print-line-uni ""
   else let h = __heap-save
   in let text = codex-emit-def (list-at ir-defs i) ctor-names renames
   in act
    print-line-uni text
    let restored = __heap-restore h
    in emit-streaming-ir-defs ir-defs ctor-names renames (i + 1) len
   end

  emit-text-streaming : Text, CompileFlags -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-text-streaming (source) (flags) =
   let fe = compile-frontend source "Program" flags
   in let emit-deck = emit-build (list-length (fe.ir.defs) * 65536 + 8388608)
   in if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then act
    print-codegen-error-header (bag-errors (fe.bag))
    print-line-uni (format-heap-marks-wd (fe.heap-marks))
    print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED: errors in bag; no text emitted"
   end
   else let wd-marks = format-heap-marks-wd (fe.heap-marks) & format-phase-metrics-wd (fe.phase-metrics)
   in let ir-defs = fe.ir.defs
   in let len = list-length ir-defs
   in if len == 0
      then let header = if fe.text-meta.chapter-title == "" then ""
           else "Chapter: " & fe.text-meta.chapter-title & "\n"
             & codex-emit-grounds-line (fe.text-meta.ground-effects) & "\n"
             & if fe.text-meta.prose == "" then "" else " " & fe.text-meta.prose & "\n\n"
      in let type-defs-text = codex-emit-type-defs (fe.type-defs) 0
      in act
       print-line-uni wd-marks
       print-line-uni (header & type-defs-text)
      end
   else let h-emit = __heap-save
   in let header = if fe.text-meta.chapter-title == "" then ""
      else "Chapter: " & fe.text-meta.chapter-title & "\n"
        & codex-emit-grounds-line (fe.text-meta.ground-effects) & "\n"
        & if fe.text-meta.prose == "" then "" else " " & fe.text-meta.prose & "\n\n"
   in let type-defs-text = codex-emit-type-defs (fe.type-defs) 0
   in let first-text = codex-emit-def (list-at ir-defs 0) (fe.text-meta.ctor-names) (fe.renames)
   in act
    print-line-uni wd-marks
    print-line-uni (header & type-defs-text & first-text)
    let restored = __heap-restore h-emit
    in emit-streaming-ir-defs ir-defs (fe.text-meta.ctor-names) (fe.renames) 1 len
   end

Section: Entry Point

  emit-cdx : Text, Text, CompileFlags, Boolean -> [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-cdx (source) (mode) (flags) (force-disk) =
   let exit-mode = if has-mode-flag mode "repl" then ExitRepl
    else if has-mode-flag mode "uefi" then ExitUefi
    else Exit
   in let want-map = has-mode-flag mode "map"
   in let want-disk = if force-disk then True else has-mode-flag mode "diskout"
   in let poison = has-mode-flag mode "poison"
   in let trace = has-mode-flag mode "trace"
   in let debug = has-mode-flag mode "debug"
   in let prof = has-mode-flag mode "profile"
   in let wd-mode = if has-mode-flag mode "pet" then WatchdogPet else WatchdogProgress
   in let hosted-target = if has-mode-flag mode "hosted-windows" then 2 else if has-mode-flag mode "hosted" then 1 else 0
   in act
    if debug then print-line-uni ("DBG:frontend src=" & show (text-length source))
    else print-line-uni ""
    if prof then print-line-uni ("PROF-BEGIN: " & show (prof-start 0))
    else print-line-uni ""
    let fe = compile-frontend-cdx source "Codex_Codex" flags
    in act
     if debug then act
      print-line-uni ("DBG:emit defs=" & show (list-length (fe.cdx-ir.defs)))
      dbg-print-def-names (fe.cdx-ir.defs) 0 (list-length (fe.cdx-ir.defs))
     end
     else print-line-uni ""
     let result = compile-to-cdx-with-exit-mode fe exit-mode poison trace wd-mode hosted-target
     in act
      if prof then print-line-uni ("PROF-END: " & show (prof-dump 0))
      else print-line-uni ""
      print-codegen-error-header (bag-errors (result.bag))
      print-bag-notices (result.bag)
      if want-map then emit-binary-tail-with-map result want-disk
      else emit-binary-tail result want-disk
     end
    end
   end

  emit-binary-size : EmitChapterResult -> Integer
  emit-binary-size (result) =
   list-length (result.header-bytes) + result.content-len + list-length (result.tail-bytes)

  disk-halted : Boolean -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-halted (to-disk) = act
   print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED: errors in bag; no binary emitted"
   disk-stop to-disk bp-orange
  end

  emit-binary-tail : EmitChapterResult, Boolean -> [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-binary-tail (result) (to-disk) =
   if bag-has-errors (result.bag) then disk-halted to-disk
   else act
    print-line-uni ("SIZE:" & integer-to-text (emit-binary-size result))
    if to-disk then emit-binary-to-disk result
    else act
     write-binary (result.header-bytes)
     write-binary-buf (result.content-buf) 0 (result.content-len)
     write-binary (result.tail-bytes)
    end
   end

 write-binary and write-binary-buf reach a board through neither of their two
 arms: the fast one probes for codex-vm on port 0x511 and the fallback writes
 COM1 a byte at a time, and a UEFI machine with no UART answers neither. So a
 metal compile puts the CDX on the boot volume instead, and the verdict beside
 it, because print-line-uni is that same serial line.

 DISK mode takes this arm unconditionally, and emit-from-disk says so with an
 argument rather than emit-cdx re-deriving it from the mode line. Measured
 2026-08-09 with a probe at the head of emit-cdx: on that path `mode` is not a
 damaged string but not a string at all, arriving as twelve bytes of unrelated
 memory, so every has-mode-flag answers False and `parse-mode-cmd mode` does
 not equal "DISK" either. Deriving the sink from a value in that state is what
 made an earlier attempt dead code.

 The segments go down uncopied. The content is already a buffer, and routing it
 through a List would cost eight bytes of arena per byte of file.

  emit-binary-to-disk : EmitChapterResult -> [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-binary-to-disk (result) = act
   disk-rung "compiled" 1 bp-blue bp-orange
   ok <- fat16-write-segments "OUT.CDX" (result.header-bytes) (result.content-buf) (result.content-len) (result.tail-bytes)
   emit-disk-verdict ok (emit-binary-size result)
  end

 The verdict re-reads the directory instead of taking the writer's word.
 Measured 2026-08-11: with the medium read-only, codex-vm printed
 `WARN: cannot reopen disk ... for write` on every sector and
 fat16-write-segments still answered True, so a rung that trusted `ok` was a
 rung that could not fail.

  disk-entry-size : Maybe Fat16DirEntry -> Integer
  disk-entry-size (m) = when m
    is Just (e) -> e.de-size
    is None -> 0 - 1

  disk-landed : Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  disk-landed (ok) (got) (want) =
    if got /= want then 0
    else if ok then 1
    else 0

  emit-disk-verdict : Boolean, Integer -> [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-disk-verdict (ok) (size) = act
   vol <- fat16-boot-volume
   entry <- fat16-resolve-path vol "OUT.CDX"
   emit-disk-say (disk-landed ok (disk-entry-size entry) size) size (disk-entry-size entry)
  end

 The footer: verdict, total wall seconds, then the phase table the pipeline
 banked through bp-log-phase. A row's first qword is eight packed label
 bytes; unpacking stops at the first zero byte.

  disk-unpack8 : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  disk-unpack8 (v) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= 8 then acc
   else let b = int-mod (bit-shru v (i * 8)) 256
   in if b == 0 then acc
   else disk-unpack8 v (i + 1) (acc & char-encode (code-to-char (from-unicode b)))

  disk-print-phases : Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-print-phases (i) (n) =
   if i >= n then print-text ""
   else act
    print-line-uni ("PHASE " & disk-unpack8 (peek-qword (bp-log-rows + i * 24) 0) 0 "" & " " & integer-to-text (peek-qword (bp-log-rows + i * 24) 8) & "s heap " & integer-to-text (peek-qword (bp-log-rows + i * 24) 16) & "MB")
    disk-print-phases (i + 1) n
   end

  emit-disk-say : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-disk-say (landed) (size) (got) = act
   disk-mark "wrote" landed bp-white bp-blue
   let line = if landed == 1 then "OK OUT.CDX " & integer-to-text size else "FAILED " & integer-to-text got & " of " & integer-to-text size
   in act
    v <- fat16-write-file "OUT.TXT" line
    print-line-uni ("DISK-OUT: " & line)
    t <- disk-build-footer landed
    disk-stop True (if landed == 1 then bp-white else bp-blue)
   end
  end

  disk-build-footer : Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-build-footer (landed) =
   if bp-armed 0 == False then print-text ""
   else act
    p <- disk-print-phases 0 (peek-qword bp-log-count-cell 0)
    print-line-uni ("BUILD " & (if landed == 1 then "YAY" else "NAY") & " " & bp-hms-text (bp-rtc-seconds 0) & " total " & integer-to-text (phase-took (peek-qword bp-log-start-cell 0) (bp-rtc-seconds 0)) & "s")
   end

  emit-binary-tail-with-map : EmitChapterResult, Boolean -> [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-binary-tail-with-map (result) (to-disk) =
   if bag-has-errors (result.bag) then disk-halted to-disk
   else act
    print-line-uni ("SIZE:" & integer-to-text (emit-binary-size result))
    if to-disk then emit-binary-to-disk result
    else act
     write-binary (result.header-bytes)
     write-binary-buf (result.content-buf) 0 (result.content-len)
     write-binary (result.tail-bytes)
    end
    emit-symbol-map (result.symbol-map) 0 (list-length (result.symbol-map))
   end

  emit-symbol-map : List Text, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-symbol-map (lines) (i) (len) =
   if len == 0 then print-line-uni "MAP:0"
   else act
    print-line-uni ("MAP:" & show len)
    emit-map-lines lines i len
   end

  emit-map-lines : List Text, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-map-lines (lines) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then print-line-uni "MAP-END"
   else act
    print-line-uni (list-at lines i)
    emit-map-lines lines (i + 1) len
   end

  emit-text : CompileTarget, Text, CompileFlags -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-text (target) (source) (flags) =
   let fe = compile-frontend source "Program" flags
   in let h-pre-emit = __heap-save
   in let text = compile-text fe target
   in let h-post-emit = __heap-save
   in act
    print-codegen-error-header (bag-errors (fe.bag))
    if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED: errors in bag; no text emitted"
    else print-line-uni text
    print-line-uni ((format-heap-marks-wd (fe.heap-marks) & format-emit-marks h-pre-emit h-post-emit "WD:PHASE-"))
   end

 Both IR front doors emit the SAME IR, and this is the function that makes that
 true. From main 19558 until 20144 emit-ir-cce lifted and emit-ir-uni did not,
 so `-IrUni` and `-IrCce` carried different IR for the same program while
 `-IrUni` was the only readable one. COMPILER-29's cause was recorded wrong
 twice off an `-IrUni` dump for exactly that reason. Both call this now, so a
 future divergence has to be written down deliberately.

 `no-lift` is the deliberate exception and it has one caller, build/jonquil.ps1,
 which needs the SOURCE-shaped defs: lifting relocates a lambda body into
 __lam_N, and jonquil compares a def's string data against its OWN header, so a
 self-reproducing construction hidden inside a lambda would move out from under
 the comparison. Suppressing the lift there preserves exactly the arm that
 shipped before this change.

  lift-ir-for-emit : IRChapter, CompileFlags -> IRChapter
  lift-ir-for-emit (ir) (flags) =
   if flags.no-lift then ir
   else let lift-deck-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-lift-floor demand-lift-guard-band
   in let lift-base = build lift-deck-height
   in let lift-ceiling = lift-base + lift-deck-height
   in deck-record (lift-lambdas ir lift-ceiling)

  emit-ir-uni : Text, CompileFlags -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-ir-uni (source) (flags) =
   let fe = compile-frontend-ir source "Program" flags
   in let h-pre-emit = __heap-save
   in act
    print-codegen-error-header (bag-errors (fe.bag))
    print-bag-notices (fe.bag)
    if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED: errors in bag; no IR emitted"
    else let lifted-ir = lift-ir-for-emit (fe.ir) flags
     in act
     print-line-uni "IR-BEGIN"
     print-line-uni (emit-ir-chapter (ir-prune-unreachable-roots lifted-ir ir-emit-roots) (fe.text-meta) (fe.type-defs))
     print-line-uni "IR-END"
    end
    let h-post-emit = __heap-save
    in print-line-uni ((format-heap-marks-wd (fe.heap-marks) & format-emit-marks h-pre-emit h-post-emit "WD:PHASE-"))
   end

 The SIZE-framed wire format the emit plugs consume: the SIZE line must
 precede the first payload byte, and the closing print-line supplies the
 one raw linefeed that follows the payload. Only emission knows a def's
 length, hence two passes -- ir-defs-wire-size for the total, then
 ir-print-defs through print-text so the wire bytes are exactly what
 emit-ir-chapter produced.

  emit-ir-cce : Text, CompileFlags -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-ir-cce (source) (flags) =
   let fe = compile-frontend-ir source "Program" flags
   in let h-pre-emit = __heap-save
   in act
    if flags.wire-trace then print-line-uni ("FE-DONE frontier " & integer-to-text h-pre-emit) else print-text ""
    print-codegen-error-header (bag-errors (fe.bag))
    if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED: errors in bag; no IR emitted"
    else let lifted-ir = lift-ir-for-emit (fe.ir) flags
     in let raw-defs = lifted-ir.defs
     in act
      if flags.wire-trace then ir-trace-dce-cost raw-defs 0 (list-length raw-defs) else print-text ""
      let ir = ir-prune-unreachable-roots lifted-ir ir-emit-roots
      in let defs-len = list-length (ir.defs)
      in act
       if flags.wire-trace then ir-trace-def-sizes (ir.defs) 0 defs-len else print-text ""
       let prefix = emit-ir-chapter-prefix ir (fe.text-meta) (fe.type-defs)
       in let total = text-length prefix + ir-defs-wire-size (ir.defs) 0 defs-len 0 + 3
       in act
        print-line-uni ("SIZE:" & show total)
        print-text prefix
        ir-print-defs (ir.defs) 0 defs-len
        print-line ""
       end
      end
     end
    let h-post-emit = __heap-save
    in print-line-uni ((format-heap-marks-wd (fe.heap-marks) & format-emit-marks h-pre-emit h-post-emit "WD:PHASE-"))
   end

  emit-measure : Text, CompileFlags -> [Console] Nothing
  emit-measure (source) (flags) =
   let fe = compile-frontend source "Program" flags
   in let h-pre-emit = __heap-save
   in let text = compile-text fe CtCodexText
   in let h-post-emit = __heap-save
   in print-line-uni ((format-heap-marks (fe.heap-marks) & format-emit-marks h-pre-emit h-post-emit "PHASE-") & format-phase-decks (fe.phase-metrics) 0 (list-length (fe.phase-metrics)) "" & "EMIT-BYTES:" & integer-to-text (text-length text) & "\n")

  format-phase-decks : List PhaseMetrics, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  format-phase-decks (ms) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i == len then acc
   else let m = list-at ms i
   in format-phase-decks ms (i + 1) len (acc & "DECK-" & integer-to-text i & ":phase=" & (m.label) & " origin=" & integer-to-text (m.deck-origin) & " end=" & integer-to-text (m.deck-end) & " used=" & integer-to-text (m.deck-end - m.deck-origin) & " bivy-origin=" & integer-to-text (m.bivy-origin) & " bivy-hwm=" & integer-to-text (m.bivy-hwm) & " bivy-used=" & integer-to-text (m.bivy-hwm - m.bivy-origin) & "\n")

  parse-mode-cmd : Text -> Text
  parse-mode-cmd (mode) =
   let parts = text-split mode " "
   in if list-length parts == 0 then mode
      else list-at parts 0

  mode-flag-value : Text, Text, Integer -> Integer
  mode-flag-value (mode) (prefix) (dflt) =
   let parts = text-split mode " "
   in mode-value-loop parts prefix (text-length prefix) 1 (list-length parts) dflt

  mode-value-loop : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mode-value-loop (parts) (prefix) (plen) (i) (len) (dflt) =
   if i >= len then dflt
   else let p = list-at parts i
   in if text-starts-with p prefix
      then text-to-integer (substring p plen (text-length p - plen))
      else mode-value-loop parts prefix plen (i + 1) len dflt

  mode-flag-text : Text, Text -> Text
  mode-flag-text (mode) (prefix) =
   let parts = text-split mode " "
   in mode-text-loop parts prefix (text-length prefix) 1 (list-length parts)

  mode-text-loop : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  mode-text-loop (parts) (prefix) (plen) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then ""
   else let p = list-at parts i
   in if text-starts-with p prefix
      then substring p plen (text-length p - plen)
      else mode-text-loop parts prefix plen (i + 1) len

  has-mode-flag : Text, Text -> Boolean
  has-mode-flag (mode) (flag) =
   let parts = text-split mode " "
   in has-flag-loop parts flag 1 (list-length parts)

  has-flag-loop : List Text, Text, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  has-flag-loop (parts) (flag) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then False
   else if list-at parts i == flag then True
   else has-flag-loop parts flag (i + 1) len

  dbg-print-def-names : List IRDef, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  dbg-print-def-names (defs) (i) (len) = act
   if i >= len then print-line-uni ""
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in act
    print-line-uni ("DBG:def " & show i & " " & d.name)
    dbg-print-def-names defs (i + 1) len
   end
  end

  parse-mode-flags : Text, Integer -> CompileFlags
  parse-mode-flags (mode) (unit-len) =
   CompileFlags { prose = has-mode-flag mode "prose", escape-check = has-mode-flag mode "escape-check", poison-compact = has-mode-flag mode "poison-compact", deck-scale = effective-deck-scale mode unit-len, phase-trace = has-mode-flag mode "debug", wire-trace = has-mode-flag mode "wsz", cost-report = has-mode-flag mode "cost-report", no-lift = has-mode-flag mode "no-lift", pipeline = pipeline-of mode }

 The volume is resolved from the disk's own GPT rather than assumed to
 start at LBA 2048. That constant is only right for an image our own
 GptWriter laid down; a stick partitioned elsewhere puts the volume at a
 different offset. 2048 remains the fallback when there is no readable GPT.

  emit-from-disk : Text -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  emit-from-disk (mode) = act
   disk-rung "mode" 1 bp-yellow bp-cyan
   path-result <- read-line
   vol <- disk-locate-volume
   disk-rung "volume" (bp-truth (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector == 512)) bp-magenta bp-yellow
   d <- disk-diag path-result
   m <- disk-diag-mem vol
   v <- disk-diag-vol vol
   when disk-source vol disk-default-path
     is Just (source) ->
       let o = bp-pass 1 bp-orange bp-magenta
       in let foreword-prefix = disk-resolve-forewords vol source mode
       in let full = foreword-prefix & source
       in let flags = parse-mode-flags mode (text-length full)
       in emit-cdx full mode flags True
     is None -> disk-no-source ("DISK: file not found: " & disk-default-path)
  end

 The volume rung reads the BPB rather than the mount's own answer.
 fat16-boot-volume returns a record whatever the medium said, so a payload
 built without UEFI mode reads down the bare-metal path, gets zeroes, and
 mounts a volume that satisfies every pointer test.

 The source rung paints on the way through and does not trace, because
 `mode` is intact in this frame and damaged one call deeper. The next rung's
 line names it: nothing reaches `compiled` without ORANGE having been painted.

 On this box every sector of the source is a separate firmware Block I/O call
 over USB, and a 2.78 MB source is 5,429 of them. The bed's disk is an
 in-memory image, so the same read costs nothing there and the difference does
 not appear until a stick is in a machine.

 The two phases are counted separately because they fail differently. The
 chain walk is I/O and is slow only on metal; the conversion is arithmetic and
 costs the same in both. Which of them a stall lands in is what no flight has
 been able to say.

 The counter starts AT the threshold so the first bar is drawn before the
 first sector is asked for. A heartbeat whose first beat is minutes away
 answers the question too late to be worth having.

  disk-read-report-every : Integer
  disk-read-report-every = 64

  disk-convert-chunk : Integer
  disk-convert-chunk = 65536

  disk-sector-total : Fat16Volume, Integer -> Integer
  disk-sector-total (vol) (size) =
   (size + (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) - 1) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)

  disk-read-tick : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  disk-read-tick (since) (n) (total) =
   if since < disk-read-report-every then 0
   else bp-progress n total 0 bp-white

  disk-read-chain : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] List Integer
  disk-read-chain (vol) (cluster) (remaining) (sector-off) (acc) (n) (since) (total) =
   if remaining <= 0 then acc
   else if fat16-is-end cluster then acc
   else act
    t <- disk-read-tick since n total
    buf <- block-read-sector (fat16-cluster-sector vol cluster + sector-off)
    disk-read-step vol cluster remaining sector-off acc buf n (if since < disk-read-report-every then since + 1 else 0) total
   end

  disk-read-step : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] List Integer
  disk-read-step (vol) (cluster) (remaining) (sector-off) (acc) (buf) (n) (since) (total) =
   let bytes-this = if remaining < (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) then remaining else (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)
   in let acc2 = fat16-copy-bytes buf 0 bytes-this acc
   in let next-sector-off = sector-off + 1
   in if next-sector-off >= (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster)
     then disk-read-chain vol (fat16-next-cluster vol cluster) (remaining - bytes-this) 0 acc2 (n + 1) since total
     else disk-read-chain vol cluster (remaining - bytes-this) next-sector-off acc2 (n + 1) since total

 The chunking exists for the bar and for nothing else: fat16-source-chars
 threads its accumulator, so converting in pieces pushes onto the same list
 the single call would have built and the text is identical either way.

  disk-convert : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> Text
  disk-convert (tbl) (bs) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then text-concat-list acc
   else let p = bp-progress i len bp-progress-height bp-cyan
   in let hi = if i + disk-convert-chunk > len then len else i + disk-convert-chunk
   in disk-convert tbl bs hi len (fat16-source-chars tbl bs i hi acc)

 The board's state, printed as integers only. Every previous build died before
 its first line here, and each of them built that line out of the path Text --
 so nothing below touches the path as text until its length has been shown to
 be sane. The addresses are proc 0's filesystem and network scope
 (proc-table-base + proc-scope-offset / + proc-net-scope-offset) and the serial
 ring's write and read positions (serial-write-pos-addr, serial-read-pos-addr),
 all in X86_64Boot.codex.

  disk-diag-stamp : Integer
  disk-diag-stamp = 20260815

  disk-diag : Maybe Text -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-diag (pr) = act
   print-line-uni ("DIAG stamp " & integer-to-text disk-diag-stamp)
   print-line-uni ("DIAG scope " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 20544 0) & " net " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 20504 0))
   print-line-uni ("DIAG ring w " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 28704 0) & " r " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 28712 0))
   disk-diag-path pr
  end

 Where things LIVE, and whether painting moves them. The 2026-08-13 flight of
 a5fix.img read every field of the volume record as 71777214294589695, which
 is 00FF00FF00FF00FF -- two MAGENTA pixels -- after the volume rung's magenta
 fill, on a board where the same binary under OVMF reads them correctly. So
 either the record's memory is being painted over, or the record pointer has
 come to point into painted memory, and the addresses decide which: the
 record's own address against the handoff's base + h*stride*4 extent, read
 raw off the block the stub published. The stomp pair reads one field, paints
 the full field magenta again, and reads it back: a value that MOVES convicts
 the paint live, with every address needed for the arithmetic already on the
 glass.

  disk-diag-mem : Fat16Volume -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-diag-mem (vol) = act
   print-line-uni ("DIAG mem vol " & integer-to-text (address-of vol) & " heap " & integer-to-text __heap-save)
   print-line-uni ("DIAG hand base " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 126976 16) & " wh " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 126976 24) & " stride " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 126976 32))
   print-line-uni ("DIAG stomp pre " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector))
   p <- bp-paint bp-magenta
   print-line-uni ("DIAG stomp post " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector))
  end

 Which volume was actually mounted. fat16-boot-volume takes the first usable
 GPT partition and the ladder only ever checked bytes-per-sector, so any real
 FAT volume on the machine passes -- including one on a different disk, which
 would mount cleanly and hold no SOURCE.SRC. Our own image is part 2048, root
 32 sectors, 26591 total.

  disk-diag-vol : Fat16Volume -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-diag-vol (vol) = act
   print-line-uni ("DIAG vol part " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-part-start) & " bps " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) & " spc " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster))
   print-line-uni ("DIAG vol fat " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-fat-start) & " root " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-root-start) & " ents " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-root-entry-count))
   print-line-uni ("DIAG vol data " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-data-start) & " total " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-total-sectors) & " clusters " & integer-to-text (vol.vol-cluster-count))
  end

 Measured on the ASUS 2026-08-13: the three lines above print and this one does
 not, and the None arm would have printed too, so the Maybe read-line returns
 for the SECOND call cannot be dispatched on at all. The first call's value is
 sound -- the mode line reaches dispatch-on-mode and selects DISK. The ring is
 sound: write 32, read 16, both lines consumed, the sixteen extra bytes are the
 UART poll's phantoms and sit past the read point. So the bytes are right and
 the value built from them is not. Left here as the record of what was measured;
 the path below no longer depends on it.

  disk-diag-path : Maybe Text -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-diag-path (pr) = print-line-uni "DIAG path SKIPPED (see chapter note)"

 The name is fixed by the image builder: build-img -Source always writes
 SOURCE.SRC, and the stub's second stdin line has never said anything else.

  disk-default-path : Text
  disk-default-path = "SOURCE.SRC"

 The device behind block-read-sector depends on how the block helper bound
 it. Bound through LoadedImage->DeviceHandle the firmware hands a PARTITION,
 whose LBA 0 is the volume's own boot sector and whose GPT read fails; bound
 through the LocateProtocol fallback it is a raw disk, and on a board with
 more than one the firmware chooses which. So the mount is a ladder judged
 by content rather than by the BPB alone -- a foreign FAT volume parses
 cleanly and holds no source file: take the GPT walk if the source resolves
 on it, then partition-relative LBA 0, and otherwise keep the GPT walk's
 answer so the geometry that gets printed is the mount that refused.

  disk-vol-has-source : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Block] Boolean
  disk-vol-has-source (vol) =
   if fat16-vol-is-usable vol == False then False
   else when fat16-resolve-path vol disk-default-path
     is Just (e) -> True
     is None -> False

  disk-locate-volume : [Device.Block] Fat16Volume
  disk-locate-volume = act
   g <- fat16-boot-volume
   ok <- disk-vol-has-source g
   if ok then g else disk-locate-volume-zero g
  end

 fat16-parse-bpb divides by the bytes-per-sector and sectors-per-cluster it
 reads off the sector, so LBA 0 is probed raw before fat16-init is allowed
 near it: on a raw disk LBA 0 is a protective MBR whose byte 11 is zero, and
 handing that to the parser is the divide fault Fat16's own prose warns
 about. 11 and 13 are the FAT BPB's bytes-per-sector and sectors-per-cluster
 offsets.

  disk-locate-volume-zero : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Block] Fat16Volume
  disk-locate-volume-zero (g) = act
   buf <- block-read-sector 0
   disk-locate-volume-zero-buf g buf
  end

  disk-locate-volume-zero-buf : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Block] Fat16Volume
  disk-locate-volume-zero-buf (g) (buf) =
   let bps = peek-byte buf 11 + peek-byte buf 12 * 256
   in let spc = peek-byte buf 13
   in if (bps == 512) & (spc > 0) then disk-locate-volume-zero-init g else g

  disk-locate-volume-zero-init : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Block] Fat16Volume
  disk-locate-volume-zero-init (g) = act
   z <- fat16-init 0
   ok <- disk-vol-has-source z
   if ok then z else g
  end

  disk-diag-len : Text, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-diag-len (p) (n) = act
   print-line-uni ("DIAG path len " & integer-to-text n)
   disk-diag-chars p n
  end

  disk-diag-chars : Text, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-diag-chars (p) (n) =
   if n <= 0 then print-line-uni "DIAG path EMPTY"
   else if n > 64 then print-line-uni "DIAG path len INSANE"
   else print-line-uni ("DIAG path c0 " & integer-to-text (char-code-at p 0)
     & " c1 " & integer-to-text (char-code-at p (if n > 1 then 1 else 0))
     & " c2 " & integer-to-text (char-code-at p (if n > 2 then 2 else 0))
     & " c3 " & integer-to-text (char-code-at p (if n > 3 then 3 else 0)))

 Each step announces itself BEFORE it acts, so the last line on the glass names
 the step that did not return rather than the last one that did.

 The build header. Wall clock off the CMOS RTC (armed-guarded, zeroes in any
 bed with no handoff), then the log cells are zeroed so the footer's phase
 table starts empty, and the start second is banked for the footer's total.

  disk-build-header : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-build-header (path) =
   if bp-armed 0 == False then print-text ""
   else act
    print-line-uni ("BUILD " & bp-rtc-date-text 0 & " " & bp-hms-text (bp-rtc-seconds 0) & " " & path)
    z <- bp-poke64 bp-log-count-cell 0
    s <- bp-poke64 bp-log-start-cell (bp-rtc-seconds 0)
    print-text ""
   end

  disk-source : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Console, Device.Block] Maybe Text
  disk-source (vol) (path) = act
   hd <- disk-build-header path
   print-line-uni ("DISK-SOURCE resolving [" & path & "] over " & integer-to-text (disk-root-sectors vol) & " root sectors")
   disk-source-found vol path
  end

  disk-source-found : Fat16Volume, Text -> [Console, Device.Block] Maybe Text
  disk-source-found (vol) (path) =
   when fat16-resolve-path vol path
     is Just (entry) -> disk-source-read vol entry
     is None -> None

  disk-source-read : Fat16Volume, Fat16DirEntry -> [Console, Device.Block] Maybe Text
  disk-source-read (vol) (entry) = act
   print-line-uni ("DISK-SOURCE found cluster " & integer-to-text (entry.de-cluster) & " size " & integer-to-text (entry.de-size) & " sectors " & integer-to-text (disk-sector-total vol (entry.de-size)))
   disk-source-bytes vol entry
  end

 The checksum is FNV-1a over the raw bytes as read, printed before the CCE
 conversion so a conversion defect cannot contaminate the fingerprint. The
 offset basis and prime are FNV's own constants; the products wrap, which is
 what FNV requires and what bare imul does.

  disk-fnv : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  disk-fnv (bs) (i) (n) (h) =
   if i >= n then h
   else disk-fnv bs (i + 1) n (w64-mul (bit-xor h (list-at bs i)) 1099511628211)

 The seed constant is FNV-1a 64's offset basis, #CBF29CE484222325. Its top bit
 is set, so read back as a signed i64 it is a large negative number, which is
 why a fingerprint printed as decimal reads negative.

  disk-build-fnv : Fat16DirEntry, List Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-build-fnv (entry) (bs) =
   if bp-armed 0 == False then print-text ""
   else print-line-uni ("SOURCE " & integer-to-text (entry.de-size) & " bytes fnv " & integer-to-text (disk-fnv bs 0 (list-length bs) (#CBF29CE484222325)))

  disk-source-bytes : Fat16Volume, Fat16DirEntry -> [Console, Device.Block] Maybe Text
  disk-source-bytes (vol) (entry) = act
   bs <- disk-read-chain vol (entry.de-cluster) (entry.de-size) 0 [] 0 disk-read-report-every (disk-sector-total vol (entry.de-size))
   f <- disk-build-fnv entry bs
   Just (disk-convert cce-to-unicode-table bs 0 (list-length bs) [])
  end

  disk-root-sectors : Fat16Volume -> Integer
  disk-root-sectors (vol) =
   ((vol.vol-root-entry-count) * 32 + (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) - 1) / (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector)

 The print comes FIRST. disk-rung holds on a failed rung and never returns, so
 a reason printed after it is a reason nobody has ever read: both of these
 callers looked identical on the glass for as long as the ladder has existed.

 The memory line prints LAST, in the failure path itself, because the
 firmware console scrolls and keeps no history: the 2026-08-13 boot printed
 the addresses that would have settled the corruption question and they were
 off the top of the glass before the failure line landed. The last lines are
 the only ones an operator can photograph.

  disk-no-source : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-no-source (why) = act
   print-line-uni why
   print-line-uni ("DIAG heap " & integer-to-text __heap-save & " hand base " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 126976 16) & " wh " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 126976 24) & " stride " & integer-to-text (peek-qword 126976 32))
   h <- bp-hold-band-if-armed bp-magenta
   print-line-uni "DISK-LADDER stopped at source"
  end

  compile-plain : Text, Text, Text, CompileFlags -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  compile-plain (source) (cmd) (mode) (flags) =
   if cmd == "CDX" then emit-cdx source mode flags (has-mode-flag mode "diskout")
   else if cmd == "IR-UNI" then emit-ir-uni source flags
   else if cmd == "IR-CCE" then emit-ir-cce source flags
   else if cmd == "MEASURE" then emit-measure source flags
   else if cmd == "TEXT" then emit-text-streaming source flags
   else refuse-unknown-mode cmd

 TEXT is named here rather than reached by falling through, and the fall-through
 is a refusal. An `is otherwise` on a front door makes a setting that does
 nothing look exactly like one that works (L-ACCEPTED): measured at seed
 8769F31E, `ELF`, `EFI`, `UEFI` and `IMG` each emitted text byte-identical to
 `TEXT` and to a deliberately nonsensical `ZZZZ-NOT-A-MODE` once the WD:/HEAP:
 telemetry every consumer already strips is removed, with `CDX` at 89,466 bytes
 as the control.

 The refusal ships only because a census said no live caller dies by it. The
 mode token is the first space-separated word, so flags ride along untouched;
 every producer in the tree sends CDX, TEXT, IR-UNI, IR-CCE, MEASURE, or DISK,
 which `dispatch-on-mode` takes before this. The single producer of anything
 else is `codex/plugs/elf/extract-x86-output.ps1`, which sends `ELF` and which
 nothing invokes: both greps that name it are comments, one of them the elf
 plug's own instructions for a workflow that this defect had already broken.

  refuse-unknown-mode : Text -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  refuse-unknown-mode (cmd) = act
   print-line-uni "CODEGEN-ERRORS:1"
   print-line-uni ("  unknown mode '" & cmd & "': nothing emitted, because a mode this compiler does not implement must not come back looking like a successful TEXT build. Modes are CDX, TEXT, IR-UNI, IR-CCE, MEASURE, DISK and RESOLVE.")
   print-line-uni ("CODEGEN-HALTED: unknown mode '" & cmd & "'; nothing emitted")
  end

  compile-with-quotations : SourceSplit, Text, Text, CompileFlags -> [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing
  compile-with-quotations (split) (cmd) (mode) (flags) =
   let mountain = init-phase-allocator
   in let keep-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-quote-keep-floor 0
   in let keep-base = build keep-height
   in let scratch-height = scaled-floor (flags.deck-scale) demand-quote-scratch-floor 0
   in let scratch-base = build scratch-height
   in let base-ws = build-works (split.sp-works)
   in let base-bundle = base-ws.ws-bundle
   in act
    bundle <- if has-mode-flag mode "store" then store-read-bundle base-bundle else act base-bundle end
    let qr = resolve-quotations (split.sp-body) bundle
    in if bag-has-errors (qr.qr-bag)
       then refuse-quotations (bag-errors (qr.qr-bag))
       else admit-quotations split cmd mode flags keep-base keep-height (qr.qr-prefix)
   end

  refuse-quotations : List Diagnostic -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  refuse-quotations (errs) = act
   print-codegen-error-header errs
   print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED: quotation refused; nothing emitted"
  end

  admit-quotations : SourceSplit, Text, Text, CompileFlags, Integer, Integer, Text -> [Console, FileSystem] Nothing
  admit-quotations (split) (cmd) (mode) (flags) (keep-base) (keep-height) (prefix) =
   if check-deck-overflow (text-length prefix * 2 + 4096) keep-height == 1
   then refuse-quotations (bag-errors (deck-overflow-bag "QUOTE-KEEP"))
   else let dset = __deck-set keep-base
   in let kept = deck-record (copy-sx-text keep-base prefix)
   in let sealed = compact-phase flags poison-parse
   in compile-plain (kept & split.sp-body) cmd mode flags

 RESOLVE's wire, for the consumers built against it (the Prism page and
 its beds; the boundary and this wire are recorded in
 PrismDevEnvironment.md's stage-2 convergence): source arrives on stdin
 exactly as TEXT/CDX take it; the disk is the linear-memory contract
 (`disk_reserve` before `_start` on wasm, the boot medium on metal);
 stdout carries the RESOLVED UNIT between `RESOLVE-BEGIN` and
 `RESOLVE-END` lines, in UNICODE like every other text mode and not in
 the CCE wire encoding -- the frame's whole purpose is to be read by a
 host, and `print-text` is `IR-CCE`'s writer, so a frame printed with it
 came back as mojibake for a consumer that had done nothing wrong --
 resolved chapters first, renamed
 `Chapter: quire--name` exactly as `disk-load-cite` does, then the source
 verbatim. A host with no volume (`block-sector-count` 0) gets the source
 back unchanged: the mount is never attempted, so the zeroed-BPB divide a
 bare medium produces cannot happen here. Resolution is best-effort: a
 cite whose chapter is not on the volume prepends nothing, and the
 downstream compile answers ONE CDX3007 at the cite (measured against a
 library image and its no-library control, 2026-08-28 -- an earlier
 draft of this paragraph claimed a use-site cascade, and the measurement
 killed it). `disk-load-cite` still prints one
 `CITE-MISSING <quire> <name>` line per absent chapter, before the
 frame, as a refinement CDX3007 cannot make: CDX3007's "not present in
 this compilation unit" is also what a typo'd chapter name produces,
 while CITE-MISSING says specifically that the medium was asked and did
 not have it, and it says so before a compile is paid for.

  emit-resolved : Text -> [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing
  emit-resolved (mode) = act
   source-raw <- read-file-uni mode
   let source = utf8-to-cce source-raw
   in act
    sectors <- block-sector-count
    prefix <- if sectors == 0 then act "" end
     else act
      vol <- disk-locate-volume
      disk-resolve-forewords vol source mode
     end
    print-line-uni "RESOLVE-BEGIN"
    print-uni (prefix & source)
    print-line-uni ""
    print-line-uni "RESOLVE-END"
   end
  end

  dispatch-on-mode : Text -> [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing
  dispatch-on-mode (mode) = act
   let cmd = parse-mode-cmd mode
   in if cmd == "DISK" then emit-from-disk mode
   else if cmd == "RESOLVE" then emit-resolved mode
   else act
    source-raw <- read-file-uni mode
    let raw = utf8-to-cce source-raw
    in let split = split-quoted-works raw
    in let flags = parse-mode-flags mode (text-length raw)
    in if has-quotations (split.sp-body)
       then compile-with-quotations split cmd mode flags
       else compile-plain (split.sp-body) cmd mode flags
   end
  end

  codex-opening : [Console, FileSystem, Device.Block] Nothing = act
   disk-rung "entered" 1 bp-cyan bp-red
   mode-result <- read-line
   when mode-result
    is Just (mode) -> dispatch-on-mode mode
    is None -> disk-no-mode 0
  end

  disk-no-mode : Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-no-mode (dummy) = act
   disk-rung "mode" 0 bp-yellow bp-cyan
   print-line-uni "Codex: no input mode on stdin"
  end

Page 1


Chapter: Parsmi--WASM Emitter
  cites Foreword chapter CCE
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

Section: Keywords and Sanitization

  wat-keywords : List Text =
   ["block", "br", "call", "data", "drop", "elem", "else", "end",
    "export", "func", "global", "if", "import", "local", "loop",
    "memory", "module", "mut", "nop", "offset", "param", "result",
    "return", "select", "start", "table", "then", "type", "unreachable"]

  wat-is-keyword : Text, Integer -> Boolean
  wat-is-keyword (name) (i) =
   if i == list-length wat-keywords then False
   else if list-at wat-keywords i == name then True
   else wat-is-keyword name (i + 1)

  wat-sanitize : Text -> Text
  wat-sanitize (name) =
   let s = text-replace name "-" "_"
   in if wat-is-keyword s 0 then s & "_v"
      else s

  wat-escape : Text -> Text
  wat-escape (s) =
   let s1 = text-replace s "\\" "\\\\"
   in text-replace s1 "\"" "\\\""

Section: Arity Map

  ArityEntry = record {
   name : Text,
   arity : Integer between 0 and 255,
   generic-count : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  bsearch-arity-pos : List ArityEntry, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bsearch-arity-pos (entries) (name) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then lo
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in let mid-name = (list-at entries mid).name
   in if text-compare name mid-name <= 0 then bsearch-arity-pos entries name lo mid
      else bsearch-arity-pos entries name (mid + 1) hi

  sorted-insert-arity : List ArityEntry, ArityEntry -> List ArityEntry
  sorted-insert-arity (xs) (e) =
   let len = list-length xs
   in let pos = bsearch-arity-pos xs (e.name) 0 len
   in list-insert-at xs pos e

  build-arity-map : List IRDef, Integer, List ArityEntry -> List ArityEntry
  build-arity-map (defs) (i) (acc) =
   if i == list-length defs then acc
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in build-arity-map defs (i + 1) (sorted-insert-arity acc (ArityEntry {
    name = d.name,
    arity = __narrow (list-length (d.params)),
    generic-count = 0
   }))

  lookup-arity : List ArityEntry, Text -> Integer
  lookup-arity (entries) (name) =
   let len = list-length entries
   in let pos = bsearch-arity-pos entries name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then -1
      else let e = list-at entries pos
      in if e.name == name then e.arity else -1

 WebAssembly has no function pointers, so a function used as a VALUE becomes
 an index into a table and the call becomes `call_indirect`. The index is the
 function's position in this sorted arity list, which is also the order the
 `elem` segment lists them in, so the two cannot drift: both are derived from
 the same list rather than kept in step by hand.

  lookup-fn-index : List ArityEntry, Text -> Integer
  lookup-fn-index (entries) (name) =
   let len = list-length entries
   in let pos = bsearch-arity-pos entries name 0 len
   in if pos >= len then -1
      else if (list-at entries pos).name == name then pos else -1

  wat-max-arity : List ArityEntry, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wat-max-arity (entries) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length entries then acc
   else let a = (list-at entries i).arity
   in wat-max-arity entries (i + 1) (if a > acc then a else acc)

  wat-params-of : Integer, Text -> Text
  wat-params-of (n) (acc) =
   if n <= 0 then acc else wat-params-of (n - 1) (acc & " (param i64)")

 One signature per arity, named by it. Every Codex value on this target is
 i64, so an arity fixes a signature completely and `call_indirect` can name
 it without inspecting the callee.

  wat-emit-fn-types : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  wat-emit-fn-types (n) (i) (acc) =
   if i > n then acc
   else let row = "  (type $fn" & integer-to-text i & " (func" & wat-params-of i "" & " (result i64)))\n"
   in wat-emit-fn-types n (i + 1) (acc & row)

  wat-emit-elem : List ArityEntry, Integer, Text -> Text
  wat-emit-elem (entries) (i) (acc) =
   acc & wat-emit-elem-span entries i (list-length entries)

  wat-emit-elem-span : List ArityEntry, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-elem-span (entries) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then ""
   else if hi - lo == 1 then " $" & wat-sanitize ((list-at entries lo).name)
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in wat-emit-elem-span entries lo mid & wat-emit-elem-span entries mid hi

 `$fn1` is emitted even when nothing in the module has arity 1, because
 `$clo_apply1`'s no-capture fast path names it unconditionally and a module
 of nothing but `opening` would otherwise carry a call to a type it never
 declares.

  wat-fn-type-ceiling : List ArityEntry -> Integer
  wat-fn-type-ceiling (entries) =
   let m = wat-max-arity entries 0 0
   in if m < 1 then 1 else m

  wat-emit-table : List ArityEntry -> Text
  wat-emit-table (entries) =
   let n = list-length entries
   in "  (table $ft " & integer-to-text n & " funcref)\n" &
      wat-emit-fn-types (wat-fn-type-ceiling entries) 0 "" &
      "  (elem $ft (i32.const 0)" & wat-emit-elem entries 0 "" & ")\n"

Section: String Table

  StringEntry = record {
   value : Text,
   offset : Integer
  }

  strtab-contains : List StringEntry, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  strtab-contains (xs) (s) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if (list-at xs i).value == s then True
   else strtab-contains xs s (i + 1)

  strtab-add : List StringEntry, Text -> List StringEntry
  strtab-add (xs) (s) =
   if strtab-contains xs s 0 then xs
   else list-push xs (StringEntry { value = s, offset = 0 })

  strtab-lookup : List StringEntry, Text, Integer -> Integer
  strtab-lookup (xs) (s) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then 0
   else if (list-at xs i).value == s then (list-at xs i).offset
   else strtab-lookup xs s (i + 1)

 A missing entry answers 0, and the table starts at 1024, so 0 is not an address
 any string has. Every site that turns a lookup into a POINTER goes through this
 one, which refuses with a marker naming the string rather than emitting a
 pointer to linear-memory address 0 and printing whatever is there. A collector
 that fails to reach some new site is then a named refusal instead of plausible
 output (L-BAILVALUE).

  wat-strtab-ref : List StringEntry, Text -> Text
  wat-strtab-ref (strings) (s) =
   let off = strtab-lookup strings s 0
   in if off == 0 then "(unreachable (; wasm plug: string literal absent from the table: " & s & " ;))"
      else "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & "))"

  strtab-assign-offsets : List StringEntry, Integer, Integer, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  strtab-assign-offsets (xs) (i) (off) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else let e = list-at xs i
   in let sz = 4 + text-length (e.value)
   in strtab-assign-offsets xs (i + 1) (off + sz) (list-push acc (StringEntry { value = e.value, offset = off }))

  strtab-end-offset : List StringEntry -> Integer
  strtab-end-offset (xs) =
   if list-length xs == 0 then 1024
   else let last = list-at xs (list-length xs - 1)
   in last.offset + 4 + text-length (last.value)

  collect-strings-expr : IRExpr, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-expr (e) (acc) =
   when e
    is IrIntLit (n) (sp) -> acc
    is IrNumLit (n) (sp) -> acc
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> strtab-add acc s
    is IrBoolLit (b) (sp) -> acc
    is IrCharLit (n) (sp) -> acc
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> acc
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr r (collect-strings-expr l acc)
    is IrNegate (operand) (nty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr operand acc
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr el (collect-strings-expr t (collect-strings-expr c acc))
    is IrLet (name) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr body (collect-strings-expr val acc)
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr a (collect-strings-expr f acc)
    is IrLambda (params) (body) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr body acc
    is IrList (elems) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-list elems acc
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-branches branches (collect-strings-expr scrut acc)
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-stmts stmts acc
    is IrHandle (eff) (body) (clauses) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr body acc
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr body acc
    is IrRecord (name) (fields) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-fields fields acc
    is IrFieldAccess (rec) (field) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr rec acc
    is IrFieldStore (rec) (field) (val) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr val (collect-strings-expr rec acc)
    is IrFork (body) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr body acc
    is IrAwait (task) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr task acc
    is IrTry (max) (body) (fb) (fail-stmts) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-stmts fail-stmts (collect-strings-stmts fb (collect-strings-stmts body acc))
    is IrError (msg) (ty) (sp) -> acc

  collect-strings-list : List IRExpr, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-list (xs) (acc) =
   collect-strings-list-loop xs acc 0

  collect-strings-list-loop : List IRExpr, List StringEntry, Integer -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-list-loop (xs) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else collect-strings-list-loop xs (collect-strings-expr (list-at xs i) acc) (i + 1)

  collect-strings-branches : List IRBranch, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-branches (bs) (acc) =
   collect-strings-branches-loop bs acc 0

  collect-strings-branches-loop : List IRBranch, List StringEntry, Integer -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-branches-loop (bs) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length bs then acc
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in collect-strings-branches-loop bs (collect-strings-pat (b.pattern) (collect-strings-expr (b.body) (collect-strings-expr (b.guard) acc))) (i + 1)

 A Text literal in a PATTERN is a string this module needs and no expression
 mentions, so the collector has to reach into the pattern or the table has no
 entry to look up.

  collect-strings-pat : IRPat, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-pat (p) (acc) =
   when p
    is IrLitPat (t) (ty) (sp) -> if wat-is-text-type ty then strtab-add acc t else acc
    is IrCtorPat (n) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> collect-strings-pats subs acc 0
    is otherwise -> acc

  collect-strings-pats : List IRPat, List StringEntry, Integer -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-pats (ps) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then acc
   else collect-strings-pats ps (collect-strings-pat (list-at ps i) acc) (i + 1)

  collect-strings-stmts : List IRActStmt, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-stmts (ss) (acc) =
   collect-strings-stmts-loop ss acc 0

  collect-strings-stmts-loop : List IRActStmt, List StringEntry, Integer -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-stmts-loop (ss) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ss then acc
   else let s = list-at ss i
   in let acc1 = when s
    is IrDoBind (name) (ty) (val) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr val acc
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> collect-strings-expr e acc
   in collect-strings-stmts-loop ss acc1 (i + 1)

  collect-strings-fields : List IRFieldVal, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-fields (fs) (acc) =
   collect-strings-fields-loop fs acc 0

  collect-strings-fields-loop : List IRFieldVal, List StringEntry, Integer -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-fields-loop (fs) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fs then acc
   else collect-strings-fields-loop fs (collect-strings-expr ((list-at fs i).value) acc) (i + 1)

  collect-strings-defs : List IRDef, Integer, List StringEntry -> List StringEntry
  collect-strings-defs (defs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length defs then acc
   else collect-strings-defs defs (i + 1) (collect-strings-expr ((list-at defs i).body) acc)

  byte-to-hex : Integer -> Text
  byte-to-hex (b) =
   let hi = b / 16
   in let lo = int-mod b 16
   in "\\" & hex-digit hi & hex-digit lo

  hex-digit : Integer -> Text
  hex-digit (n) =
   if n == 0 then "0" else if n == 1 then "1" else if n == 2 then "2"
   else if n == 3 then "3" else if n == 4 then "4" else if n == 5 then "5"
   else if n == 6 then "6" else if n == 7 then "7" else if n == 8 then "8"
   else if n == 9 then "9" else if n == 10 then "a" else if n == 11 then "b"
   else if n == 12 then "c" else if n == 13 then "d" else if n == 14 then "e"
   else "f"

  i32-to-hex-bytes : Integer -> Text
  i32-to-hex-bytes (v) =
   byte-to-hex (int-mod v 256) &
   byte-to-hex (int-mod (v / 256) 256) &
   byte-to-hex (int-mod (v / 65536) 256) &
   byte-to-hex (int-mod (v / 16777216) 256)

  wat-escape-data : Text, Integer, Integer, List Text -> Text
  wat-escape-data (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then text-concat-list acc
   else wat-escape-data s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (byte-to-hex (char-code (char-at s i))))

  emit-data-section : StringEntry -> Text
  emit-data-section (e) =
   let len = text-length (e.value)
   in "  (data (i32.const " & integer-to-text (e.offset) & ") \"" & i32-to-hex-bytes len & wat-escape-data (e.value) 0 len [] & "\")\n"

  emit-data-sections : List StringEntry, Integer -> Text
  emit-data-sections (xs) (i) =
   emit-data-span xs i (list-length xs)

  emit-data-span : List StringEntry, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-data-span (xs) (lo) (hi) =
   if lo >= hi then ""
   else if hi - lo == 1 then emit-data-section (list-at xs lo)
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in emit-data-span xs lo mid & emit-data-span xs mid hi

Section: CCE Output Tables

 2176 is the first CCE code point above tier 1, so the flat table covers
 tiers 0 and 1 and the block table covers tier 2. Both are generated from
 `to-unicode` rather than transcribed: a transcribed copy is a second source
 of truth for the mapping and drifts from the seed's silently.

  cce-tab-entries : Integer = 2176

  cce-tab-chunk-size : Integer = 128

  cce-tab-bytes : Integer = cce-tab-entries * 2

  cce-t2-entry-bytes : Integer = 12

  cce-out-buf-bytes : Integer = 256

  cce-flush-at : Integer = 240

  cce-in-buf-bytes : Integer = 65536

  cce-tables-bytes : Integer =
   cce-tab-bytes + cce-tier2-block-count * cce-t2-entry-bytes + cce-out-buf-bytes +
   cce-rev-entries + cce-in-buf-bytes

  cce-digit-zero : Integer = from-unicode 48

  cce-minus : Integer = from-unicode 45

  cce-dot : Integer = from-unicode 46

  cce-newline : Integer = from-unicode 10

 The input boundary is the output boundary run backwards, and it needs the
 reverse table. 128 entries is the whole of it: a byte under 128 is ASCII
 whichever way it came, and `from-unicode` answers for exactly that band.
 Anything above is left as the replacement rather than guessed at, which is
 what x86-64's `__read_line` does with the same 128-entry table.

  cce-rev-entries : Integer = 128

  cce-rev-chunk : Integer, Integer, List Text -> Text
  cce-rev-chunk (start) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= cce-rev-entries then text-concat-list acc
   else let u = from-unicode (start + i)
   in let b = if u < 0 then 0 else if u > 255 then 0 else u
   in cce-rev-chunk start (i + 1) (list-push acc (byte-to-hex b))

  cce-u16-hex : Integer -> Text
  cce-u16-hex (v) =
   byte-to-hex (int-mod v 256) & byte-to-hex (int-mod (v / 256) 256)

  cce-tab-chunk : Integer, Integer, List Text -> Text
  cce-tab-chunk (start) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= cce-tab-chunk-size then text-concat-list acc
   else cce-tab-chunk start (i + 1) (list-push acc (cce-u16-hex (to-unicode (start + i))))

  cce-t2-chunk : Integer, List Text -> Text
  cce-t2-chunk (block) (acc) =
   if block >= cce-tier2-block-count then text-concat-list acc
   else cce-t2-chunk (block + 1) (list-push acc
    (i32-to-hex-bytes (cce-tier2-cce-base block) &
     i32-to-hex-bytes (cce-tier2-block-size block) &
     i32-to-hex-bytes (cce-tier2-block-unicode-base block)))

  wat-stream-cce-tab : Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  wat-stream-cce-tab (base) (start) =
   if start >= cce-tab-entries then print-uni ""
   else let h = __heap-save
   in let chunk = cce-tab-chunk start 0 []
   in act
    print-uni ("  (data (i32.const " & integer-to-text (base + start * 2) & ") \"" & chunk & "\")\n")
    let restored = __heap-restore h
    in wat-stream-cce-tab base (start + cce-tab-chunk-size)
   end

  wat-stream-cce-tables : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  wat-stream-cce-tables (tab-base) (t2-base) (rev-base) = act
   wat-stream-cce-tab tab-base 0
   print-uni ("  (data (i32.const " & integer-to-text t2-base & ") \"" & cce-t2-chunk 0 [] & "\")\n")
   print-uni ("  (data (i32.const " & integer-to-text rev-base & ") \"" & cce-rev-chunk 0 0 [] & "\")\n")
  end

Section: Type Lookups

  find-record-field-index : List RecordField, Text, Integer -> Integer
  find-record-field-index (fields) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fields then 0
   else if (list-at fields i).name.value == name then i
   else find-record-field-index fields name (i + 1)

  resolve-record-fields : List ATypeDef, Text, Integer -> List RecordField
  resolve-record-fields (tds) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then []
   else when list-at tds i
    is ARecordTypeDef (n) (tp) (fds) (recmut) (sp) ->
     if n.value == name then afields-to-rfields fds 0 []
     else resolve-record-fields tds name (i + 1)
    is otherwise -> resolve-record-fields tds name (i + 1)

  afields-to-rfields : List ARecordFieldDef, Integer, List RecordField -> List RecordField
  afields-to-rfields (afs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length afs then acc
   else let af = list-at afs i
   in afields-to-rfields afs (i + 1) (list-push acc (RecordField { name = af.name, type-val = atype-to-codex-type (af.type-expr) }))

 A clamping bounded field saturates at its declared band. WebAssembly has no
 integer min or max instruction -- i64.min and i64.max do not exist, only the
 float forms -- so the clamp is two `select`s, each testing with i64.gt_s or
 i64.lt_s. `select` yields its FIRST operand when the condition is non-zero.

 The operand is repeated four times in the emitted text, so it must be a
 `local.get` and never an arbitrary expression, or the value would be
 evaluated four times. Both call sites store into `$_tv` first, which is what
 makes the repetition safe.

  WatBand = record {
   clamps : Boolean,
   wraps : Boolean,
   blo : Integer,
   bhi : Integer
  }

  wat-no-band : WatBand
  wat-no-band = WatBand { clamps = False, wraps = False, blo = 0, bhi = 0 }

  wat-atype-band : ATypeExpr -> WatBand
  wat-atype-band (te) =
   when te
    is ABoundedIntType (b) (lo) (hi) (mode) (s) ->
     when mode
      is OvClamping -> WatBand { clamps = True, wraps = False, blo = lo, bhi = hi }
      is OvWrapping -> WatBand { clamps = False, wraps = True, blo = lo, bhi = hi }
      is otherwise -> wat-no-band
    is otherwise -> wat-no-band

  wat-band-in-afields : List ARecordFieldDef, Text, Integer -> WatBand
  wat-band-in-afields (fs) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fs then wat-no-band
   else if (list-at fs i).name.value == name then wat-atype-band ((list-at fs i).type-expr)
   else wat-band-in-afields fs name (i + 1)

  wat-band-for-field : List ATypeDef, Text, Integer -> WatBand
  wat-band-for-field (tds) (field-name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then wat-no-band
   else when list-at tds i
    is ARecordTypeDef (n) (tp) (fds) (recmut) (sp) ->
     let b = wat-band-in-afields fds field-name 0
     in if b.clamps | b.wraps then b else wat-band-for-field tds field-name (i + 1)
    is otherwise -> wat-band-for-field tds field-name (i + 1)

 A wrapping band is admitted only at a hardware width (`bounds-are-hw-width`,
 `Types/CodexTypeHelpers.codex`), so the band IS the width and no table is
 needed: an unsigned band's `hi` is exactly the mask, and a signed band's `hi`
 names which sign-extending instruction narrows it. A band that is neither
 refuses rather than answering, because a wrong wrap and a correct one are the
 same kind of number.

  wat-wrap : WatBand, Text -> Text
  wat-wrap (b) (v) =
   if b.blo == 0 then "(i64.and " & v & " (i64.const " & integer-to-text (b.bhi) & "))"
   else if b.bhi == 127 then "(i64.extend8_s " & v & ")"
   else if b.bhi == 32767 then "(i64.extend16_s " & v & ")"
   else if b.bhi == 2147483647 then "(i64.extend32_s " & v & ")"
   else if b.bhi == 9223372036854775807 then v
   else "(unreachable (; no wasm form for a wrapping band " & integer-to-text (b.blo) & " to " & integer-to-text (b.bhi) & " ;))"

  wat-clamp : WatBand, Text -> Text
  wat-clamp (b) (v) =
   if b.wraps then wat-wrap b v
   else if b.clamps == False then v
   else let hi-c = "(i64.const " & integer-to-text (b.bhi) & ")"
   in let lo-c = "(i64.const " & integer-to-text (b.blo) & ")"
   in let hi-done = "(select " & hi-c & " " & v & " (i64.gt_s " & v & " " & hi-c & "))"
   in "(select " & lo-c & " " & hi-done & " (i64.lt_s " & hi-done & " " & lo-c & "))"

  field-index-from-ctx : WasmCtx, CodexType, Text -> Integer
  field-index-from-ctx (ctx) (ty) (field-name) =
   when ty
    is RecordTy (name) (args) (fields) ->
     if list-length fields > 0 then find-record-field-index fields field-name 0
     else find-record-field-index (record-fields-by-name (ctx.type-defs) (name.value) 0) field-name 0
    is ConstructedTy (name) (args) -> find-record-field-index (resolve-record-fields (ctx.type-defs) (name.value) 0) field-name 0
    is TypeCon (name) -> find-record-field-index (resolve-record-fields (ctx.type-defs) (name.value) 0) field-name 0
    is otherwise -> 0

  find-typedef-by-name : List ATypeDef, Text, Integer -> Integer
  find-typedef-by-name (tds) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then -1
   else when list-at tds i
    is ARecordTypeDef (n) (tp) (fs) (recmut) (sp) -> if n.value == name then i else find-typedef-by-name tds name (i + 1)
    is AVariantTypeDef (n) (tp) (cs) (sp) -> if n.value == name then i else find-typedef-by-name tds name (i + 1)
    is AUnitTypeDef (n) (unitbase) (sp) -> if n.value == name then i else find-typedef-by-name tds name (i + 1)

  find-ctor-tag : List AVariantCtorDef, Text, Integer -> Integer
  find-ctor-tag (ctors) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ctors then -1
   else if (list-at ctors i).name.value == name then i
   else find-ctor-tag ctors name (i + 1)

  find-ctor-in-typedefs : List ATypeDef, Text, Integer -> Integer
  find-ctor-in-typedefs (tds) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then -1
   else when list-at tds i
    is AVariantTypeDef (n) (tp) (ctors) (sp) ->
     let tag = find-ctor-tag ctors name 0
     in if tag >= 0 then tag else find-ctor-in-typedefs tds name (i + 1)
    is otherwise -> find-ctor-in-typedefs tds name (i + 1)

  is-ctor-name : List ATypeDef, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  is-ctor-name (tds) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then False
   else when list-at tds i
    is AVariantTypeDef (n) (tp) (ctors) (sp) ->
     if find-ctor-tag ctors name 0 >= 0 then True
     else is-ctor-name tds name (i + 1)
    is otherwise -> is-ctor-name tds name (i + 1)

Section: Emitter Context

  WasmCtx = record {
   arities : List ArityEntry,
   strings : List StringEntry,
   type-defs : List ATypeDef,
   bound : List Text,
   scrut-depth : Integer
  }

  wat-scrut-name : Integer -> Text
  wat-scrut-name (d) =
   if d <= 0 then "_s" else wat-scrut-name (d - 1) & "s"

  wat-scrut : WasmCtx -> Text
  wat-scrut (ctx) = "$" & wat-scrut-name (ctx.scrut-depth)

  wat-is-scrut : Text -> Boolean
  wat-is-scrut (n) =
   text-length n >= 2 & n == wat-scrut-name (text-length n - 2)

  wat-shift-scrut-name : Text -> Text
  wat-shift-scrut-name (n) =
   if wat-is-scrut n then wat-scrut-name (text-length n - 1) else n

  wat-shift-scrut : List Text, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  wat-shift-scrut (xs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else wat-shift-scrut xs (i + 1) (list-push acc (wat-shift-scrut-name (list-at xs i)))

  locals-merge : List Text, List Text, Integer -> List Text
  locals-merge (acc) (xs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else locals-merge (locals-add acc (list-at xs i)) xs (i + 1)

  ctx-deeper : WasmCtx -> WasmCtx
  ctx-deeper (ctx) =
   WasmCtx { arities = ctx.arities, strings = ctx.strings, type-defs = ctx.type-defs, bound = ctx.bound, scrut-depth = ctx.scrut-depth + 1 }

Section: Local Collection

  locals-contains : List Text, Text -> Boolean
  locals-contains (xs) (name) =
   locals-contains-loop xs name 0

  locals-contains-loop : List Text, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  locals-contains-loop (xs) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if list-at xs i == name then True
   else locals-contains-loop xs name (i + 1)

  locals-add : List Text, Text -> List Text
  locals-add (xs) (name) =
   if locals-contains xs name then xs
   else list-push xs name

  ctx-with : WasmCtx, List Text -> WasmCtx
  ctx-with (ctx) (bd) =
   WasmCtx { arities = ctx.arities, strings = ctx.strings, type-defs = ctx.type-defs, bound = bd, scrut-depth = ctx.scrut-depth }

  collect-locals-params : List IRParam, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-params (ps) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length ps then acc
   else collect-locals-params ps (i + 1) (locals-add acc (wat-sanitize ((list-at ps i).name)))

  collect-locals-expr : IRExpr, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-expr (e) (acc) =
   when e
    is IrIntLit (n) (sp) -> acc
    is IrNumLit (n) (sp) -> acc
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> acc
    is IrBoolLit (b) (sp) -> acc
    is IrCharLit (n) (sp) -> acc
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> locals-add (locals-add acc "_rp") "_tv"
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr r (collect-locals-expr l acc)
    is IrNegate (operand) (nty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr operand acc
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr el (collect-locals-expr t (collect-locals-expr c acc))
    is IrLet (name) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr body (collect-locals-expr val (locals-add acc (wat-sanitize name)))
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr a (collect-locals-expr f (locals-add (locals-add acc "_rp") "_tv"))
    is IrLambda (params) (body) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr body (collect-locals-params params 0 acc)
    is IrList (elems) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-list elems (locals-add (locals-add (locals-add acc "_rp") "_lp") "_tv")
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-branches branches (collect-locals-expr scrut (locals-add acc "_s"))
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-stmts stmts acc
    is IrHandle (eff) (body) (clauses) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr body acc
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr body acc
    is IrRecord (name) (fields) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-fields fields (locals-add (locals-add acc "_rp") "_tv")
    is IrFieldAccess (rec) (field) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr rec acc
    is IrFieldStore (rec) (field) (val) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr val (collect-locals-expr rec (locals-add (locals-add acc "_rp") "_tv"))
    is IrFork (body) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr body acc
    is IrAwait (task) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr task acc
    is IrTry (max) (body) (fb) (fail-stmts) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-stmts fail-stmts (collect-locals-stmts fb (collect-locals-stmts body acc))
    is IrError (msg) (ty) (sp) -> acc

  collect-locals-list : List IRExpr, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-list (xs) (acc) =
   collect-locals-list-loop xs acc 0

  collect-locals-list-loop : List IRExpr, List Text, Integer -> List Text
  collect-locals-list-loop (xs) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else collect-locals-list-loop xs (collect-locals-expr (list-at xs i) acc) (i + 1)

  collect-locals-branches : List IRBranch, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-branches (bs) (acc) =
   collect-locals-branches-loop bs acc 0

  collect-locals-branches-loop : List IRBranch, List Text, Integer -> List Text
  collect-locals-branches-loop (bs) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length bs then acc
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let acc1 = collect-locals-pat (b.pattern) acc
   in let acc2 = locals-merge acc1 (wat-shift-scrut (collect-locals-expr (b.guard) []) 0 []) 0
   in let acc3 = collect-locals-expr (b.body) acc2
   in collect-locals-branches-loop bs acc3 (i + 1)

  collect-locals-pat : IRPat, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-pat (p) (acc) =
   when p
    is IrVarPat (name) (ty) (sp) -> locals-add acc (wat-sanitize name)
    is IrCtorPat (name) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> collect-locals-pats subs acc
    is IrLitPat (text) (ty) (sp) -> acc
    is IrWildPat (sp) -> acc
    is IrVecPat (pats) (ty) (sp) -> acc

  collect-locals-pats : List IRPat, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-pats (ps) (acc) =
   collect-locals-pats-loop ps acc 0

  collect-locals-pats-loop : List IRPat, List Text, Integer -> List Text
  collect-locals-pats-loop (ps) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then acc
   else collect-locals-pats-loop ps (collect-locals-pat (list-at ps i) acc) (i + 1)

  collect-locals-stmts : List IRActStmt, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-stmts (ss) (acc) =
   collect-locals-stmts-loop ss acc 0

  collect-locals-stmts-loop : List IRActStmt, List Text, Integer -> List Text
  collect-locals-stmts-loop (ss) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ss then acc
   else let s = list-at ss i
   in let acc1 = when s
    is IrDoBind (name) (ty) (val) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr val (locals-add acc (wat-sanitize name))
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> collect-locals-expr e acc
   in collect-locals-stmts-loop ss acc1 (i + 1)

  collect-locals-fields : List IRFieldVal, List Text -> List Text
  collect-locals-fields (fs) (acc) =
   collect-locals-fields-loop fs acc 0

  collect-locals-fields-loop : List IRFieldVal, List Text, Integer -> List Text
  collect-locals-fields-loop (fs) (acc) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fs then acc
   else collect-locals-fields-loop fs (collect-locals-expr ((list-at fs i).value) acc) (i + 1)

  emit-local-decls : List Text, Integer -> Text
  emit-local-decls (names) (i) =
   if i >= list-length names then ""
   else "(local $" & list-at names i & " i64) " & emit-local-decls names (i + 1)

 A parameter already occupies a slot under its own name, so re-declaring it
 is `redefinition of parameter` and wat2wasm refuses the whole module on the
 first one. Sharing the slot is what this emitter already does for a `let`
 that shadows an outer `let`, since `locals-add` folds equal names together.

  params-contain : List IRParam, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  params-contain (ps) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then False
   else if wat-sanitize ((list-at ps i).name) == name then True
   else params-contain ps name (i + 1)

  locals-minus-params : List Text, List IRParam, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  locals-minus-params (xs) (ps) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else if params-contain ps (list-at xs i) 0 then locals-minus-params xs ps (i + 1) acc
   else locals-minus-params xs ps (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

Section: Type Definition Emission

  emit-wat-type-defs : List ATypeDef, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-type-defs (tds) (i) =
   if i == list-length tds then ""
   else emit-wat-type-def (list-at tds i) & emit-wat-type-defs tds (i + 1)

  emit-wat-type-def : ATypeDef -> Text
  emit-wat-type-def (td) =
   when td
    is ARecordTypeDef (name) (tparams) (fields) (recmut) (s) ->
     "  ;; record " & name.value & ": " & integer-to-text (list-length fields) & " fields at 8 bytes each\n"
    is AVariantTypeDef (name) (tparams) (ctors) (s) ->
     "  ;; variant " & name.value & ": tag i64 + max " & integer-to-text (wat-variant-max-fields ctors 0 0) & " fields\n"
    is AUnitTypeDef (name) (unitbase) (s) -> ""

  wat-variant-max-fields : List AVariantCtorDef, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wat-variant-max-fields (ctors) (i) (mx) =
   if i == list-length ctors then mx
   else let n = list-length ((list-at ctors i).fields)
   in wat-variant-max-fields ctors (i + 1) (if n > mx then n else mx)

Section: Constructor Equality

 A field declared at a TYPE PARAMETER is compared by POINTER, and that is the
 oracle's answer rather than a shortcut taken here: measured 2026-08-25
 against the seed, `Held "hi" == Held "hi"` is False on x86-64, because
 `subst-field-type` has no argument to substitute and the compare falls to
 the integer path. A field declared `Text` is compared by content on both.

 One function per variant type rather than the oracle's inlining, because
 inlining is what makes `==` on a RECURSIVE variant unbounded: `Wrap Leaf ==
 Wrap Leaf` over `Nest = | Leaf | Wrap (Nest)` crashes the x86-64 compiler in
 `alloc-temp` with an invalid opcode, and the same type with no `==` compiles
 clean.

  wat-eq-fn-prefix : Text = "$cx_eq_"

  wat-variant-typedef-name : List ATypeDef, Text -> Text
  wat-variant-typedef-name (tds) (n) =
   if text-length n == 0 then ""
   else let i = find-typedef-by-name tds n 0
   in if i < 0 then ""
      else when list-at tds i
       is AVariantTypeDef (vn) (tp) (cs) (s) -> n
       is otherwise -> ""

  wat-eq-sum-name : List ATypeDef, CodexType -> Text
  wat-eq-sum-name (tds) (ty) =
   when ty
    is SumTy (name) (args) (ctors) -> wat-variant-typedef-name tds (name.value)
    is ConstructedTy (name) (args) -> wat-variant-typedef-name tds (name.value)
    is TypeCon (name) -> wat-variant-typedef-name tds (name.value)
    is UnitTy (name) (inner) -> wat-eq-sum-name tds inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (ret) -> wat-eq-sum-name tds ret
    is otherwise -> ""

  wat-atype-head-name : ATypeExpr -> Text
  wat-atype-head-name (at) =
   when at
    is ANamedType (n) (s) -> n.value
    is AAppType (ctor) (args) (s) -> wat-atype-head-name ctor
    is ALinearType (inner) (s) -> wat-atype-head-name inner
    is ABoundedIntType (base) (lo) (hi) (mode) (s) -> wat-atype-head-name base
    is AConstrainedType (cn) (vn) (inner) (s) -> wat-atype-head-name inner
    is otherwise -> ""

 A `Real` field is compared as an IEEE-754 double, which is what `emit-eq-op`
 does with a `RealTy` operand. Every real on this target is carried as f64
 bits in an i64 slot, `IrNumLit` included, so there is one width to reinterpret
 and no f32 case to separate.

  wat-eq-field-cmp : List ATypeDef, ATypeExpr, Text, Text -> Text
  wat-eq-field-cmp (tds) (fty) (la) (lb) =
   let fn = wat-atype-head-name fty
   in if fn == "Text" then "(call $text_eq " & la & " " & lb & ")"
      else if fn == "Real" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (f64.eq (f64.reinterpret_i64 " & la & ") (f64.reinterpret_i64 " & lb & ")))"
      else if text-length (wat-variant-typedef-name tds fn) > 0 then "(call " & wat-eq-fn-prefix & wat-sanitize fn & " " & la & " " & lb & ")"
      else "(i64.extend_i32_u (i64.eq " & la & " " & lb & "))"

  wat-eq-field-tests : List ATypeDef, List ATypeExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-eq-field-tests (tds) (fs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fs then ""
   else let off = 8 + i * 8
   in let la = "(i64.load (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $a)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")))"
   in let lb = "(i64.load (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $b)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")))"
   in "      (if (i64.eqz " & wat-eq-field-cmp tds (list-at fs i) la lb & ") (then (return (i64.const 0))))\n" &
      wat-eq-field-tests tds fs (i + 1)

  wat-eq-ctor-arms : List ATypeDef, List AVariantCtorDef, Integer -> Text
  wat-eq-ctor-arms (tds) (ctors) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ctors then ""
   else let fs = (list-at ctors i).fields
   in (if list-length fs == 0 then ""
       else "    (if (i64.eq (local.get $t) (i64.const " & integer-to-text i & ")) (then\n" &
            wat-eq-field-tests tds fs 0 &
            "      (return (i64.const 1))))\n") &
      wat-eq-ctor-arms tds ctors (i + 1)

  wat-all-ctors-nullary : List AVariantCtorDef, Integer -> Boolean
  wat-all-ctors-nullary (ctors) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ctors then True
   else if list-length ((list-at ctors i).fields) > 0 then False
   else wat-all-ctors-nullary ctors (i + 1)

  wat-eq-func : List ATypeDef, Text, List AVariantCtorDef -> Text
  wat-eq-func (tds) (tn) (ctors) =
   let hdr = "  (func " & wat-eq-fn-prefix & wat-sanitize tn & " (param $a i64) (param $b i64) (result i64)\n"
   in if wat-all-ctors-nullary ctors 0 then
    hdr & "    (i64.extend_i32_u (i64.eq (i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $a))) (i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $b))))))\n\n"
   else
    hdr &
    "    (local $t i64)\n" &
    "    (local.set $t (i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $a))))\n" &
    "    (if (i64.ne (local.get $t) (i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $b)))) (then (return (i64.const 0))))\n" &
    wat-eq-ctor-arms tds ctors 0 &
    "    (i64.const 1))\n\n"

  wat-eq-funcs : List ATypeDef, Integer -> Text
  wat-eq-funcs (tds) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then ""
   else (when list-at tds i
    is AVariantTypeDef (name) (tparams) (ctors) (s) -> wat-eq-func tds (name.value) ctors
    is otherwise -> "") & wat-eq-funcs tds (i + 1)

Section: Expression Emission

 4096 is the depth at which this emitter gives up, and it refuses there
 rather than answering. It bailed at 256 with `(i64.const 0)` until
 2026-08-27, which is a WRONG ANSWER and not a bound: a chain of 300 nested
 `let`s printed 44 where x86-64 printed 300, assembling and running clean
 with no diagnostic anywhere. `let` is what makes that reachable, because it
 recurses without nesting its output, so wat2wasm's own parser limit (which
 sits between 200 and 250 folded expressions) never sees it. The number is
 chosen well above anything real: the largest program we have is the
 compiler's own 2.9 MB source, and it self-compiles byte-identically, so
 nothing in it reaches even the old 256.

  emit-wat-expr : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  emit-wat-expr (ctx) (e) = emit-wat-expr-at ctx e 0

  emit-wat-expr-at : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-expr-at (ctx) (e) (depth) =
   if depth >= 4096 then "(unreachable (; wasm plug: expression nested past 4096, no form ;))"
   else when e
    is IrIntLit (n) (sp) -> "(i64.const " & integer-to-text n & ")"
    is IrNumLit (n) (sp) -> "(i64.const " & integer-to-text n & ")"
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> wat-strtab-ref (ctx.strings) s
    is IrBoolLit (b) (sp) -> if b then "(i64.const 1)" else "(i64.const 0)"
    is IrCharLit (n) (sp) -> "(i64.const " & integer-to-text n & ")"
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-name ctx n
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-binary ctx op l r ty depth
    is IrNegate (operand) (nty) (sp) -> if wat-is-real-type (ir-expr-type operand) then wat-f64-out ("(f64.neg " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx operand (depth + 1)) & ")")
                                        else "(i64.sub (i64.const 0) " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx operand (depth + 1) & ")"
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-if-chain ctx e depth
    is IrLet (name) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) -> "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx val (depth + 1) & ") " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx body (depth + 1)
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-apply ctx e
    is IrLambda (params) (body) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx body (depth + 1)
    is IrList (elems) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-list ctx elems depth
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-match ctx scrut branches depth
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-act ctx stmts
    is IrHandle (eff) (body) (clauses) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx body (depth + 1)
    is IrWithTimeout (secs) (effs) (scopes) (body) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx body (depth + 1)
    is IrRecord (name) (fields) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-record ctx fields ty depth
    is IrFieldAccess (rec) (field) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-field-access ctx rec field ty depth
    is IrFieldStore (rec) (field) (val) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-field-store ctx rec field val ty depth
    is IrFork (body) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx body (depth + 1)
    is IrAwait (task) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx task (depth + 1)
    is IrTry (max) (body) (fb) (fail-stmts) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-act ctx body
    is IrError (msg) (ty) (sp) -> "(unreachable)"

 An `else if` chain is the THIRD nesting site, and flattening the two match
 paths does not touch it. Measured against arm64-stdio.wat with both match
 twins already flat: max WAT depth 312, unmoved, and the deepest point is
 `a64_emit_direct_call` carrying 154 `(if ` and not one `(block $_a`. The
 chain is 152 alternating `if`/`else` levels, which is this emitter's arm and
 the arm count of the plug's instruction dispatch. The baseline table in
 `plugs-backlog.md` 2.03 measured "longest consecutive `else if` chain
 against WAT depth" and that is exactly what this is.

 Same repair as the match arms: the chain's else-spine becomes siblings under
 one outer block, so the nesting an `if` chain contributes is TWO whatever the
 chain length. A test that fails branches to the end of its own block and the
 next sibling runs; one that passes branches out of the whole chain carrying
 its value. Falling off the last sibling runs the final `else`, whose value is
 the block's result, which is what the innermost `else` used to say.

  emit-wat-if-chain : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-if-chain (ctx) (e) (depth) =
   "(block $_i (result i64) " & emit-wat-if-arms ctx e depth & ")"

  emit-wat-if-arms : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-if-arms (ctx) (e) (depth) =
   when e
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) ->
     "(block $_c (br_if $_c (i32.eqz (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx c (depth + 1) & "))) (br $_i " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx t (depth + 1) & ")) "
       & emit-wat-if-arms ctx el depth
    is otherwise -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx e (depth + 1)

 The labels are plain rather than numbered, so a chain nested inside an arm's
 body shadows them and an inner chain's branch belongs to the inner chain.
 That is the wanted reading and it is the same one the match arms rely on for
 `$_m`. The spine walk does NOT advance `depth`, which is the point: the guard
 counts real nesting and the spine no longer nests.

 `$_rp`, `$_lp` and `$_tv` are ONE set of scratch locals per function, so a
 construction nested inside an enclosing construction's field expression
 overwrites the enclosing object's pointer, and every remaining field store
 lands in the inner object. Measured 2026-08-25 in the compiler's own module:
 `LexCollected { collected-tokens = ll-push acc (make-token (deck-record
 EndOfFile) 0 st), ... }` allocated 16 bytes, then `deck-record EndOfFile`
 reset `$_rp` to its own 8-byte block, so the token list was stored over the
 `EndOfFile` tag and the errors 8 bytes past the block. `is-done` then read
 4294967316 where 0 belongs, the parser never saw end of file, and the whole
 compiler spun in `skip-to-next-line`.

 The guard is the WASM OPERAND STACK, which is per-invocation and needs no
 second local: push the pointer, evaluate the field, pop it back. `(local.set
 $x)` with no folded operand pops, which is what makes this two instructions
 rather than a shadow stack. It is emitted only where the field expression
 can allocate, because emitting it everywhere grows the module by roughly a
 third.

  wat-scratch-safe : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Boolean
  wat-scratch-safe (ctx) (e) =
   when e
    is IrIntLit (n) (sp) -> True
    is IrNumLit (n) (sp) -> True
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> True
    is IrBoolLit (b) (sp) -> True
    is IrCharLit (n) (sp) -> True
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) -> wat-name-scratch-safe ctx n
    is IrBinary (op) (l) (r) (ty) (sp) -> wat-scratch-safe ctx l & wat-scratch-safe ctx r
    is IrNegate (operand) (nty) (sp) -> wat-scratch-safe ctx operand
    is IrFieldAccess (rec) (field) (ty) (sp) -> wat-scratch-safe ctx rec
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) -> wat-scratch-safe ctx f & wat-scratch-safe ctx a
    is otherwise -> False

  wat-name-scratch-safe : WasmCtx, Text -> Boolean
  wat-name-scratch-safe (ctx) (n) =
   if is-ctor-name (ctx.type-defs) n 0 then False
   else if n == "read-line" then False
   else if n == "read-line-cce" then False
   else if n == "__record-set" then False
   else True

  wat-guard-scratch : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Text, Text -> Text
  wat-guard-scratch (ctx) (e) (slot) (emitted) =
   if wat-scratch-safe ctx e then "(local.set $_tv " & emitted & ") "
   else "(local.get " & slot & ") (local.set $_tv " & emitted & ") (local.set " & slot & ") "

  emit-wat-record : WasmCtx, List IRFieldVal, CodexType, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-record (ctx) (fields) (ty) (depth) =
   let fc = list-length fields
   in let sz = fc * 8
   in let alloc = "(local.set $_rp (i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.const " & integer-to-text sz & "))))"
   in let stores = emit-wat-record-fields ctx fields ty 0 depth
   in alloc & " " & stores & "(local.get $_rp)"

  emit-wat-record-fields : WasmCtx, List IRFieldVal, CodexType, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-record-fields (ctx) (fields) (ty) (i) (depth) =
   if i >= list-length fields then ""
   else let f = list-at fields i
   in let off = wat-slot-in-ty ctx ty (f.name) i * 8
   in let band = wat-band-for-rec (ctx.type-defs) ty (f.name)
   in wat-guard-scratch ctx (f.value) "$_rp" (emit-wat-expr-at ctx (f.value) (depth + 1)) &
      "(i64.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")) " & wat-clamp band "(local.get $_tv)" & ") " & emit-wat-record-fields ctx fields ty (i + 1) depth

  find-field-in-typedefs : List ATypeDef, Text, Integer -> Integer
  find-field-in-typedefs (tds) (field-name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length tds then 0
   else when list-at tds i
    is ARecordTypeDef (n) (tp) (fds) (recmut) (sp) ->
     let idx = find-afield-index fds field-name 0
     in if idx >= 0 then idx else find-field-in-typedefs tds field-name (i + 1)
    is otherwise -> find-field-in-typedefs tds field-name (i + 1)

 A field-access or field-store node spells its field `name/slot`, and those
 two node kinds are the only ones that carry the slot: a `field-val` inside a
 record construction carries the bare name. The slot is the authority, so it
 is read rather than searched for. `find-field-in-typedefs` remains as the
 fallback for a node with no slot, and it answers 0 for a name it cannot
 find, which is why a plug that hands it the unstripped `name/slot` reads
 slot 0 for every field in the program.

  wat-slash-pos : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wat-slash-pos (f) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then (-1)
   else if char-to-text (char-at f i) == "/" then i
   else wat-slash-pos f (i + 1) len

  wat-field-bare : Text -> Text
  wat-field-bare (f) =
   let slash = wat-slash-pos f 0 (text-length f)
   in if slash >= 0 then substring f 0 slash else f

  wat-field-slot : List ATypeDef, Text -> Integer
  wat-field-slot (tds) (f) =
   let len = text-length f
   in let slash = wat-slash-pos f 0 len
   in if slash >= 0 then text-to-integer (substring f (slash + 1) (len - slash - 1))
      else find-field-in-typedefs tds f 0

 A band read by name across every typedef takes the first CLAMPING field of
 that name, so a clamping field in one record clamps a same-named field in
 another that does not declare a band. Where the record's own type is known,
 which is every construction site, the band is read from THAT typedef.

  wat-type-name : CodexType -> Text
  wat-type-name (ty) =
   when ty
    is RecordTy (name) (args) (fields) -> name.value
    is ConstructedTy (name) (args) -> name.value
    is TypeCon (name) -> name.value
    is otherwise -> ""

  wat-band-in-typedef : List ATypeDef, Text, Text -> WatBand
  wat-band-in-typedef (tds) (type-name) (field-name) =
   let i = find-typedef-by-name tds type-name 0
   in if i < 0 then wat-no-band
      else when list-at tds i
       is ARecordTypeDef (n) (tp) (fds) (recmut) (sp) -> wat-band-in-afields fds field-name 0
       is otherwise -> wat-no-band

  wat-band-for-rec : List ATypeDef, CodexType, Text -> WatBand
  wat-band-for-rec (tds) (ty) (field-name) =
   let tn = wat-type-name ty
   in if text-length tn > 0 then wat-band-in-typedef tds tn field-name
      else wat-band-for-field tds field-name 0

  find-afield-index : List ARecordFieldDef, Text, Integer -> Integer
  find-afield-index (fds) (name) (i) =
   if i >= list-length fds then -1
   else if (list-at fds i).name.value == name then i
   else find-afield-index fds name (i + 1)

  emit-wat-field-access : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Text, CodexType, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-field-access (ctx) (rec) (field-name) (result-ty) (depth) =
   let idx = wat-slot-for-expr ctx rec field-name
   in let off = idx * 8
   in "(i64.load (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx rec (depth + 1) & ") (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")))"

  emit-wat-field-store : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Text, IRExpr, CodexType, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-field-store (ctx) (rec) (field-name) (val) (result-ty) (depth) =
   let idx = wat-slot-for-expr ctx rec field-name
   in let off = idx * 8
   in let band = wat-band-for-field (ctx.type-defs) (wat-field-bare field-name) 0
   in "(local.set $_rp " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx rec (depth + 1) & ") " &
      wat-guard-scratch ctx val "$_rp" (emit-wat-expr-at ctx val (depth + 1)) &
      "(i64.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")) " & wat-clamp band "(local.get $_tv)" & ") " &
      "(local.get $_rp)"

  emit-wat-list : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-list (ctx) (elems) (depth) =
   let n = list-length elems
   in let cap = n
   in let sz = 8 + cap * 8
   in let alloc = "(local.set $_lp (i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.const " & integer-to-text sz & "))))"
   in let store-len = "(i32.store (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_lp)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text n & "))"
   in let store-cap = "(i32.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_lp)) (i32.const 4)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text cap & "))"
   in let stores = emit-wat-list-elems ctx elems 0 depth
   in alloc & " " & store-len & " " & store-cap & " " & stores & "(local.get $_lp)"

  emit-wat-list-elems : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-list-elems (ctx) (elems) (i) (depth) =
   emit-wat-list-span ctx elems i (list-length elems) depth

  emit-wat-list-span : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-list-span (ctx) (elems) (lo) (hi) (depth) =
   if lo >= hi then ""
   else if hi - lo == 1 then emit-wat-list-elem ctx elems lo depth
   else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
   in emit-wat-list-span ctx elems lo mid depth & emit-wat-list-span ctx elems mid hi depth

  emit-wat-list-elem : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-list-elem (ctx) (elems) (i) (depth) =
   let off = 8 + i * 8
   in wat-guard-scratch ctx (list-at elems i) "$_lp" (emit-wat-expr-at ctx (list-at elems i) (depth + 1)) &
      "(i64.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_lp)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")) (local.get $_tv)) "

  emit-wat-name : WasmCtx, Text -> Text
 `unreachable` is stack-polymorphic, so a refusal assembles wherever a value
 is expected and traps only if that path actually runs.

 A NAME BOUND IN THIS FUNCTION IS A LOCAL, WHATEVER ELSE THE TREE CALLS IT.
 The arity lookup below turns a name with arity into a funcref table index, so
 a parameter that shares its name with a top-level definition was emitted as
 that definition's index instead of the parameter. `copy-as-chapter-guarded`
 takes a parameter `ch` and the compiler also defines a three-argument `ch`,
 so every `ch.defs` in that function read its fields off table index 3440.
 Locals shadow globals in Codex, so the bound set is consulted first.

  emit-wat-name (ctx) (n) =
   if locals-contains (ctx.bound) (wat-sanitize n) then "(local.get $" & wat-sanitize n & ")"
   else if n == "True" then "(i64.const 1)"
   else if n == "False" then "(i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "Nothing" then "(i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "__heap-save" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (global.get $heap_ptr))"
   else if n == "__deck-pos" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (global.get $deck_ptr))"
   else if n == "__deck-enter" then "(block (result i64) (call $__deck_enter) (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "__deck-exit" then "(block (result i64) (call $__deck_exit) (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "__self-type-defs" then "(call $list_with_capacity (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "read-line" then wat-emit-maybe-line ctx "$read_line_raw"
   else if n == "read-line-cce" then wat-emit-maybe-line ctx "$read_line_cce_raw"
   else if n == "block-sector-count" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.div_u (global.get $disk_size) (i32.const 512)))"
   else if n == "process-get-pid" then "(i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "hosted-kind" then "(i64.const 1)"
   else if is-ctor-name (ctx.type-defs) n 0 then emit-wat-ctor ctx n []
   else let ar = lookup-arity (ctx.arities) n
   in if ar == 0 then "(call $" & wat-sanitize n & ")"
      else if ar > 0 then "(i64.const " & integer-to-text (lookup-fn-index (ctx.arities) n) & ")"
      else wat-refuse-name n

 `~` and `~0` are the x86-64 emitter's semantics, not wasm's: `emit-approx-eq`
 converts each operand to its IEEE-754 total ORDINAL, takes the absolute
 difference and compares it unsigned against a tolerance in ULPs. The two
 tolerances are `4` for `~` and `0` for `~0`, which is `cmp-ri 4` and `cmp-ri 0`
 under `setcc cc-be` in `X86_64.codex`. `f64.eq` would agree with both on equal
 and on far-apart operands and disagree on every value between, which is the
 whole span the operator exists for.

  emit-wat-binary : WasmCtx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, CodexType, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-binary (ctx) (op) (l) (r) (ty) (depth) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt | IrSubInt | IrMulInt ->
     if int-ty-wraps ty then "(" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
     else "(call " & wat-int-trap-fn op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
    is IrAppendText -> "(call $text_append " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
    is IrAppendList -> "(call $list_append " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
    is IrConsList -> "(call $list_cons " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
    is IrEq -> if wat-is-text-type (ir-expr-type l) then "(call $text_eq " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
              else let sn = wat-eq-sum-name (ctx.type-defs) (ir-expr-type l)
              in if text-length sn > 0 then "(call " & wat-eq-fn-prefix & wat-sanitize sn & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
              else "(i64.extend_i32_u (" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))"
    is IrNotEq -> if wat-is-text-type (ir-expr-type l) then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i64.eqz (call $text_eq " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")))"
                 else let sn = wat-eq-sum-name (ctx.type-defs) (ir-expr-type l)
                 in if text-length sn > 0 then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i64.eqz (call " & wat-eq-fn-prefix & wat-sanitize sn & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")))"
                 else "(i64.extend_i32_u (" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))"
    is IrLtVec | IrGtVec | IrLtEqVec | IrGtEqVec -> wat-emit-vec-cmp ctx op l r depth
    is IrLt -> if wat-is-real-type (ir-expr-type l) then "(i64.extend_i32_u (f64.lt " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1)) & " " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1)) & "))"
                         else "(i64.extend_i32_u (" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))"
    is IrGt -> if wat-is-real-type (ir-expr-type l) then "(i64.extend_i32_u (f64.gt " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1)) & " " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1)) & "))"
                         else "(i64.extend_i32_u (" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))"
    is IrLtEq -> if wat-is-real-type (ir-expr-type l) then "(i64.extend_i32_u (f64.le " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1)) & " " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1)) & "))"
                             else "(i64.extend_i32_u (" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))"
    is IrGtEq -> if wat-is-real-type (ir-expr-type l) then "(i64.extend_i32_u (f64.ge " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1)) & " " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1)) & "))"
                             else "(i64.extend_i32_u (" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))"
    is IrAddNum | IrAddRealApprox | IrAddRealTrapping | IrAddRealSaturating | IrSubNum | IrSubRealApprox | IrSubRealTrapping | IrSubRealSaturating | IrMulNum | IrMulRealApprox | IrMulRealTrapping | IrMulRealSaturating | IrDivNum | IrDivRealApprox | IrDivRealTrapping | IrDivRealSaturating ->
     wat-emit-real-arith ctx op l r depth
    is IrAddVec | IrSubVec | IrMulVec | IrDivVec -> wat-emit-vec-arith ctx op l r depth
    is IrPowInt -> "(call $cx_ipow " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"
    is IrApproxEq -> wat-emit-approx-eq ctx l r depth 4
    is IrApproxEqExact -> wat-emit-approx-eq ctx l r depth 0
    is otherwise -> "(" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & ")"

  wat-int-trap-fn : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  wat-int-trap-fn (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt -> "$cx_add_trap"
    is IrSubInt -> "$cx_sub_trap"
    is otherwise -> "$cx_mul_trap"

  wat-bin-instr : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  wat-bin-instr (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddInt -> "i64.add"
    is IrSubInt -> "i64.sub"
    is IrMulInt -> "i64.mul"
    is IrDivInt -> "i64.div_s"
    is IrRemInt -> "i64.rem_s"
    is IrPowInt -> "i64.pow-does-not-exist"
    is IrAddNum | IrAddRealApprox | IrAddRealTrapping | IrAddRealSaturating -> "f64.add"
    is IrSubNum | IrSubRealApprox | IrSubRealTrapping | IrSubRealSaturating -> "f64.sub"
    is IrMulNum | IrMulRealApprox | IrMulRealTrapping | IrMulRealSaturating -> "f64.mul"
    is IrDivNum | IrDivRealApprox | IrDivRealTrapping | IrDivRealSaturating -> "f64.div"
    is IrAddVec -> "f64x2.add"
    is IrSubVec -> "f64x2.sub"
    is IrMulVec -> "f64x2.mul"
    is IrDivVec -> "f64x2.div"
    is IrEq -> "i64.eq"
    is IrNotEq -> "i64.ne"
    is IrLt | IrLtVec -> "i64.lt_s"
    is IrGt | IrGtVec -> "i64.gt_s"
    is IrLtEq | IrLtEqVec -> "i64.le_s"
    is IrGtEq | IrGtEqVec -> "i64.ge_s"
    is IrAnd -> "i64.and"
    is IrOr -> "i64.or"
    is IrAppendText -> "i64.add"
    is IrAppendList -> "i64.add"
    is IrConsList -> "i64.add"
    is IrApproxEq -> "i64.eq"
    is IrApproxEqExact -> "i64.eq"

 A match used to emit its arms NESTED, each one inside the previous arm's
 `else`, so an N-arm match cost N levels of parenthesis in the WAT. That is a
 property of the arm COUNT and not of the program's size, which is why a 9.8 MB
 compiler module sat at depth 188 while a 2.1 MB instruction-dispatch module
 reached 309: measured 2026-08-28, the deepest point in codex-compiler.wat is a
 constructor-tag chain of exactly this shape. wabt's JS wat2wasm parses
 expressions recursively and dies with "memory access out of bounds" somewhere
 between 188 and 309, which held the riscv and arm64 board modules unshippable.
 THE MATCH HAS A TCO TWIN and it is the one the compiler's own deep path takes;
 flattening this one alone moved the depth 188 to 188. Both are flat now, and
 the twin's prose says how the two were told apart.

 The arms are siblings now, one block each under a single outer block, so the
 nesting a match contributes is TWO whatever the arm count. An arm that does
 not match branches to the end of its own block and the next sibling runs; an
 arm that matches branches out of the whole match carrying its value. Falling
 off the end of every arm is the no-match case, which is what the old innermost
 `else` said.

 Every arm takes the same shape, including the ones that always match, which
 carry a constant-true condition rather than a special form. The old code had
 four separate always-matches cases and each of them re-emitted the tail of the
 match, which is how the nesting got in.

  emit-wat-match : WasmCtx, IRExpr, List IRBranch, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-match (ctx) (scrut) (branches) (depth) =
   if list-length branches == 0 then "(unreachable)"
   else "(local.set " & wat-scrut ctx & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx scrut & ") (block $_m (result i64) "
        & emit-wat-match-body ctx branches 0 depth & "(unreachable))"

  emit-wat-match-body : WasmCtx, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-match-body (ctx) (branches) (i) (depth) =
   if i >= list-length branches then ""
   else let b = list-at branches i
   in let arm = emit-wat-match-arm ctx (b.pattern) (b.guard) (b.body) branches i depth
   in arm & emit-wat-match-body ctx branches (i + 1) depth

 The label is plain rather than numbered, and a match nested inside an arm's
 body shadows it. That is the wanted reading: an inner match's branch belongs
 to the inner match. The branch out sits in the arm block and not inside the
 body, so the body's own blocks have closed before the label is resolved.

 PRE runs before the condition and POST after it, and the split is load-bearing
 rather than tidy. A variable pattern must bind BEFORE its guard is tested,
 because the guard names what the pattern bound. A constructor pattern must NOT
 bind before its tag is tested, because a payload load on an object of the
 wrong tag can address past the object and trap, which is why its guard stays
 gated inside the condition rather than anded flat with it.

  emit-wat-arm-block : Text, Text, Text, Text -> Text
  emit-wat-arm-block (pre) (cond) (post) (body) =
   "(block $_a " & pre & "(br_if $_a (i32.eqz " & cond & ")) " & post
     & "(br $_m " & body & ")) "

 A hex literal in a PATTERN reaches here as its SPELLING, and Codex spells hex
 with a leading #, which wat2wasm refuses as "unexpected token #A9". wat takes
 0x, so the prefix is rewritten; a decimal spelling carries no # and is left
 alone, and a sign survives because only the prefix moves. The expression side
 never needed this: an IrIntLit carries a value, not a spelling.

  wat-lit-pat-const : Text -> Text
  wat-lit-pat-const (t) =
   if t == "True" then "1"
   else if t == "False" then "0"
   else text-replace t "#" "0x"

 A Text literal pattern is the one literal whose SPELLING is not its value. The
 scrutinee is a pointer, so the test is `$text_eq` against the string table
 entry, the same place an `IrTextLit` expression reads. Splicing the spelling
 into an `i64.const` is what `wat2wasm` refused as `unexpected token "sin"`.

  wat-lit-pat-test : WasmCtx, Text, CodexType -> Text
  wat-lit-pat-test (ctx) (text) (ty) =
   if wat-is-text-type ty then
    "(i32.wrap_i64 (call $text_eq (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ") " & wat-strtab-ref (ctx.strings) text & "))"
   else "(i64.eq (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ") (i64.const " & wat-lit-pat-const text & "))"

  wat-guard-trivial : IRExpr -> Boolean
  wat-guard-trivial (g) =
   when g
    is IrBoolLit (v) (sp) -> v
    is otherwise -> False

  emit-wat-guard-test : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  emit-wat-guard-test (ctx) (guard) = "(i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr (ctx-deeper ctx) guard & ")"

  wat-arm-cond : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  wat-arm-cond (ctx) (guard) =
   if wat-guard-trivial guard then "(i32.const 1)" else emit-wat-guard-test ctx guard

  emit-wat-match-arm : WasmCtx, IRPat, IRExpr, IRExpr, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-match-arm (ctx) (pat) (guard) (body) (branches) (i) (depth) =
   when pat
    is IrLitPat (text) (ty) (sp) ->
     let lit-test = wat-lit-pat-test ctx text ty
     in let cond = if wat-guard-trivial guard then lit-test
        else "(i32.and " & lit-test & " " & emit-wat-guard-test ctx guard & ")"
     in emit-wat-arm-block "" cond "" (emit-wat-expr ctx body)
    is IrCtorPat (name) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-ctor-pat ctx name subs guard body branches i depth
    is IrVarPat (name) (ty) (sp) ->
     let bind = "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ")) "
     in emit-wat-arm-block bind (wat-arm-cond ctx guard) "" (emit-wat-expr ctx body)
    is IrWildPat (sp) ->
     emit-wat-arm-block "" (wat-arm-cond ctx guard) "" (emit-wat-expr ctx body)
    is IrVecPat (pats) (ty) (sp) ->
     let vcond = wat-emit-vec-pat-cond ctx pats ty
     in let cond = if wat-guard-trivial guard then vcond
        else "(i32.and " & vcond & " " & emit-wat-guard-test ctx guard & ")"
     in emit-wat-arm-block "" cond "" (emit-wat-expr ctx body)

 A payload load on an object of the wrong tag can address past the object,
 which traps. The tag test gates the guard inside the condition rather than
 the two being anded flat.

  emit-wat-ctor-pat : WasmCtx, Text, List IRPat, IRExpr, IRExpr, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-ctor-pat (ctx) (name) (subs) (guard) (body) (branches) (i) (depth) =
   let tag = find-ctor-in-typedefs (ctx.type-defs) name 0
   in let bind-subs = emit-wat-bind-ctor-subs ctx subs 0
   in if (i == list-length branches - 1) & wat-guard-trivial guard & (wat-ctor-has-lit-subs subs 0 == False) then
    emit-wat-arm-block "" "(i32.const 1)" bind-subs (emit-wat-expr ctx body)
   else emit-wat-arm-block "" (wat-ctor-arm-cond ctx tag subs guard bind-subs) bind-subs (emit-wat-expr ctx body)

 A LITERAL inside a constructor pattern is a TEST, not a binding, and
 `emit-wat-bind-ctor-subs` had no arm for one: it bound a variable sub-pattern
 and sent everything else to `is otherwise`, so `BInt (0)` matched every `BInt`
 and every arm answered the first one's body. The tests fold onto the tag test
 rather than beside it, because a payload load on an object of the wrong tag can
 address past the object and trap, which is the reason the guard is already
 gated this way.

  wat-ctor-has-lit-subs : List IRPat, Integer -> Boolean
  wat-ctor-has-lit-subs (subs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length subs then False
   else when list-at subs i
    is IrLitPat (t) (ty) (sp) -> True
    is otherwise -> wat-ctor-has-lit-subs subs (i + 1)

  wat-and-tests : Text, Text -> Text
  wat-and-tests (acc) (t) =
   if text-length acc == 0 then t else "(i32.and " & acc & " " & t & ")"

  wat-ctor-sub-tests : WasmCtx, List IRPat, Integer, Text -> Text
  wat-ctor-sub-tests (ctx) (subs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length subs then acc
   else let fld = "(i64.load (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ")) (i32.const " & integer-to-text (8 + i * 8) & ")))"
   in when list-at subs i
    is IrLitPat (t) (ty) (sp) ->
     wat-ctor-sub-tests ctx subs (i + 1) (wat-and-tests acc
      (if wat-is-text-type ty
       then "(i32.wrap_i64 (call $text_eq " & fld & " " & wat-strtab-ref (ctx.strings) t & "))"
       else "(i64.eq " & fld & " (i64.const " & wat-lit-pat-const t & "))"))
    is otherwise -> wat-ctor-sub-tests ctx subs (i + 1) acc

  wat-ctor-arm-cond : WasmCtx, Integer, List IRPat, IRExpr, Text -> Text
  wat-ctor-arm-cond (ctx) (tag) (subs) (guard) (bind-subs) =
   let tag-test = "(i64.eq (i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & "))) (i64.const " & integer-to-text tag & "))"
   in let subt = wat-ctor-sub-tests ctx subs 0 ""
   in let inner = if wat-guard-trivial guard then subt
      else wat-and-tests subt (emit-wat-guard-test ctx guard)
   in if text-length inner == 0 then tag-test
      else "(if (result i32) " & tag-test & " (then " & bind-subs & inner & ") (else (i32.const 0)))"

  emit-wat-bind-ctor-subs : WasmCtx, List IRPat, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-bind-ctor-subs (ctx) (subs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length subs then ""
   else let off = 8 + i * 8
   in let sub = list-at subs i
   in (when sub
    is IrVarPat (name) (ty) (sp) -> "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " (i64.load (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ")) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")))) "
    is IrWildPat (sp) -> ""
    is IrVecPat (pats) (ty) (sp) -> ""
    is otherwise -> "") & emit-wat-bind-ctor-subs ctx subs (i + 1)

  emit-wat-act : WasmCtx, List IRActStmt -> Text
  emit-wat-act (ctx) (stmts) =
   emit-wat-act-stmts ctx stmts 0

  emit-wat-act-stmts : WasmCtx, List IRActStmt, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-act-stmts (ctx) (stmts) (i) =
   if i == list-length stmts then "(i64.const 0)"
   else let s = list-at stmts i
   in let is-last = i == list-length stmts - 1
   in (when s
    is IrDoBind (name) (ty) (val) (sp) -> "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx val & ") "
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> if is-last then emit-wat-expr ctx e else "(drop " & emit-wat-expr ctx e & ") ") & (if is-last then "" else emit-wat-act-stmts ctx stmts (i + 1))

Section: Apply Emission

  emit-wat-apply : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  emit-wat-apply (ctx) (e) =
   let chain = collect-apply-chain e []
   in let root = chain.root
   in let args = chain.args
   in when root
    is IrName (n) (ty) (sp) ->
     let bi = wat-try-builtin ctx n args
     in if text-length bi > 0 then bi
     else if is-ctor-name (ctx.type-defs) n 0 then emit-wat-ctor ctx n args
     else if lookup-arity (ctx.arities) n < 0 then
      (if locals-contains (ctx.bound) (wat-sanitize n)
       then wat-emit-indirect ctx args ("(local.get $" & wat-sanitize n & ")")
       else wat-refuse-name n)
     else wat-emit-named-apply ctx n args (lookup-arity (ctx.arities) n)
    is IrLambda (ps) (lbody) (lty) (lsp) -> emit-wat-lambda-apply ctx ps lbody args
    is otherwise -> wat-emit-indirect ctx args (emit-wat-expr ctx root)

 The callee is an index into `$ft`, so it narrows to i32 at the call. A name
 the arity map does not know is a LOCAL holding a function value, and that
 arm used to emit `emit-wat-apply-args` alone: the arguments were evaluated
 and the call was dropped, so `map-list f xs` silently became `xs`. Nothing
 downstream ran this plug's output, which is why a missing call never
 surfaced as a wrong answer.

 Applying a VALUE goes one argument at a time through `$clo_apply1`, which is
 what makes a short application a closure rather than a call that never
 happens. The old arm emitted one `call_indirect` over the whole argument
 list, which is right only when the application saturates and the runtime
 cannot know whether it does.

  wat-emit-indirect : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Text -> Text
  wat-emit-indirect (ctx) (args) (callee) =
   let n = list-length args
   in if n >= 2 & n <= wat-fn-type-ceiling (ctx.arities)
      then "(call $clo_apply" & integer-to-text n & " " & callee & wat-emit-indirect-args ctx args 0 & ")"
   else wat-emit-apply-chain ctx args 0 callee

  wat-emit-indirect-args : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-indirect-args (ctx) (args) (i) =
   if i >= list-length args then ""
   else " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args i) & wat-emit-indirect-args ctx args (i + 1)

  wat-emit-apply-chain : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer, Text -> Text
  wat-emit-apply-chain (ctx) (args) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length args then acc
   else let step = "(call $clo_apply1 " & acc & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args i) & ")"
   in wat-emit-apply-chain ctx args (i + 1) step

 A name whose arity the map knows takes the direct call when the application
 SATURATES, which is the ordinary case and the hot one. Short of that it
 builds a closure; past it, the saturating prefix is called and the surplus
 applied to whatever comes back.

  wat-emit-named-apply : WasmCtx, Text, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-named-apply (ctx) (n) (args) (ar) =
   let k = list-length args
   in if k == ar then "(call $" & wat-sanitize n & " " & emit-wat-apply-args ctx args 0 & ")"
   else if k < ar then
    let seed = "(i64.const " & integer-to-text (lookup-fn-index (ctx.arities) n) & ")"
    in wat-emit-apply-chain ctx args 0 seed
   else
    let sat = "(call $" & wat-sanitize n & " " & emit-wat-apply-args ctx (list-take args ar) 0 & ")"
    in wat-emit-apply-chain ctx (list-drop args ar) 0 sat


 WebAssembly has no closures and no way to invoke a lambda where it stands, and
 the wire applies one at its definition site. The old arm emitted
 `call_indirect` over the arguments and then spliced the BODY beside them as
 another operand, so `lam_two` read `local.get $x` for a local nothing declares
 and the module would not assemble.

 Every parameter is bound into a local of its own name and the body follows.
 That is legal because this emitter already resolves an `IrName` to
 `local.get $name`, so the body needs no rewriting; the locals are declared by
 `collect-locals-expr`, which now walks a lambda's parameters as well as its
 body. Binding rather than substituting is what stops an argument that is a
 CALL from being evaluated once per use.

 A lambda applied to FEWER arguments than it declares is a partial application
 and has no wasm form; it refuses rather than emitting a body whose remaining
 parameters are unset.

  emit-wat-lambda-apply : WasmCtx, List IRParam, IRExpr, List IRExpr -> Text
  emit-wat-lambda-apply (ctx) (ps) (lbody) (args) =
   if list-length args < list-length ps
   then "(unreachable (; wasm plug: partial application of a lambda has no form ;))"
   else emit-wat-lambda-binds ctx ps args 0 & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx lbody

  emit-wat-lambda-binds : WasmCtx, List IRParam, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-lambda-binds (ctx) (ps) (args) (i) =
   if i >= list-length ps then ""
   else "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize ((list-at ps i).name) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args i) & ") "
        & emit-wat-lambda-binds ctx ps args (i + 1)

 The byte loop is in the runtime and the `Maybe` wrapping is here, because a
 constructor's tag comes from the type-definition order and a fixed runtime
 string cannot know it. `read-line-cce` shares this arm: bare metal reads
 CCE off the wire without converting, and this host's bytes arrive as ASCII,
 where the two readings are the same byte.

  wat-emit-maybe-line : WasmCtx, Text -> Text
  wat-emit-maybe-line (ctx) (reader) =
   let just-tag = find-ctor-in-typedefs (ctx.type-defs) "Just" 0
   in let none-tag = find-ctor-in-typedefs (ctx.type-defs) "None" 0
   in "(local.set $_tv (call " & reader & ")) " &
      "(if (result i64) (i64.eqz (local.get $_tv)) " &
      "(then (local.set $_rp (i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.const 8)))) " &
      "(i64.store (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i64.const " & integer-to-text none-tag & ")) " &
      "(local.get $_rp)) " &
      "(else (local.set $_rp (i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.const 16)))) " &
      "(i64.store (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i64.const " & integer-to-text just-tag & ")) " &
      "(i64.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i32.const 8)) (local.get $_tv)) " &
      "(local.get $_rp)))"

  emit-wat-ctor : WasmCtx, Text, List IRExpr -> Text
  emit-wat-ctor (ctx) (name) (args) =
   let tag = find-ctor-in-typedefs (ctx.type-defs) name 0
   in let fc = list-length args
   in let sz = 8 + fc * 8
   in let alloc = "(local.set $_rp (i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.const " & integer-to-text sz & "))))"
   in let store-tag = "(i64.store (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i64.const " & integer-to-text tag & "))"
   in let stores = emit-wat-ctor-fields ctx args 0
   in alloc & " " & store-tag & " " & stores & "(local.get $_rp)"

  emit-wat-ctor-fields : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-ctor-fields (ctx) (args) (i) =
   if i >= list-length args then ""
   else let off = 8 + i * 8
   in wat-guard-scratch ctx (list-at args i) "$_rp" (emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args i)) &
      "(i64.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")) (local.get $_tv)) " & emit-wat-ctor-fields ctx args (i + 1)

  emit-wat-apply-args : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-apply-args (ctx) (args) (i) =
   if i == list-length args then ""
   else emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args i) & " " & emit-wat-apply-args ctx args (i + 1)

  wat-is-text-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  wat-is-text-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is TextTy -> True
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> wat-is-text-type inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (ret) -> wat-is-text-type ret
    is otherwise -> False

 A real is carried as its f64 BITS in an i64 slot, which is what every local
 declaration and `wat-eq-field-cmp` already assume. It reaches an f64 only
 inside the operator that needs one, and comes straight back. `IrNumLit` used
 to emit an f64 VALUE instead, so a real that passed through a local was a type
 error rather than a wrong answer: `let y = 2.25` was refused outright with
 `type mismatch in local.set, expected [i64] but got [f64]`, and real
 arithmetic worked only while every operand was a literal.

 AN APPROXIMATE REAL IS COMPUTED AT f32 AND NOT AT f64, which is what makes an
 overflow overflow. The value is carried as a promoted f64 everywhere else here,
 so `f64.add` on two singles answers a finite double where the single arithmetic
 x86-64 emits (`real-sse-prefix f32`) answers an infinity, and the saturating
 clamp then has nothing to fire on. `real-approx-modes` distinguishes the two by
 one bit: 2139095039 is the largest finite single, 2139095040 is `+inf`.

 The mode wrapper is `emit-real-trapping-arith` and `emit-real-saturating-arith`
 (`X86_64.codex:1887`) read as masks rather than as shifts. Exponent all-ones
 means infinity or NaN; a nonzero mantissa within that means NaN. Trapping traps
 on either. Saturating answers 0 for a NaN and DECREMENTS the bit pattern for an
 infinity, which is the largest finite magnitude of the same sign because the
 decrement never reaches the sign bit. The NaN-to-zero is x86-64's answer and is
 a language decision rather than a repair, so it is copied rather than revisited.

 A ULP is a property of the WIDTH, so the tolerance has to be counted in the
 operand's own representation. An approximate value is carried here as a
 promoted f64, and the two smallest denormals either side of zero are 2 ULPs
 apart as singles and astronomically far apart as doubles, which is the line
 `real-approx-equality` grades.

 A VECTOR IS A POINTER TO A 16-BYTE BOX, which is the language's own contract
 rather than a choice made here: `DevelopersGuide.md` measures every
 vector-returning builtin at `fixed` 16 bytes and `vec-load-at` as 16 bytes at
 an address. One box is exactly one `v128`, so the lanes are the shape and the
 CARRIER stays i64 like every other pointer, which is why no local declaration
 changes. The emitter used to write `f64x2.add` straight onto the two i64
 operands, which `wat2wasm` refuses as a type mismatch, and `vec-splat` had no
 form at all.

 The shape is `16 / lanes` bytes each, and the lane count is in the NAME
 (`vec-` is 2, `vec4-` is 4) for a splat, whose argument cannot carry it, and in
 the operand's `VectorTy` everywhere else. A shape this does not name refuses
 rather than guessing, and so does integer division: wasm SIMD has no integer
 divide at any width, so `i64x2.div` does not exist to emit.

  wat-vec-shape : Integer, CodexType -> Text
  wat-vec-shape (lanes) (elem) =
   if wat-is-real-type elem then
    (if lanes == 2 then "f64x2" else if lanes == 4 then "f32x4" else "")
   else if wat-is-integer-elem elem then
    (if lanes == 2 then "i64x2" else if lanes == 4 then "i32x4" else "")
   else ""

  wat-is-integer-elem : CodexType -> Boolean
  wat-is-integer-elem (ty) =
   when ty
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (mode) -> True
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> wat-is-integer-elem inner
    is otherwise -> False

  wat-vec-of-type : CodexType -> Text
  wat-vec-of-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is VectorTy (n) (elem) -> wat-vec-shape n elem
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> wat-vec-of-type inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (ret) -> wat-vec-of-type ret
    is otherwise -> ""

  wat-vec-lane-bytes : Text -> Integer
  wat-vec-lane-bytes (shape) = if shape == "f64x2" | shape == "i64x2" then 8 else 4

  wat-vec-refuse : Text -> Text
  wat-vec-refuse (why) = "(unreachable (; wasm plug: " & why & " ;))"

  wat-emit-vec-splat : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-splat (ctx) (arg) (lanes) =
   let shape = wat-vec-shape lanes (ir-expr-type arg)
   in let v = emit-wat-expr ctx arg
   in if shape == "f64x2" then "(call $cx_vec_box (f64x2.splat (f64.reinterpret_i64 " & v & ")))"
      else if shape == "f32x4" then "(call $cx_vec_box (f32x4.splat (f32.demote_f64 (f64.reinterpret_i64 " & v & "))))"
      else if shape == "i64x2" then "(call $cx_vec_box (i64x2.splat " & v & "))"
      else if shape == "i32x4" then "(call $cx_vec_box (i32x4.splat (i32.wrap_i64 " & v & ")))"
      else wat-vec-refuse ("no vector shape for a splat of " & integer-to-text lanes & " lanes")

  wat-emit-vec-extract : WasmCtx, IRExpr, IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-extract (ctx) (vec) (idx) =
   let shape = wat-vec-of-type (ir-expr-type vec)
   in if text-length shape == 0 then wat-vec-refuse "no vector shape for an extract"
   else let at = "(i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx vec & ") (i32.mul (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx idx & ") (i32.const " & integer-to-text (wat-vec-lane-bytes shape) & ")))"
   in if shape == "f64x2" | shape == "i64x2" then "(i64.load " & at & ")"
      else if shape == "f32x4" then "(i64.reinterpret_f64 (f64.promote_f32 (f32.load " & at & ")))"
      else "(i64.extend_i32_s (i32.load " & at & "))"

  wat-emit-vec-arith : WasmCtx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-arith (ctx) (op) (l) (r) (depth) =
   let shape = wat-vec-of-type (ir-expr-type l)
   in let isdiv = when op is IrDivVec -> True is otherwise -> False
   in if text-length shape == 0 then wat-vec-refuse "no vector shape for this arithmetic"
   else if isdiv & (shape == "i64x2" | shape == "i32x4") then
    wat-vec-refuse "wasm SIMD has no integer division, so a vector integer divide has no form"
   else "(call $cx_vec_box (" & shape & wat-vec-op-suffix op & " (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & ")) (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))))"

 A VECTOR PATTERN was not tested either, the same shape as a literal inside a
 constructor pattern: `IrVecPat` bound nothing and tested nothing, so
 `is Vector [0, 0]` matched every vector and `vec-pattern` answered `zero` three
 times. The lanes are compared against their literals out of the box. A float
 shape refuses rather than comparing: a real literal's SPELLING is not an
 `i64.const`, and nothing in the corpus writes one.

  wat-emit-vec-pat-cond : WasmCtx, List IRPat, CodexType -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-pat-cond (ctx) (pats) (ty) =
   let lanes = list-length pats
   in if lanes == 2 then wat-vec-pat-lanes ctx 8 pats 0 ""
      else if lanes == 4 then wat-vec-pat-lanes ctx 4 pats 0 ""
      else wat-vec-refuse ("no vector pattern form for " & integer-to-text lanes & " lanes")

  wat-vec-pat-lanes : WasmCtx, Integer, List IRPat, Integer, Text -> Text
  wat-vec-pat-lanes (ctx) (lb) (pats) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length pats then (if text-length acc == 0 then "(i32.const 1)" else acc)
   else let at = "(i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ")) (i32.const " & integer-to-text (i * lb) & "))"
   in let load = if lb == 8 then "(i64.load " & at & ")" else "(i64.extend_i32_s (i32.load " & at & "))"
   in when list-at pats i
    is IrLitPat (t) (pty) (sp) ->
     wat-vec-pat-lanes ctx lb pats (i + 1) (wat-and-tests acc ("(i64.eq " & load & " (i64.const " & wat-lit-pat-const t & "))"))
    is otherwise -> wat-vec-pat-lanes ctx lb pats (i + 1) acc

  wat-emit-vec-named-arith : WasmCtx, Text, List IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-named-arith (ctx) (suffix) (args) =
   let l = list-at args 0
   in let shape = wat-vec-of-type (ir-expr-type l)
   in if text-length shape == 0 then wat-vec-refuse "no vector shape for this arithmetic"
   else if suffix == ".div" & (shape == "i64x2" | shape == "i32x4") then
    wat-vec-refuse "wasm SIMD has no integer division, so a vector integer divide has no form"
   else "(call $cx_vec_box (" & shape & suffix & " (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx l & ")) (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & "))))"

  wat-emit-vec-reduce : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-reduce (ctx) (vec) =
   let shape = wat-vec-of-type (ir-expr-type vec)
   in if text-length shape == 0 then wat-vec-refuse "no vector shape for a reduction"
      else "(call $cx_vec_sum_" & shape & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx vec & ")"

  wat-vec-op-suffix : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  wat-vec-op-suffix (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddVec -> ".add"
    is IrSubVec -> ".sub"
    is IrMulVec -> ".mul"
    is otherwise -> ".div"

 A lane comparison answers all-ones or all-zeros in every lane, which is the
 same 16-byte box a vector is, so a mask needs no representation of its own.
 The integer shapes spell their comparisons `_s`; the float ones do not.

  wat-emit-vec-cmp : WasmCtx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-cmp (ctx) (op) (l) (r) (depth) =
   let shape = wat-vec-of-type (ir-expr-type l)
   in if text-length shape == 0 then wat-vec-refuse "no vector shape for this comparison"
   else let signed = if shape == "i64x2" | shape == "i32x4" then "_s" else ""
   in "(call $cx_vec_box (" & shape & wat-vec-cmp-suffix op & signed & " (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1) & ")) (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1) & "))))"

  wat-vec-cmp-suffix : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  wat-vec-cmp-suffix (op) =
   when op
    is IrLtVec -> ".lt"
    is IrGtVec -> ".gt"
    is IrLtEqVec -> ".le"
    is otherwise -> ".ge"

 Every mask builtin is declared over `VectorMask 2` and there is no other width
 in the language, so the query is `i64x2.bitmask` with no type consulted: two
 lanes, one bit each, all-set being 3.

  wat-emit-mask-query : WasmCtx, Text, IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-mask-query (ctx) (n) (m) =
   let bits = "(call $cx_mask_bits " & emit-wat-expr ctx m & ")"
   in if n == "mask-any" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.ne " & bits & " (i32.const 0)))"
      else if n == "mask-all" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.eq " & bits & " (i32.const 3)))"
      else if n == "mask-none" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.eqz " & bits & "))"
      else "(call $cx_mask_count " & emit-wat-expr ctx m & ")"

  wat-emit-vec-select : WasmCtx, List IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-vec-select (ctx) (args) =
   "(call $cx_vec_box (v128.bitselect (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")) (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")) (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & "))))"

  wat-emit-approx-eq : WasmCtx, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-approx-eq (ctx) (l) (r) (depth) (tol) =
   let a = emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1)
   in let b = emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1)
   in if wat-is-real-f32 (ir-expr-type l)
      then "(call $__approx_eq32 " & a & " " & b & " (i32.const " & integer-to-text tol & "))"
      else "(call $__approx_eq " & a & " " & b & " (i64.const " & integer-to-text tol & "))"

  wat-is-real-f32 : CodexType -> Boolean
  wat-is-real-f32 (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> when w is RwF32 -> True is otherwise -> False
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> wat-is-real-f32 inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (ret) -> wat-is-real-f32 ret
    is otherwise -> False

  wat-f32-instr : IRBinaryOp -> Text
  wat-f32-instr (op) =
   when op
    is IrAddNum | IrAddRealApprox | IrAddRealTrapping | IrAddRealSaturating -> "f32.add"
    is IrSubNum | IrSubRealApprox | IrSubRealTrapping | IrSubRealSaturating -> "f32.sub"
    is IrMulNum | IrMulRealApprox | IrMulRealTrapping | IrMulRealSaturating -> "f32.mul"
    is otherwise -> "f32.div"

  wat-real-mode-fn : IRBinaryOp, Boolean -> Text
  wat-real-mode-fn (op) (f32) =
   when op
    is IrAddRealTrapping | IrSubRealTrapping | IrMulRealTrapping | IrDivRealTrapping ->
     if f32 then "$cx_real_trap32" else "$cx_real_trap64"
    is IrAddRealSaturating | IrSubRealSaturating | IrMulRealSaturating | IrDivRealSaturating ->
     if f32 then "$cx_real_sat32" else "$cx_real_sat64"
    is otherwise -> ""

  wat-emit-real-arith : WasmCtx, IRBinaryOp, IRExpr, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  wat-emit-real-arith (ctx) (op) (l) (r) (depth) =
   let f32 = wat-is-real-f32 (ir-expr-type l)
   in let lf = wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx l (depth + 1))
   in let rf = wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr-at ctx r (depth + 1))
   in let raw = if f32
      then "(f64.promote_f32 (" & wat-f32-instr op & " (f32.demote_f64 " & lf & ") (f32.demote_f64 " & rf & ")))"
      else "(" & wat-bin-instr op & " " & lf & " " & rf & ")"
   in let mode-fn = wat-real-mode-fn op f32
   in if text-length mode-fn == 0 then wat-f64-out raw
      else "(call " & mode-fn & " " & wat-f64-out raw & ")"

  wat-is-real-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  wat-is-real-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is RealTy (w) (m) -> True
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> wat-is-real-type inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (ret) -> wat-is-real-type ret
    is otherwise -> False

  wat-f64-in : Text -> Text
  wat-f64-in (x) = "(f64.reinterpret_i64 " & x & ")"

  wat-f64-out : Text -> Text
  wat-f64-out (x) = "(i64.reinterpret_f64 " & x & ")"

  wat-emit-print-line : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-print-line (ctx) (arg) =
   let ty = ir-expr-type arg
   in if wat-is-text-type ty then "(call $wasi_print_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx arg & ") (call $wasi_print_newline) (i64.const 0)"
   else "(call $wasi_print_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx arg & ") (call $wasi_print_newline) (i64.const 0)"

 `__record-set` names its field with a string, so the slot has to be resolved
 from the RECORD'S OWN type. Resolving it from the name alone searches every
 type-def and stops at the first match, which is a different record whenever
 two of them share a field name. `ParseResult.parse-bag` is slot 4 and
 `Document.parse-bag` is slot 14, so setting a Document's parse-bag wrote slot
 4, which is `Document.instance-defs`. The name-only lookup stays as the
 fallback for a wire whose recorded type did not resolve, because that is what
 it was doing before and it is right whenever the name is unique.

  wat-slot-in-ty : WasmCtx, CodexType, Text, Integer -> Integer
  wat-slot-in-ty (ctx) (ty) (field-name) (fallback) =
   let fs = wat-fields-of-ty ctx ty
   in if list-length fs == 0 then fallback
      else let k = find-record-field-index fs field-name 0
      in if (list-at fs k).name.value == field-name then k else fallback

  wat-slot-for-expr : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Text -> Integer
  wat-slot-for-expr (ctx) (rec) (field-name) =
   let named = wat-field-slot (ctx.type-defs) field-name
   in wat-slot-in-ty ctx (ir-expr-type rec) (wat-field-bare field-name) named

  wat-fields-of-ty : WasmCtx, CodexType -> List RecordField
  wat-fields-of-ty (ctx) (ty) =
   when ty
    is RecordTy (name) (args) (fields) ->
     if list-length fields > 0 then fields
     else record-fields-by-name (ctx.type-defs) (name.value) 0
    is ConstructedTy (name) (args) -> resolve-record-fields (ctx.type-defs) (name.value) 0
    is TypeCon (name) -> resolve-record-fields (ctx.type-defs) (name.value) 0
    is otherwise -> []

  wat-emit-record-set : WasmCtx, List IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-record-set (ctx) (args) =
   let rec-expr = list-at args 0
   in let field-expr = list-at args 1
   in let val-expr = list-at args 2
   in let field-name = when field-expr
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> s
    is otherwise -> ""
   in let rec-ty = ir-expr-type rec-expr
   in let idx = wat-slot-for-expr ctx rec-expr field-name
   in let off = idx * 8
   in "(local.set $_rp " & emit-wat-expr ctx rec-expr & ") " &
      wat-guard-scratch ctx val-expr "$_rp" (emit-wat-expr ctx val-expr) &
      "(i64.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_rp)) (i32.const " & integer-to-text off & ")) (local.get $_tv)) " &
      "(local.get $_rp)"

  wat-emit-show : WasmCtx, IRExpr -> Text
  wat-emit-show (ctx) (arg) =
   let ty = ir-expr-type arg
   in if wat-is-text-type ty then emit-wat-expr ctx arg
   else if wat-is-boolean-type ty then "(call $bool_to_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx arg & ")"
   else if wat-is-real-type ty then "(call $__f64_to_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx arg & ")"
   else "(call $i64_to_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx arg & ")"

  wat-is-boolean-type : CodexType -> Boolean
  wat-is-boolean-type (ty) =
   when ty
    is BooleanTy -> True
    is UnitTy (n) (inner) -> wat-is-boolean-type inner
    is EffectfulTy (effs) (scopes) (ret) -> wat-is-boolean-type ret
    is otherwise -> False

 `peek` and `poke` address as base plus offset, which is the pair the x86
 arms take, so the sum is built once here rather than at five call sites.

  wat-addr-of : WasmCtx, List IRExpr -> Text
  wat-addr-of (ctx) (args) =
   "(i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & "))"

 These name hardware and a host filesystem. A wasm module has neither, and
 there is no approximation of a disk sector or an I/O port that is better
 than refusing: `unreachable` is stack-polymorphic, so each assembles where a
 value is expected and traps only if that path runs. Emitting something
 plausible instead is this quire's first standing hazard.

  wat-refuse-name : Text -> Text
  wat-refuse-name (n) =
   "(unreachable) (; no wasm form for " & n & " ;)"

  wat-no-such-thing : Text -> Boolean
  wat-no-such-thing (n) =
   n == "port-out-byte" |
   n == "port-in-byte" |
   n == "prof-dump" |
   n == "prof-start"

  wat-try-builtin : WasmCtx, Text, List IRExpr -> Text
  wat-try-builtin (ctx) (n) (args) =
   if n == "__heap-restore" then "(block (result i64) (global.set $heap_ptr (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")) (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "peek-byte" then "(i64.load8_u " & wat-addr-of ctx args & ")"
   else if n == "peek-qword" then "(i64.load " & wat-addr-of ctx args & ")"
   else if n == "poke-byte" then "(i64.store8 " & wat-addr-of ctx args & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "poke-16" then "(i64.store16 " & wat-addr-of ctx args & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "address-of" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "__narrow" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "variant-tag" then "(i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & "))"
   else if n == "min" then "(call $cx_min " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "__memset" then "(call $cx_memset " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "__heap-advance" then "(block (result i64) (drop (call $bump_alloc (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & "))) (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "__deck-set" then "(block (result i64) (global.set $deck_ptr (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")) (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "__deck-alloc" then "(block (result i64) (call $__deck_enter) (i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & "))) (call $__deck_exit))"
   else if n == "deck-record" & list-length args == 1 then "(block (result i64) (call $__deck_enter) " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " (call $__deck_exit))"
   else if n == "alloc-bytes" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (call $bump_alloc (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")))"
   else if n == "print-text" then "(call $wasi_print_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "print-uni" then "(call $wasi_print_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "write-binary" then "(call $write_binary " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "write-binary-buf" then "(call $write_binary_buf " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "char-code-at" then "(i64.load8_u (i32.add (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i32.const 4)) (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")))"
   else if n == "block-read-sector" then "(call $block_read_sector " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "block-write-sector" then "(call $block_write_sector " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "process-get-scope" | n == "process-get-network-scope" then "(call $process_scope_empty " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if wat-no-such-thing n then "(unreachable (; wasm plug: " & n & " has no form on this target ;))"
   else if n == "print-line-uni" then wat-emit-print-line ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "print-line" then wat-emit-print-line ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "print-error-uni" | n == "print-error" then wat-emit-print-line ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "print-line-raw" then "(call $cx_print_text_raw " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "abs" then "(call $cx_abs " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "max" then "(call $cx_max " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "show" then wat-emit-show ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "integer-to-text" then "(call $i64_to_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "text-to-integer" then "(call $cx_text_to_integer " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "text-to-double-bits" then "(call $text_to_double " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "raw-bytes-to-text" then "(call $raw_bytes_to_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "read-serial-cce" then "(call $read_serial_cce " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "read-file-uni" then "(call $read_file_uni " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "read-file-raw" then "(call $read_file_raw " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "text-starts-with" then "(call $cx_text_starts_with " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "text-contains" then "(call $cx_text_contains " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "text-replace" then "(call $cx_text_replace " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "text-split" then "(call $cx_text_split " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "text-length" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")))"
   else if n == "char-at" then "(call $char_at " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "char-to-text" then "(call $char_to_text " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "char-encode" then "(call $char_encode " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "substring" then "(call $substring " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "list-length" | n == "__list-len" then "(call $list_length " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "list-at" then "(call $list_at " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "list-push" | n == "list-snoc" then "(call $list_push " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "char-code" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "code-to-char" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "__list-head" then "(call $list_at " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " (i64.const 0))"
   else if n == "__list-tail" then "(call $cx_list_tail " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "is-digit" then "(call $__is_digit " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "is-letter" then "(call $__is_letter " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "is-whitespace" then "(call $__is_whitespace " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "negate" then if wat-is-real-type (ir-expr-type (list-at args 0)) then wat-f64-out ("(f64.neg " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)) & ")")
                              else "(i64.sub (i64.const 0) " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "int-mod" then "(call $codex_mod " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-and" then "(i64.and " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-or" then "(i64.or " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-xor" then "(i64.xor " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-shl" then "(i64.shl " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-shr" then "(i64.shr_s " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-shru" then "(i64.shr_u " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "bit-not" then "(i64.xor " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " (i64.const -1))"
   else if n == "process-exit" then "(unreachable)"
   else if n == "text-compare" then "(call $__text_compare " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "real-to-bits" | n == "bits-to-real" | n == "from-real-approx" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "to-real-trapping" | n == "from-real-trapping" | n == "to-real-saturating" | n == "from-real-saturating" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "to-real-approx-trapping" | n == "from-real-approx-trapping" | n == "to-real-approx-saturating" | n == "from-real-approx-saturating" then emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "real-from-int" then wat-f64-out ("(f64.convert_i64_s " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")")
   else if n == "real-approx-from-int" then wat-f64-out ("(f64.promote_f32 (f32.convert_i64_s " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & "))")
   else if n == "real-to-int" | n == "real-approx-to-int" then "(call $cx_real_to_int " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "to-real-approx" then wat-f64-out ("(f64.promote_f32 (f32.demote_f64 " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)) & "))")
   else if n == "vec-splat" then wat-emit-vec-splat ctx (list-at args 0) 2
   else if n == "vec4-splat" then wat-emit-vec-splat ctx (list-at args 0) 4
   else if n == "vec-extract" | n == "vec4-extract" then wat-emit-vec-extract ctx (list-at args 0) (list-at args 1)
   else if n == "vec-add" then wat-emit-vec-named-arith ctx ".add" args
   else if n == "vec-sub" then wat-emit-vec-named-arith ctx ".sub" args
   else if n == "vec-mul" then wat-emit-vec-named-arith ctx ".mul" args
   else if n == "vec-div" then wat-emit-vec-named-arith ctx ".div" args
   else if n == "vec-reduce-add" then wat-emit-vec-reduce ctx (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "vec-load-at" then "(call $cx_vec_box (v128.load (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")))"
   else if n == "vec-store-at" then "(call $cx_vec_store " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "vec-select" then wat-emit-vec-select ctx args
   else if n == "mask-any" | n == "mask-all" | n == "mask-none" | n == "mask-count" then wat-emit-mask-query ctx n (list-at args 0)
   else if n == "real-approx-to-bits" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.reinterpret_f32 (f32.demote_f64 " & wat-f64-in (emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0)) & ")))"
   else if n == "bits-to-real-approx" then wat-f64-out ("(f64.promote_f32 (f32.reinterpret_i32 (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")))")
   else if n == "blit-framebuf" then "(call $blit_framebuf (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")) (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "__buf-write-byte" then "(call $buf_write_byte " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "__buf-write-bytes" then "(call $buf_write_bytes " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "__buf-read-bytes" then "(call $buf_read_bytes " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "__list-with-capacity" then "(call $list_with_capacity " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "list-insert-at" then "(call $list_insert_at " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "__linked-list-empty" then "(i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "__linked-list-push" then "(call $ll_push " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")"
   else if n == "__linked-list-to-list" then "(call $ll_to_list " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "text-concat-list" then "(call $text_concat_list " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ")"
   else if n == "list-set-at" then "(call $list_set_at " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")"
   else if n == "__record-set" then wat-emit-record-set ctx args
   else if n == "poke-32" then "(i32.store (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & ")) (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 2) & ")) (i64.const 0)"
   else if n == "peek-32" then "(i64.extend_i32_u (i32.load (i32.add (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 0) & ") (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args 1) & "))))"
   else ""

Section: Tail Call Optimization

  is-self-tail-call : Text, IRExpr -> Boolean
  is-self-tail-call (fname) (e) =
   when e
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) ->
     let chain = collect-apply-chain e []
     in when chain.root
      is IrName (n) (ty2) (sp2) -> n == fname
      is otherwise -> False
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) ->
     is-self-tail-call fname t | is-self-tail-call fname el
    is IrLet (n) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) ->
     is-self-tail-call fname body
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) ->
     is-self-tail-call-branches fname branches 0
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) ->
     is-self-tail-call-last-exec fname stmts
    is otherwise -> False

  is-self-tail-call-branches : Text, List IRBranch, Integer -> Boolean
  is-self-tail-call-branches (fname) (bs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length bs then False
   else if is-self-tail-call fname ((list-at bs i).body) then True
   else is-self-tail-call-branches fname bs (i + 1)

  is-self-tail-call-last-exec : Text, List IRActStmt -> Boolean
  is-self-tail-call-last-exec (fname) (stmts) =
   if list-length stmts == 0 then False
   else when list-at stmts (list-length stmts - 1)
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> is-self-tail-call fname e
    is otherwise -> False

  emit-tco-temp-decls : Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-tco-temp-decls (i) (count) =
   if i >= count then ""
   else "(local $_tco_" & integer-to-text i & " i64) " & emit-tco-temp-decls (i + 1) count

  emit-tco-store-args : WasmCtx, List IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-tco-store-args (ctx) (args) (i) =
   if i >= list-length args then ""
   else "(local.set $_tco_" & integer-to-text i & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx (list-at args i) & ") " & emit-tco-store-args ctx args (i + 1)

  emit-tco-copy-params : List IRParam, Integer -> Text
  emit-tco-copy-params (params) (i) =
   if i >= list-length params then ""
   else "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize ((list-at params i).name) & " (local.get $_tco_" & integer-to-text i & ")) " & emit-tco-copy-params params (i + 1)

  emit-wat-expr-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-expr-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (e) (depth) =
   if depth >= 256 then emit-wat-expr ctx e
   else when e
    is IrApply (f) (a) (ty) (sp) ->
     let chain = collect-apply-chain e []
     in when chain.root
      is IrName (n) (ty2) (sp2) ->
       if n == fname then
        emit-tco-store-args ctx (chain.args) 0 & emit-tco-copy-params params 0 & "(br $tco_loop)"
       else emit-wat-tail-apply ctx e n (chain.args)
      is otherwise -> emit-wat-apply ctx e
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) ->
     emit-wat-if-chain-tco ctx fname params e depth
    is IrLet (name) (ty) (val) (body) (sp) ->
     "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx val (depth + 1) & ") " & emit-wat-expr-tco ctx fname params body (depth + 1)
    is IrMatch (scrut) (branches) (ty) (sp) ->
     emit-wat-match-tco ctx fname params scrut branches depth
    is IrAct (stmts) (ty) (sp) ->
     emit-wat-act-stmts-tco ctx fname params stmts 0 depth
    is otherwise -> emit-wat-expr-at ctx e depth

 The TCO twin of the chain above. An arm here may itself be a self-tail call,
 which emits its argument stores and a `(br $tco_loop)` and produces no value;
 that rides inside `(br $_i ...)` exactly as it already rides inside the match
 arms' `(br $_m ...)`, because a branch out makes what follows unreachable.

  emit-wat-if-chain-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-if-chain-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (e) (depth) =
   "(block $_i (result i64) " & emit-wat-if-arms-tco ctx fname params e depth & ")"

  emit-wat-if-arms-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, IRExpr, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-if-arms-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (e) (depth) =
   when e
    is IrIf (c) (t) (el) (ty) (sp) ->
     "(block $_c (br_if $_c (i32.eqz (i32.wrap_i64 " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx c (depth + 1) & "))) (br $_i " & emit-wat-expr-tco ctx fname params t (depth + 1) & ")) "
       & emit-wat-if-arms-tco ctx fname params el depth
    is otherwise -> emit-wat-expr-tco ctx fname params e (depth + 1)

  emit-wat-act-stmts-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, List IRActStmt, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-act-stmts-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (stmts) (i) (depth) =
   if i == list-length stmts then "(i64.const 0)"
   else let s = list-at stmts i
   in let is-last = i == list-length stmts - 1
   in (when s
    is IrDoBind (name) (ty) (val) (sp) -> "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " " & emit-wat-expr ctx val & ") "
    is IrDoExec (e) (sp) -> if is-last then emit-wat-expr-tco ctx fname params e (depth + 1) else "(drop " & emit-wat-expr ctx e & ") ") & (if is-last then "" else emit-wat-act-stmts-tco ctx fname params stmts (i + 1) depth)

 A call in tail position to ANOTHER known function is a wasm `return_call`,
 which the engine runs in the caller's frame: mutual tail recursion -- the
 lexer's scan-token/skip-prose-line cycle, ping/pong -- runs in constant
 stack, which no self-loop can do and which is the difference between a
 browser's fixed stack and a flag only wasmtime honors. The dispatch order
 mirrors emit-wat-apply: builtins, constructors and locals-as-values never
 reach it, and only an application that SATURATES the callee's arity
 qualifies; everything else keeps the ordinary path. A builtin can carry a
 bracket (deck-record) or scratch protocol, so routing those through the
 plain path first is what keeps the enter/exit balance exact.

  emit-wat-tail-apply : WasmCtx, IRExpr, Text, List IRExpr -> Text
  emit-wat-tail-apply (ctx) (e) (n) (args) =
   let bi = wat-try-builtin ctx n args
   in if text-length bi > 0 then emit-wat-apply ctx e
   else if is-ctor-name (ctx.type-defs) n 0 then emit-wat-apply ctx e
   else if locals-contains (ctx.bound) (wat-sanitize n) then emit-wat-apply ctx e
   else let ar = lookup-arity (ctx.arities) n
   in if ar > 0 & list-length args == ar then
    "(return_call $" & wat-sanitize n & " " & emit-wat-apply-args ctx args 0 & ")"
   else emit-wat-apply ctx e

 This is the TCO twin of the match emission above and it is the one that was
 actually making the module deep. The measured deepest point in
 `codex-compiler.wat` is a constructor-tag chain reading
 `(if (result i64) (i64.eq (i64.load (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $_s))) ...`,
 which is THIS function's shape: `emit-wat-expr-at`'s `IrIf` always puts
 `(i32.wrap_i64` immediately after `(result i64)` and the measured text does
 not have it, and `local.get $_s` is a match scrutinee rather than anything an
 `if` produces. Flattening the non-TCO twin alone moved the depth 188 to 188,
 because the compiler's constructor dispatch is self-tail-recursive and never
 takes that path.

 Two shapes the old code had that the arm form removes. A last literal or
 constructor arm was emitted with NO test at all, so an inexhaustive match ran
 the last arm on a value it did not match; the non-TCO twin has always tested
 and fallen through to `(unreachable)`, and these now agree. And an unguarded
 wildcard in the middle discarded every later arm rather than emitting them
 dead, which was a special case rather than a saving.

  emit-wat-match-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, IRExpr, List IRBranch, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-match-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (scrut) (branches) (depth) =
   if list-length branches == 0 then "(unreachable)"
   else "(local.set " & wat-scrut ctx & " " & emit-wat-expr-at ctx scrut (depth + 1) & ") (block $_m (result i64) "
        & emit-wat-branches-tco ctx fname params branches 0 depth & "(unreachable))"

  emit-wat-branches-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-branches-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (bs) (i) (depth) =
   if i >= list-length bs then ""
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in let arm = emit-wat-arm-tco ctx fname params (b.pattern) (b.guard) (b.body) bs i depth
   in arm & emit-wat-branches-tco ctx fname params bs (i + 1) depth

  emit-wat-arm-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRParam, IRPat, IRExpr, IRExpr, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-arm-tco (ctx) (fname) (params) (pat) (guard) (bexpr) (bs) (i) (depth) =
   let body = emit-wat-expr-tco ctx fname params bexpr (depth + 1)
   in when pat
    is IrLitPat (text) (ty) (sp) ->
     let lit-test = wat-lit-pat-test ctx text ty
     in let cond = if wat-guard-trivial guard then lit-test
        else "(i32.and " & lit-test & " " & emit-wat-guard-test ctx guard & ")"
     in emit-wat-arm-block "" cond "" body
    is IrCtorPat (name) (subs) (ty) (sp) -> emit-wat-ctor-arm-tco ctx name subs guard body bs i
    is IrVarPat (name) (ty) (sp) ->
     let bind = "(local.set $" & wat-sanitize name & " (local.get " & wat-scrut ctx & ")) "
     in emit-wat-arm-block bind (wat-arm-cond ctx guard) "" body
    is IrWildPat (sp) ->
     emit-wat-arm-block "" (wat-arm-cond ctx guard) "" body
    is IrVecPat (pats) (ty) (sp) ->
     let vcond = wat-emit-vec-pat-cond ctx pats ty
     in let cond = if wat-guard-trivial guard then vcond
        else "(i32.and " & vcond & " " & emit-wat-guard-test ctx guard & ")"
     in emit-wat-arm-block "" cond "" body

  emit-wat-ctor-arm-tco : WasmCtx, Text, List IRPat, IRExpr, Text, List IRBranch, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-ctor-arm-tco (ctx) (name) (subs) (guard) (body) (bs) (i) =
   let tag = find-ctor-in-typedefs (ctx.type-defs) name 0
   in let bind-subs = emit-wat-bind-ctor-subs ctx subs 0
   in if (i == list-length bs - 1) & wat-guard-trivial guard & (wat-ctor-has-lit-subs subs 0 == False) then
    emit-wat-arm-block "" "(i32.const 1)" bind-subs body
   else emit-wat-arm-block "" (wat-ctor-arm-cond ctx tag subs guard bind-subs) bind-subs body

Section: Definition Emission

  emit-wat-def : WasmCtx, IRDef -> Text
  emit-wat-def (ctx) (d) =
   let name = wat-sanitize (d.name)
   in let all-locals = collect-locals-expr (d.body) (collect-locals-params (d.params) 0 [])
   in let locals = locals-minus-params all-locals (d.params) 0 []
   in let bound = collect-locals-params (d.params) 0 all-locals
   in let dctx = ctx-with ctx bound
   in if is-self-tail-call (d.name) (d.body) then
    let pc = list-length (d.params)
    in let tco-temps = emit-tco-temp-decls 0 pc
    in let local-text = emit-local-decls locals 0
    in "  (func $" & name & " " & emit-wat-def-params (d.params) 0 & "(result i64) " & local-text & tco-temps & "\n    (loop $tco_loop (result i64) " & emit-wat-expr-tco dctx (d.name) (d.params) (d.body) 0 & "))\n"
   else let local-text = emit-local-decls locals 0
   in "  (func $" & name & " " & emit-wat-def-params (d.params) 0 & "(result i64) " & local-text & "\n    " & emit-wat-expr-tco dctx "" (d.params) (d.body) 0 & ")\n"

  emit-wat-def-params : List IRParam, Integer -> Text
  emit-wat-def-params (params) (i) =
   if i == list-length params then ""
   else "(param $" & wat-sanitize ((list-at params i).name) & " i64) " & emit-wat-def-params params (i + 1)

Section: WASI Runtime Helpers

 The two `env` imports are emitted only when the program calls them. A WASI
 host supplies `wasi_snapshot_preview1` and nothing else, so importing
 `blit_framebuf` and `on_key` unconditionally made EVERY emitted module fail
 to instantiate -- `unknown import: env::blit_framebuf` under wasmtime -- which
 is why nothing had ever run this plug's output. A browser host that does
 provide them is unaffected: a program that calls them still declares them.

  wat-runtime-header : Integer, Boolean, Boolean -> Text
  wat-runtime-header (heap-start) (needs-blit) (needs-key) =
   "(module\n" &
   "  (import \"wasi_snapshot_preview1\" \"fd_write\" (func $fd_write (param i32 i32 i32 i32) (result i32)))\n" &
   "  (import \"wasi_snapshot_preview1\" \"fd_read\" (func $fd_read (param i32 i32 i32 i32) (result i32)))\n" &
   (if needs-blit then "  (import \"env\" \"blit_framebuf\" (func $blit_framebuf (param i32)))\n" else "") &
   (if needs-key then "  (import \"env\" \"on_key\" (func $on_key_import (param i32) (result i32)))\n" else "") &
   "  (memory (export \"memory\") 256)\n" &
   "  (global $heap_ptr (mut i32) (i32.const " & integer-to-text heap-start & "))\n" &
   "  (global $heap_start i32 (i32.const " & integer-to-text heap-start & "))\n" &
   "  (global $deck_ptr (mut i32) (i32.const " & integer-to-text heap-start & "))\n" &
   "  (global $bivy_save (mut i32) (i32.const " & integer-to-text heap-start & "))\n" &
   "  (global $deck_depth (mut i32) (i32.const 0))\n" &
   "  (global $rd_len (mut i32) (i32.const 0))\n" &
   "  (global $rd_pos (mut i32) (i32.const 0))\n" &
   "  (global $disk_base (mut i32) (i32.const 0))\n" &
   "  (global $disk_size (mut i32) (i32.const 0))\n" &
   "  (func $__heap_reset (global.set $heap_ptr (global.get $heap_start)))\n" &
   "  (export \"__heap_reset\" (func $__heap_reset))\n" &
   "  (func $__deck_enter\n" &
   "    (if (i32.eqz (global.get $deck_depth)) (then\n" &
   "      (global.set $bivy_save (global.get $heap_ptr))\n" &
   "      (global.set $heap_ptr (global.get $deck_ptr))))\n" &
   "    (global.set $deck_depth (i32.add (global.get $deck_depth) (i32.const 1))))\n" &
   "  (func $__deck_exit\n" &
   "    (global.set $deck_depth (i32.sub (global.get $deck_depth) (i32.const 1)))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.eqz (global.get $deck_depth)) (then\n" &
   "      (global.set $deck_ptr (global.get $heap_ptr))\n" &
   "      (global.set $heap_ptr (global.get $bivy_save)))))\n" &
   wat-rt-ipow &
   wat-rt-int-trap &
   wat-rt-real-to-int &
   "  (func $codex_mod (param $a i64) (param $b i64) (result i64) (local $m i64) (local $r i64)\n" &
   "    (local.set $m (select (i64.sub (i64.const 0) (local.get $b)) (local.get $b) (i64.lt_s (local.get $b) (i64.const 0))))\n" &
   "    (local.set $r (i64.rem_s (local.get $a) (local.get $m)))\n" &
   "    (select (i64.add (local.get $r) (local.get $m)) (local.get $r) (i64.lt_s (local.get $r) (i64.const 0))))\n\n"

 `real-to-int` on x86-64 is `cvttsd2si` (X86_64Builtins.codex): truncate toward
 zero, and the integer indefinite INT64_MIN for a NaN, an infinity, or a value
 whose truncation does not fit. `i64.trunc_sat_f64_s` agrees on all of those
 except NaN (saturates to 0) and positive overflow (saturates to INT64_MAX), so
 those two are tested first.

 A plain Integer traps on signed overflow (COMPILER-36 contract); only a node
 typed with the wrapping band takes the raw instruction. wasm has no overflow
 flag and no i128, so each check is on the result: add and sub overflow iff
 the sign of the result differs from both operands (add) or the operands'
 signs differ and the result's sign differs from the left's (sub); mul checks
 the quotient, which is exact whenever no overflow occurred and cannot equal
 the multiplier otherwise, because the wrapped result is off by a multiple of
 2^64 and the divisor is below 2^63. The two divisions that themselves trap,
 a = 0 and (i64-min / -1), are answered before the quotient is taken.

  wat-rt-int-trap : Text =
   "  (func $cx_add_trap (param $a i64) (param $b i64) (result i64) (local $r i64)\n" &
   "    (local.set $r (i64.add (local.get $a) (local.get $b)))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.lt_s (i64.and (i64.xor (local.get $r) (local.get $a)) (i64.xor (local.get $r) (local.get $b))) (i64.const 0)) (then (unreachable)))\n" &
   "    (local.get $r))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cx_sub_trap (param $a i64) (param $b i64) (result i64) (local $r i64)\n" &
   "    (local.set $r (i64.sub (local.get $a) (local.get $b)))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.lt_s (i64.and (i64.xor (local.get $a) (local.get $b)) (i64.xor (local.get $a) (local.get $r))) (i64.const 0)) (then (unreachable)))\n" &
   "    (local.get $r))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cx_mul_trap (param $a i64) (param $b i64) (result i64) (local $r i64)\n" &
   "    (local.set $r (i64.mul (local.get $a) (local.get $b)))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.eqz (local.get $a)) (then (return (local.get $r))))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.eq (local.get $a) (i64.const -1))\n" &
   "      (then (if (i64.eq (local.get $b) (i64.const -9223372036854775808)) (then (unreachable))) (return (local.get $r))))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.ne (i64.div_s (local.get $r) (local.get $a)) (local.get $b)) (then (unreachable)))\n" &
   "    (local.get $r))\n\n"

  wat-rt-ipow : Text =
   "  (func $cx_ipow (param $b i64) (param $e i64) (result i64)\n" &
   "    (local $acc i64)\n" &
   "    (local.set $acc (i64.const 1))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.lt_s (local.get $e) (i64.const 0)) (then (return (i64.const 0))))\n" &
   "    (block $done (loop $pow\n" &
   "      (br_if $done (i64.eqz (local.get $e)))\n" &
   "      (local.set $acc (i64.mul (local.get $acc) (local.get $b)))\n" &
   "      (local.set $e (i64.sub (local.get $e) (i64.const 1)))\n" &
   "      (br $pow)))\n" &
   "    (local.get $acc))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cx_abs (param $x i64) (result i64)\n" &
   "    (if (i64.lt_s (local.get $x) (i64.const 0))\n" &
   "      (then (return (i64.sub (i64.const 0) (local.get $x)))))\n" &
   "    (local.get $x))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cx_max (param $a i64) (param $b i64) (result i64)\n" &
   "    (if (i64.ge_s (local.get $a) (local.get $b)) (then (return (local.get $a))))\n" &
   "    (local.get $b))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cx_print_text_raw (param $t i64)\n" &
   "    (local $p i32)\n" &
   "    (local.set $p (i32.wrap_i64 (local.get $t)))\n" &
   "    (call $write_raw (i32.add (local.get $p) (i32.const 4)) (i32.load (local.get $p))))\n\n"

  wat-rt-real-to-int : Text =
   "  (func $cx_real_to_int (param $b i64) (result i64) (local $f f64)\n" &
   "    (local.set $f (f64.reinterpret_i64 (local.get $b)))\n" &
   "    (if (result i64) (f64.ne (local.get $f) (local.get $f))\n" &
   "      (then (i64.const -9223372036854775808))\n" &
   "      (else (if (result i64) (f64.ge (local.get $f) (f64.const 9223372036854775808))\n" &
   "        (then (i64.const -9223372036854775808))\n" &
   "        (else (i64.trunc_sat_f64_s (local.get $f)))))))\n\n"

 THE ALLOCATOR GROWS THE MEMORY, because the declared 256 pages are 16 MB
 and the compiler compiling its own source asked for an address 206 MB in.
 Measured, not budgeted: `memory fault at wasm address 0xc4bac22 in linear
 memory of size 0x1000000`. Without the grow the bump pointer simply walks
 past the end and every access past it is an out-of-bounds trap, which reads
 as a codegen defect and is a ceiling.

 GROW IN 16 MB STEPS: V8's cost per memory.grow rises with the memory already
 held, so the COUNT of grows is what costs (measured 2026-08-31: 56,000 grows
 of one page to 3.5 GB, node 166.83 s against wasmtime 0.21 s; 1,000 grows of
 16 pages to the same 1 GB as 16,000 of one, 0.38 s against 5.45 s). A fixed
 step rather than doubling, because doubling overshoots by up to 12.5 per cent
 and a compiler-sized subject sits near wasm32's hard 4 GiB ceiling.

  wasm-grow-step-pages : Integer = 256

  wat-rt-bump-alloc : Text =
   "  (func $grow_by (param $need i32) (param $have i32)\n" &
   "    (local $pages i32)\n" &
   "    (local.set $pages (i32.add\n" &
   "      (i32.div_u (i32.sub (local.get $need) (local.get $have)) (i32.const 65536))\n" &
   "      (i32.const 1)))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.lt_u (local.get $pages) (i32.const " & integer-to-text wasm-grow-step-pages & "))\n" &
   "      (then (local.set $pages (i32.const " & integer-to-text wasm-grow-step-pages & "))))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.eq (memory.grow (local.get $pages)) (i32.const -1)) (then (unreachable))))\n\n" &
   "  (func $bump_alloc (param $size i32) (result i32)\n" &
   "    (local $ptr i32) (local $need i32) (local $have i32)\n" &
   "    (local.set $ptr (global.get $heap_ptr))\n" &
   "    (local.set $need (i32.add (local.get $ptr) (local.get $size)))\n" &
   "    (local.set $have (i32.mul (memory.size) (i32.const 65536)))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.gt_u (local.get $need) (local.get $have))\n" &
   "      (then (call $grow_by (local.get $need) (local.get $have))))\n" &
   "    (global.set $heap_ptr (local.get $need))\n" &
   "    (local.get $ptr))\n\n"

  wat-rt-print-i64 : Text =
   "  (func $wasi_print_i64 (param $val i64)\n" &
   "    (local $abs i64) (local $neg i32) (local $buf i32) (local $pos i32) (local $digit i32) (local $len i32)\n" &
   "    (local.set $buf (call $bump_alloc (i32.const 24)))\n" &
   "    (local.set $pos (i32.add (local.get $buf) (i32.const 23)))\n" &
   "    (local.set $neg (i64.lt_s (local.get $val) (i64.const 0)))\n" &
   "    (if (local.get $neg)\n" &
   "      (then (local.set $abs (local.get $val)))\n" &
   "      (else (local.set $abs (i64.sub (i64.const 0) (local.get $val)))))\n" &
   "    (if (i64.eqz (local.get $abs))\n" &
   "      (then (i32.store8 (local.get $pos) (i32.const 48))\n" &
   "        (local.set $pos (i32.sub (local.get $pos) (i32.const 1))))\n" &
   "      (else (block $done (loop $digits\n" &
   "          (br_if $done (i64.eqz (local.get $abs)))\n" &
   "          (local.set $digit (i32.wrap_i64 (i64.sub (i64.const 0) (i64.rem_s (local.get $abs) (i64.const 10)))))\n" &
   "          (i32.store8 (local.get $pos) (i32.add (local.get $digit) (i32.const 48)))\n" &
   "          (local.set $pos (i32.sub (local.get $pos) (i32.const 1)))\n" &
   "          (local.set $abs (i64.div_s (local.get $abs) (i64.const 10)))\n" &
   "          (br $digits)))))\n" &
   "    (if (local.get $neg) (then\n" &
   "        (i32.store8 (local.get $pos) (i32.const 45))\n" &
   "        (local.set $pos (i32.sub (local.get $pos) (i32.const 1)))))\n" &
   "    (local.set $len (i32.sub (i32.add (local.get $buf) (i32.const 24)) (i32.add (local.get $pos) (i32.const 1))))\n" &
   "    (i32.store (i32.const 0) (i32.add (local.get $pos) (i32.const 1)))\n" &
   "    (i32.store (i32.const 4) (local.get $len))\n" &
   "    (drop (call $fd_write (i32.const 1) (i32.const 0) (i32.const 1) (i32.const 16))))\n\n"

  wat-rt-print-misc : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wat-rt-print-misc (tab) (t2) (out) =
   "  (func $wasi_print_newline\n" &
   "    (i32.store8 (i32.const 20) (i32.const 10))\n" &
   "    (i32.store (i32.const 0) (i32.const 20))\n" &
   "    (i32.store (i32.const 4) (i32.const 1))\n" &
   "    (drop (call $fd_write (i32.const 1) (i32.const 0) (i32.const 1) (i32.const 16))))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cce_flush (param $n i32)\n" &
   "    (if (local.get $n) (then\n" &
   "      (i32.store (i32.const 0) (i32.const " & integer-to-text out & "))\n" &
   "      (i32.store (i32.const 4) (local.get $n))\n" &
   "      (drop (call $fd_write (i32.const 1) (i32.const 0) (i32.const 1) (i32.const 16))))))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cce_unicode (param $cc i32) (result i32)\n" &
   "    (local $u i32) (local $k i32) (local $e i32) (local $base i32) (local $sz i32)\n" &
   "    (if (i32.lt_u (local.get $cc) (i32.const " & integer-to-text cce-tab-entries & "))\n" &
   "      (then (return (i32.load16_u (i32.add (i32.const " & integer-to-text tab & ") (i32.shl (local.get $cc) (i32.const 1)))))))\n" &
   "    (local.set $u (i32.const 65533))\n" &
   "    (local.set $k (i32.const 0))\n" &
   "    (block $t2d (loop $t2c\n" &
   "      (br_if $t2d (i32.ge_u (local.get $k) (i32.const " & integer-to-text cce-tier2-block-count & ")))\n" &
   "      (local.set $e (i32.add (i32.const " & integer-to-text t2 & ") (i32.mul (local.get $k) (i32.const " & integer-to-text cce-t2-entry-bytes & "))))\n" &
   "      (local.set $base (i32.load (local.get $e)))\n" &
   "      (local.set $sz (i32.load (i32.add (local.get $e) (i32.const 4))))\n" &
   "      (if (i32.and (i32.ge_u (local.get $cc) (local.get $base))\n" &
   "                   (i32.lt_u (local.get $cc) (i32.add (local.get $base) (local.get $sz))))\n" &
   "        (then (return (i32.add (i32.sub (local.get $cc) (local.get $base))\n" &
   "                               (i32.load (i32.add (local.get $e) (i32.const 8)))))))\n" &
   "      (local.set $k (i32.add (local.get $k) (i32.const 1)))\n" &
   "      (br $t2c)))\n" &
   "    (local.get $u))\n\n" &
   "  (func $cce_put (param $n i32) (param $u i32) (result i32)\n" &
   "    (local $o i32)\n" &
   "    (local.set $o (i32.add (i32.const " & integer-to-text out & ") (local.get $n)))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.lt_u (local.get $u) (i32.const 128))\n" &
   "      (then (i32.store8 (local.get $o) (local.get $u))\n" &
   "        (return (i32.add (local.get $n) (i32.const 1)))))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.lt_u (local.get $u) (i32.const 2048))\n" &
   "      (then (i32.store8 (local.get $o) (i32.or (i32.shr_u (local.get $u) (i32.const 6)) (i32.const 192)))\n" &
   "        (i32.store8 (i32.add (local.get $o) (i32.const 1)) (i32.or (i32.and (local.get $u) (i32.const 63)) (i32.const 128)))\n" &
   "        (return (i32.add (local.get $n) (i32.const 2)))))\n" &
   "    (if (i32.lt_u (local.get $u) (i32.const 65536))\n" &
   "      (then (i32.store8 (local.get $o) (i32.or (i32.shr_u (local.get $u) (i32.const 12)) (i32.const 224)))\n" &
   "        (i32.store8 (i32.add (local.get $o) (i32.const 1)) (i32.or (i32.and (i32.shr_u (local.get $u) (i32.const 6)) (i32.const 63)) (i32.const 128)))\n" &
   "        (i32.store8 (i32.add (local.get $o) (i32.const 2)) (i32.or (i32.and (local.get $u) (i32.const 63)) (i32.const 128)))\n" &
