Chapter: Parsmi--CodexWasmHarness

 THE NATIVE FOURTH ARM. This is `CodexZigHarness` with one line changed: where
 that one ends `print-text (emit-zig-chapter ...)`, this one ends
 `emit-wasm-chapter-stream ...`. Everything above the last line -- the front
 end, the IR pipeline, the lambda lift, the halt gate, the roots, and the trip
 out through the IR TEXT WIRE and back -- is the ladder's, verbatim, because
 the property that makes it worth having is that this program runs the same
 code in the same order as the pipeline it stands in for.

 THE WIRE ROUND TRIP IS GONE, and this is the record of what it was doing.

 This driver used to emit the whole IR as text and parse it straight back
 before emitting, and said that was load-bearing: that the wire DERIVED what
 the AST does not carry, naming a computation of implicit type parameters in
 `IRTextEmitter`. That claim is not in the code -- both sides quote and parse
 the tparam list they are given. What the wire really derived was a positional
 FIELD SLOT, resolved against the receiver's record type and written into the
 field name as `parse-bag/14`, and the wasm plug read it rather than computing
 it. Its fallback when there was none searched every type-def by NAME and took
 the first match, which is wrong for the 59 field names in this subject that
 occur at more than one slot -- 73% of the emitted bytes.

 The plug now asks the receiver's type first and keeps the wire's number as a
 fallback (`wasm-slot-from-type`), which is the same number bare metal
 computes for itself in `X86_64Compound` and the same one the zig plug throws
 away. With that, the round trip derives nothing this arm needs.

 It cost 945 MB of 3,532 MB -- 27% -- and dropping it is byte-identical on
 the compiler's own 2.9 MB source and on 29 units of the safari port up to
 13.5 MB of WAT. `docs/the-ir-text-wire.md` is the measurement.

 THIS DRIVER IS NO LONGER THE SAME SHAPE AS THE GUEST ARM, and that is the
 cost paid. safari-codex's `harness/wasm_arm.py` compiles to IR on the seed
 and parses that text back before emitting; this one does not, so the two are
 no longer step-for-step comparable. The byte comparison above is what stands
 in for that, and it is a stronger check than the resemblance was.

 A COPY, and copies go stale. `PROVENANCE.md` names the commit. If the
 compiler's front end changes shape, this breaks loudly at the transpile --
 which is the honest direction, and is why nothing here is paraphrased.

Section: Halt

 The driver this stands in for does not emit when the bag has errors:
 opening.codex:1676-1678 prints the codegen error header and the notices,
 and then `if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-line-uni "CODEGEN-HALTED:
 errors in bag; no IR emitted"`. This harness skipped that gate until
 2026-08-25, and a cold read found what it costs: a file reading `this is
 not codex at all` produced 36,697 bytes of plausible wasm and exit 0, and so
 did a program calling a function that does not exist. For a tool that is
 handed to other people, the first thing they will feed it is a program with
 a mistake in it.

 The bags are the ones the driver merges, reached from what the frontend
 already binds: lex errors from `toks.errors` (opening.codex:449 wraps the
 same list), the parse bag from `doc.parse-bag` (:485), the resolve bag from
 `rr.bag`, and the type checker's from `cr.state.bag` -- which is what
 `check-bag0` at :620 merges. The PRINTERS are not reachable: they live in
 opening.codex, which cannot be bundled beside a harness defining `opening`,
 so this prints the count and the first error rather than the driver's full
 report.

 CODEGEN-HALTED is the marker the rest of the tree already refuses on by
 name (ast/f4_boot.py), which is why the text is that and not a new word.

  cwm-halted : List Diagnostic -> Text
  cwm-halted (es) =
   let n = list-length es
   in let e0 = list-at es 0
   in "CODEGEN-HALTED: " & show n & " error(s); no wasm emitted; first CDX" & show (e0.code) & " " & (e0.message) & "\n"

  cwm-sev-word : Integer -> Text
  cwm-sev-word (s) = if s == sev-warning then "warning" else "notice"

  cwm-notice-line : Diagnostic -> Text
  cwm-notice-line (d) = "CDX" & show (d.code) & " " & cwm-sev-word (d.severity) & ": " & (d.message)

  cwm-line-bytes : Text -> List Integer
  cwm-line-bytes (t) = list-push (text-to-utf8-bytes t) 10

  cwm-notices : List Diagnostic, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cwm-notices (ds) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length ds then acc
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if (d.severity) == sev-info then cwm-notices ds (i + 1) acc
   else cwm-notices ds (i + 1) (acc & cwm-line-bytes (cwm-notice-line d))

  cwm-info-count : List Diagnostic, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cwm-info-count (ds) (i) (n) =
   if i >= list-length ds then n
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if (d.severity) == sev-info then cwm-info-count ds (i + 1) (n + 1)
   else cwm-info-count ds (i + 1) n

  cwm-report : DiagnosticBag -> List Integer
  cwm-report (b) =
   let ds = bag-diagnostics b
   in let body = cwm-notices ds 0 []
   in let n = cwm-info-count ds 0 0
   in if n == 0 then body
   else body & cwm-line-bytes ("codexwasm: " & show n & " info diagnostics not shown")


Section: Roots

 opening.codex:1316, copied because that chapter cannot ride along.

  cwm-emit-roots : List Text
  cwm-emit-roots = ["opening", "vb-capacity-auto", "vb-read-auto", "vb-write-auto", "fat16-servicer-read", "fat16-servicer-write"]

 A DECLARED EXPORT IS A ROOT, and until this it was not.

 `wasm-exports` lets a chapter name the functions it means to be called from
 outside. The emitter reads that list -- but it reads it AFTER pruning, and a
 function written to be called from JavaScript has no callers inside the
 chapter, which is exactly what `ir-prune-unreachable-roots` deletes. The
 declaration survived and the function it named did not, so the module
 carried a comment saying the export was missing and nothing else.

 A root is precisely "this is live because something outside calls it", so
 the export list belongs in the roots and this reads it out of the IR before
 pruning. `wasm-exports` itself is rooted too, or the pruner removes the
 declaration before the emitter can read it.

 `opening` STAYS FIRST. `ir-prune-roots-indexed` returns the chapter unpruned
 when the FIRST root names no definition, so the head of this list has to be
 one that always exists; the five after it are inherited from opening.codex's
 own list and name nothing in this subject, which is harmless only because
 they are not first.

 IT SAVES THE ONE-CALLER CASE TOO, which was not the prediction. Reading
 `keep-single-caller` suggested that a definition with exactly one caller is
 folded into it and lost before any root can help. Measured, it is not:
 INLINING SUBSTITUTES AND PRUNING DELETES. The pass copies the body into the
 call site, which leaves the definition with no references, and it is the
 pruner that then removes it -- so a root prevents exactly that. A chapter
 whose `opening` calls `twice` once now emits an `$opening` that calls
 `$greet` directly, an unreferenced `$twice` that is kept and exported, and
 `twice(5) = 20` when JavaScript calls it.

 Zero, one and two-plus callers all survive. The prediction was wrong in the
 cheap direction and the reason is worth keeping: two passes were read as one
 mechanism because they have the same effect on the common case.

  cwm-text-list-of : IRExpr -> List Text
  cwm-text-list-of (e) =
   when e
    is IrList (es) (ty) (sp) -> cwm-text-list-elems es 0 []
    is otherwise -> []

  cwm-text-list-elems : List IRExpr, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  cwm-text-list-elems (es) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length es then acc
   else when list-at es i
    is IrTextLit (s) (sp) -> cwm-text-list-elems es (i + 1) (list-push acc s)
    is otherwise -> cwm-text-list-elems es (i + 1) acc

  cwm-declared-exports : List IRDef, Integer -> List Text
  cwm-declared-exports (defs) (i) =
   if i >= list-length defs then []
   else let d = list-at defs i
   in if (d.name) == "wasm-exports" then cwm-text-list-of (d.body)
      else cwm-declared-exports defs (i + 1)

Section: Driver

 The lift is opening.codex:1713-1720, and it is on that path only since
 Update 50 -- the release names it in four words, "the DDC witness holds
 after the lambda-lift break". Before that a plug never received a
 `__lam_N`, which is also why nothing noticed these harnesses had none: the
 codexzig fixed point held because NEITHER arm lifted, so the property was
 never tested against a subject carrying a lifted lambda, and the first
 time it was, it failed. It goes between the IR pipeline and
 ir-prune-unreachable-roots, where the driver puts it -- pruning first drops
 the lifted definitions, which are reachable only from the bodies the lift
 rewrote. The ceiling is 0 and emit_harness.py says why it is not the
 driver's.

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
    src <- read-file-uni "/dev/stdin"
    let mountain-base = init-phase-allocator
    in let deck-base = __heap-save
    in let deck-set = __deck-set deck-base
    in let deck-adv = __heap-advance 536870912
    in let fe = compile-frontend-cdx src "Codex_Codex" compile-flags-default
    in if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-text (cwm-halted (bag-errors (fe.bag)))
    else let cwm-exports = cwm-declared-exports ((fe.cdx-ir).defs) 0
    in let cwm-roots = cwm-emit-roots & list-push cwm-exports "wasm-exports"
    in let pruned = ir-prune-unreachable-roots (fe.cdx-ir) cwm-roots
    in act
      write-binary (cwm-report (fe.bag))
      emit-wasm-chapter-stream pruned (fe.type-defs)
    end
  end


